   1: # Expected-state declarations
   2: 
   3: One file per requirement â€” `req-016.json` here declares what
   4: `bun run at:verify req-016 --tier loop --expect` must find.
   5: 
   6: ## 1. What a declaration is
   7: 
   8: A declaration is the committed answer to "what is this requirement's suite supposed to report
   9: right now?" â€” which ids must be green, which must be red, and exactly why each red is red.
  10: 
  11: Without one, `at:verify` exits 1 whenever any id is red, and a requirement with honest reds waiting
  12: on later capability slices can never gate anything: a machine cannot tell "the eight declared green
  13: and the four declared reds" from "something broke". With one, it can.
  14: 
  15: `--expect` **refuses** rather than passes when the declaration cannot be honoured. All four of
  16: these exit 2, having run no tests at all:
  17: 
  18: - there is no declaration file for the requirement;
  19: - there is one, but it declares nothing for the tier being run;
  20: - it is malformed â€” bad JSON, an unknown tier key, a red that is not a well-formed declaration;
  21: - its ids are not in exact bijection with the acceptance file's P0 ids. A declaration that forgets
  22:   an id must never silently pass: that id would simply not be checked.
  23: 
  24: Without `--expect`, `at:verify` behaves exactly as it always has. The flag is additive.
  25: 
  26: ## 2. A red that turns green is a FAILURE
  27: 
  28: This surprises people, and it is deliberate. The declaration is the contract, so improving reality
  29: means updating the contract **in the same change**. Without that rule the gate cannot tell an
  30: improvement from a drift â€” and telling those apart is the entire point of the file.
  31: 
  32: So when a capability lands and an id goes green, the change that lands it moves the id from `red`
  33: to `green` here. That is one line in the same commit, and it is the record of what changed.
  34: 
  35: ## 3. How to write and update one
  36: 
  37: **A NEW TIER'S DECLARATION IS AUTHORED BEFORE ITS FIRST RUN, never derived from one.** The
  38: exact-match machinery pins DRIFT â€” it catches the day reality stops matching the contract â€” and it
  39: cannot establish what the contract should have said in the first place. A declaration copied out of
  40: the first run gives that run authority over its own expectations, which is the one thing a
  41: declaration exists to prevent. So the first declaration for a tier is written from the settled
  42: per-id analysis: what each id's body does, which capabilities the harness supplies at that tier, and
  43: what the criterion requires. **The run must then match it.** A divergence is investigated as a
  44: defect first; a declaration is amended toward a run only with the cause traced to code or fixture
  45: and recorded in the item's record.
  46: 
  47: Updating an EXISTING tier's declaration is the different case the rest of this section describes.
  48: Run the suite and read what it actually reports:
  49: 
  50: ```
  51: bun run at:verify req-016 --tier loop
  52: ```
  53: 
  54: Then copy the cause out of the reported detail into the declaration. A red is declared as an
  55: object carrying its **kind**, never as a free-text reason:
  56: 
  57: ```json
  58: "AT-016.01": { "kind": "capability-pending", "capabilities": ["H3 static provider scan"] }
  59: ```
  60: 
  61: ```json
  62: "AT-016.05": { "kind": "pending", "phase": "sut-missing" }
  63: ```
  64: 
  65: **The match is exact, not a substring.** The first line the harness prints is rebuilt from what you
  66: declare and compared:
  67: 
  68: - `capability-pending` must match the **whole first line**. The line is
  69:   `CapabilityPending: CAPABILITY PENDING â€” ` followed by your capability names joined with `, ` in
  70:   the order you list them. So the names must be complete and in the harness's order: declaring
  71:   `H3 static provider` when the real detail says `H3 static provider scan` fails, and so does
  72:   listing two capabilities the other way round.
  73: - `pending` must match `AtPending: <id> PENDING [<phase>] â€” ` as an **anchored prefix**. The class,
  74:   the id and the phase are all matched exactly, from the first character. Only the trailing detail
  75:   is free, because that tail is not something a declaration can determine: for `sut-missing` the
  76:   suite supplies its own words, and for `harness-missing` it embeds a module-resolution message
  77:   that differs by machine and checkout path. A declaration that could never be written on a second
  78:   machine would be a broken rule, not a stricter one.
  79: 
  80: Why exact rather than a substring: a substring cannot establish that a red has its declared
  81: **cause**. `H3 fault injection` as a free substring also matches
  82: `Error: H3 fault injection: fixture reset failed` â€” so a brand-new harness defect would satisfy a
  83: declaration that says "waiting on a capability", and the gate would pass. Matching the shape kills
  84: that: a fixture-reset failure is an `Error`, not a `CapabilityPending`, and cannot match.
  85: 
  86: **A red whose detail fits neither shape is undeclarable, and therefore fails.** That is deliberate:
  87: a red we cannot describe exactly is a red we do not understand, and it must not pass.
  88: 
  89: Two things a declaration may never contain, both refused when the file is read:
  90: 
  91: - **a comma inside a capability name.** Names are joined with `, `, so `["a, b"]` and `["a", "b"]`
  92:   produce the same line, and the declaration would stop saying which capabilities are pending;
  93: - **a redaction sentinel** such as `<redacted-token>`. That is what the runner prints *instead of*
  94:   something secret-shaped, so declaring one would match whatever had been redacted â€” a wildcard,
  95:   not a declaration. And if a real detail arrives redacted, the run fails closed with a message
  96:   saying the id is undeclarable until its detail no longer contains a secret-shaped token.
  97: 
  98: One consequence worth knowing: this couples declarations to the harness's message text. If that
  99: text changes, declarations break loudly with a diff naming the id â€” the correct failure direction.
 100: Machine-readable capability codes would decouple them and are filed as a follow-up.
 101: 
 102: ## 4. Changing a declaration is a governance act
 103: 
 104: No current-state checker can prove a red is **honest**. Nothing here can distinguish "this id is
 105: waiting on a capability that has not landed" from "someone broke this id and moved it to `red` with
 106: a matching reason". The declaration is an author's claim about intent, and the answer to that is
 107: review, not more machinery:
 108: 
 109: - treat a `green` â†’ `red` move with more suspicion than a code change;
 110: - a red that names a capability should point at the board item that will deliver it.
 111: 
 112: Making that reference mandatory and checking it is filed as a follow-up. Until then it is a
 113: reviewer's job.
 114: 
 115: ## 5. What `--expect` accounts for, and the one thing it still cannot see
 116: 
 117: Two layers of accounting run on every `--expect` pass, and both are needed.
 118: 
 119: **Test arithmetic.** Failing tests equal the declared reds, passing tests equal the declared
 120: greens, the total equals their sum, and nothing is skipped or todo. That closes the case where an
 121: extra failure no AT id claims â€” an untagged `it()` â€” hides behind an expected red.
 122: 
 123: **File accounting.** Every test *file* in the report is inspected too, because a failure can belong
 124: to a file and to no test at all: a file that fails to import, or a hook that throws. Two rules,
 125: both derived from what the reporter really emits: a file reported as failed must contain at least
 126: one failed test, and no file may carry a file-level error message. Without this a second file dying
 127: at import left every declared count untouched and the run exited 0.
 128: 
 129: **The one hole that remains, stated exactly** â€” this was measured against the installed reporter,
 130: not reasoned about. A hook that throws in a file that *also* contains a failing test is serialised
 131: identically to a healthy run of that file: same file status, same counts, same `success`. So while
 132: a red is declared in that same file, such a failure is invisible here. (A hook that throws in a
 133: file whose tests all pass IS caught, by the file accounting above.) Putting the raw vitest output
 134: in front of CI (AI4DEV-26) covers part of it; closing the rest needs a reporter-side envelope,
 135: which is filed.
 136: 
 137: Worth stating because it is easy to assume otherwise: on this axis `--expect` is **not** weaker
 138: than the plain no-flag gate. That gate's own process-exit rule only distinguishes a hook failure
 139: when no test failed at all, and `--expect` keeps exactly that rule for a declaration with no red.
 140: 
 141: ## 6. Two known limits
 142: 
 143: **Redaction.** The reported detail passes through the harness's redactor, which rewrites any run of
 144: 40 or more `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` characters to `<redacted-token>`. If a detail ever contained such a run,
 145: no declaration could reproduce the line, so that id is undeclarable: the run fails closed and says
 146: so, rather than matching something that identifies nothing. None of REQ-016's reds is affected â€”
 147: every detail contains spaces â€” but if you hit it, that is the cause.
 148: 
 149: **Integration-tier declarations now exist, and what they mean differs from the loop tier's.** Both
 150: files here declare both tiers. A loop-tier green says the shipped DECISIONS behave as the criterion
 151: requires, over storage that is a Map; an integration-tier green says the criterion holds against a
 152: real database this run rebuilt, the deployed edge functions, and the real Supabase Auth. The two are
 153: not the same claim and neither implies the other, which is why both are declared and both are run.
 154: 
 155: Two things follow that are easy to get wrong. An id green at loop and red at integration is a
 156: NORMAL state, not a defect â€” it means the decision is right and something the criterion also
 157: requires cannot be reached here, and the red names which capability that is. And an
 158: integration-tier red of kind `capability-pending` carries capability names from three different
 159: places: `sut.<key>.<method>` for a method the suite's live adapter does not back, the ledger's own
 160: names (`fixtures.worlds`, `sut.<key>`) for a suite that has no live adapter at all, and a
 161: suite-authored name for a criterion whose missing article is neither.

