ees\\agent-afec9a241af931aac\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-81\\rulings-gate1.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Gate 
1 rulings — AI4DEV-81 (per-item integration verification)\n2: \n3: Ruler: the DRAFT orchestrator sitting (fable). 
Reviewer: gpt-5.6-sol via codex, effort xhigh,\n4: read-only. Source: `artifacts/gate1-sol.output.txt` (11 findings), 
distillate\n5: `artifacts/gate1-sol.distilled.md`. Every claim below is quoted verbatim from the raw output.\n6: Every 
load-bearing code fact each finding cites was re-verified against this tree before ruling.\n7: \n8: ## Finding 1 — 
ACCEPT\n9: \n10: > \"D4 treats syntactically local connection strings as positive provenance even though they are\n11: 
> not bound to the runner's validated slot evidence.\"\n12: \n13: Verified: `localStackProblems` (runner.ts:714) 
checks URL shape and JWT claims only — all\n14: fabricatable without any prepared stack answering. The child receives 
four plain strings\n15: (db-pool.ts:1249-1254). Shape checks are a guard against the personal stack, not positive\n16: 
provenance.\n17: \n18: **Remedy adopted:** real provenance for slot-backed capabilities requires a live 
round-trip\n19: binding. `prepare()` mints a per-run attestation nonce and writes it into the slot database after\n20: 
the reset (service-role SQL); the child receives it as `AT_SLOT_ATTESTATION`. The live adapter's\n21: construction 
reads the nonce back from the slot database THROUGH the supplied coordinates and\n22: refuses on mismatch or read 
failure. The existing shape checks stay (they guard the personal\n23: stack). Positive grounds = \"these coordinates 
answered with this run's runner-minted value.\"\n24: Selftests (step 3) must include the fabricated-but-well-formed 
case sol names.\n25: \n26: ## Finding 2 — ACCEPT\n27: \n28: > \"The proposed generic live-backed route does not 
preserve the closed-table doctrine for\n29: > `sut.*` names.\"\n30: \n31: Verified: `adapterDerivedCapability` 
(capabilities.ts:311) is safe precisely because every\n32: outcome is stand-in; its own comment says a prefix that 
GRANTS a verdict is a hole.\n33: \n34: **Remedy adopted:** no route ever grants `real` by prefix. The live route 
constructs `real` only\n35: for exact names drawn from a CLOSED enumeration the live adapter module itself exports 
(its\n36: backed-method list), with two admission checks: every enumerated name must exist on the loaded\n37: adapter 
surface, and every grant must carry finding 1's attestation evidence. Names outside the\n38: enumeration are never 
real (they go through the callable-pending mechanism of finding 5). The\n39: witness table stays closed; the live 
route is a separate constructor with its own admission\n40: partition, mirroring the existing two-route design. Step 
3's selftests must prove the NEW route\n41: refuses unknown and unenumerated names — not only that the old table 
does.\n42: \n43: ## Finding 3 — ACCEPT\n44: \n45: > \"Every integration harness will still contain the `vendors.email` 
stand-in, so D2 rejects\n46: > every planned green body before it reaches the live adapter.\"\n47: \n48: Verified: 
`buildCapabilityLedger` constructs `EmailProviderSim` unconditionally (index.ts:181,\n49: :188) and the whole-ledger 
gate (registry.ts:619) refuses any stand-in above loop. The same\n50: structural problem covers `clock.controlled` 
(index.ts:175, finding 4) and `fixtures.worlds`\n51: (always stand-in on the adapter-derived route).\n52: \n53: 
**Remedy adopted:** ledger construction becomes tier-aware. At integration it constructs: a live\n54: email capability 
reading the slot stack's mail catcher (the endpoint derived from the slot's own\n55: status/config, attested per 
finding 1, probed before grant — the witness gains a live branch\n56: granting `real` on that positive evidence; the 
sim branch is untouched); the attested real clock\n57: (finding 4); and live-route fixtures/sut entries (findings 2, 
5). Whether the slot stack serves\n58: its mail catcher and at which port joins step 5's verify-first list.\n59: \n60: 
## Finding 4 — ACCEPT\n61: \n62: > \"The attested clock specified by D4 cannot satisfy the harness types because it 
deliberately\n63: > lacks the methods the contract requires.\"\n64: \n65: Verified: `Clock` requires 
`freezeAt`/`advance` (contracts.ts:54-57); `createFixtureAdapter`\n66: requires a `ControlledClock` (index.ts:41); the 
ledger constructs one unconditionally\n67: (index.ts:175).\n68: \n69: **Remedy adopted:** the contract and 
construction become tier-aware. Integration constructs an\n70: attested real clock with NO control seam; the 
`clock.controlled` witness gains the\n71: attested-real branch its refusal text already anticipates (\"no attested 
real clock backing\"),\n72: granting `real` only on the harness's own real-clock constructor's positive attestation. 
The\n73: per-tier context types (D5) carry the difference: an integration body's clock type exposes no\n74: 
`freezeAt`/`advance`, so misuse fails typecheck. The live adapter factory has its own signature\n75: and does not take 
a `ControlledClock`. Loop types and loop construction are byte-identical to\n76: before.\n77: \n78: ## Finding 5 — 
ACCEPT\n79: \n80: > \"D3 assigns provenance at the wrong granularity because every REQ-001 id shares the single\n81: > 
`sut.accounts` key.\"\n82: \n83: Verified: the suite binds exactly one sut key, `accounts` (_bind.ts:34-40).\n84: 
\n85: **Remedy adopted:** method-level backing inside the one key, exactly the \"method-level provenance\n86: and a 
new callable pending mechanism\" the finding names. The live adapter exports a closed\n87: enumeration of BACKED 
method names (finding 2); unbacked methods become callable pending proxies\n88: that throw `CapabilityPending` naming 
`sut.accounts.<method>` ON USE. A pending-on-use proxy is\n89: not a ledger stand-in — it fakes nothing and can never 
produce a green — so the whole-ledger gate\n90: passes while any id that leans on an unbacked method goes declarably 
red at use time. The\n91: `sut.accounts` ledger entry is `real` with evidence naming the backed enumeration and 
the\n92: attestation; the failure direction stays false-red, never false-green. D2's gate semantics amend\n93: 
accordingly: the gate still refuses any ledger STAND-IN above loop; use-time refusal covers the\n94: method axis.\n95: 
\n96: ## Finding 6 — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY\n97: \n98: > \"The transient `jwt_expiry` step has neither a safe 
slot-management mechanism nor an oracle\n99: > capable of proving restoration.\"\n100: \n101: Verified: `prepare()` 
owns the config mirror, the generated write, the hash marker and restarts\n102: (db-pool.ts:1184-1205); the child 
holds coordinates only. A body editing the repository file\n103: changes nothing in the running slot; a body editing 
the slot directly breaks the marker\n104: discipline; `git diff supabase/config.toml` measures the wrong object. All 
three points stand.\n105: \n106: **Remedy (different from the plan's):** there is NO transient override and NO 
restoration to\n107: prove. `generateSlotConfig` (db-pool.ts:308) gains one sanctioned, pinned transform: slot 
configs\n108: set a STANDING low `auth.jwt_expiry` (target ~120 s; exact value settled by step 5's\n109: verify-first 
against the local stack's accepted range). The value rides the existing\n110: generated-config + hash-marker + restart 
machinery, so it is applied exactly when absent and\n111: never needs restoring — it IS the slot's config, identical 
every run. This also makes the expiry\n112: arm of AT-001.12 and the client auto-refresh of AT-001.13 provable inside 
the single manifest\n113: run, with zero mid-run mutation. Struck from step 6: the in-run restore and the `git diff` 
proof.\n114: Cost: the two session-lifetime bodies each wait roughly one expiry window; accepted (D1 already\n115: 
accepted minutes per item). The generator's own comment says any other difference means the slot\n116: grades 
different behaviour — this is a deliberate, reviewed extension of its permitted transform\n117: set, asserted by its 
selftests.\n118: \n119: ## Finding 7 — ACCEPT\n120: \n121: > \"The plan declares AT-001.13 green using evidence that 
explicitly does not prove automatic\n122: > refresh.\"\n123: \n124: Verified: the criterion demands refresh 
\"automatically without forced re-login mid-work\"\n125: (at-req-001.md:28); the loop body calls `refreshSession` 
explicitly and says automatic\n126: scheduling is unproved; the transcript says only the mechanism was proved. A 
mechanism-only\n127: green at the tier whose meaning is \"proved for real\" would be a new overclaim made by this 
item.\n128: \n129: **Remedy adopted:** AT-001.13 leaves the unconditional floor. Green is permitted ONLY with a\n130: 
genuine automatic-refresh body: a real client (supabase-js with `autoRefreshToken: true`)\n131: against the slot stack 
under finding 6's standing low expiry, observing token rotation occur\n132: WITHOUT any explicit refresh call, then 
continued access. Feasibility joins step 5's\n133: verify-first list; if auto-refresh cannot be observed 
deterministically, the id is declared red\n134: with its exact kind — never a mechanism-only green. The loop tier's 
existing green for this id\n135: is pre-existing on main and is not relitigated by this item.\n136: \n137: ## Finding 
8 — ACCEPT\n138: \n139: > \"The selected AT-001.09 migration omits the check that proves verification for both\n140: > 
email-capable account types.\"\n141: \n142: Verified: the criterion says \"EITHER account type ... (NGO and 
volunteer)\" and carries the\n143: \"parameterized over account types\" note (at-req-001.md:20). The migrated set (a, 
b, b2, d) is\n144: NGO-only; the volunteer path is the email item's proof check (e).\n145: \n146: **Remedy adopted:** 
step 6's AT-001.09 body is parameterized over both account types, the\n147: volunteer path following the transcript's 
check (e) recipe.\n148: \n149: ## Finding 9 — ACCEPT\n150: \n151: > \"Signup atomicity does not prove AT-001.01, yet 
the plan makes that id a mandatory\n152: > integration green.\"\n153: \n154: Verified: the criterion demands the 
account with type NGO, the org admin membership, the\n155: recorded acknowledgment (timestamp, IP, text version) 
gating project creation, and a later\n156: sign-in (at-req-001.md:9). Atomicity is the negative arm only.\n157: \n158: 
**Remedy adopted:** AT-001.01 leaves the unconditional floor. Green requires the full-outcome\n159: oracle: successful 
NGO signup asserting the account type, the org row, the admin membership, the\n160: acknowledgment fields, the 
