Verify-first record — AI4DEV-81 (per-item integration verification)\n2: \n3: Written by the DRAFT executor. Two parts, 
in the order the amended plan asks for them:\n4: \n5: - **Part A** — step 5's four VERIFY-FIRST questions (a)-(d), 
each with the command that answered it.\n6:   Every one was measured against **slot 1**, on this machine, on 
2026-08-11.\n7: - **Part B** — step 1's confirmations. The plan's table is already settled; each cell marked\n8:   
\"step 1 confirms\" is checked here against the three proof transcripts and the deployed surfaces.\n9:   A 
confirmation that CONTRADICTS the settled disposition is reported as a dispute and is NOT\n10:   written into the 
table by this executor.\n11: \n12: Everything below is one machine's word, exactly as the three proof transcripts are. 
Nothing here is\n13: re-runnable by a reviewer without the same slot.\n14: \n15: ---\n16: \n17: ## Part A — step 5's 
verify-first questions\n18: \n19: ### (a) Does the slot's stack serve the edge functions from its mirrored 
`supabase/`?\n20: \n21: **ANSWER: yes. The slot's own edge-runtime container serves them. No separate `functions 
serve`\n22: process is managed per run.**\n23: \n24: Evidence:\n25: \n26: 1. `docker ps` lists 
`supabase_edge_runtime_ai4good-slot-1` among slot 1's containers, beside\n27:    `supabase_kong_ai4good-slot-1` on 
host port 55321.\n28: 2. A request through the slot's own gateway reaches the function:\n29: \n30:    ```\n31:    POST 
http://127.0.0.1:55321/functions/v1/complete-signup   ->  HTTP 401\n32:    ```\n33: \n34:    401 is the platform's 
`verify_jwt = true` refusal, which only a ROUTED request can receive; an\n35:    unrouted path answers 404. 
`[functions.complete-signup] verify_jwt = true` is in the config the\n36:    slot mirrors.\n37: 3. `supabase status -o 
json` for slot 1 reports `FUNCTIONS_URL = http://127.0.0.1:55321/functions/v1`.\n38: \n39: This differs from the three 
proof transcripts, and the difference is the point: those runs served\n40: functions with `bunx supabase functions 
serve` from the worktree, and AI4DEV-58's transcript records\n41: the hazard that made necessary — a machine found 
serving from a worktree that had been DELETED. The\n42: slot pool removes that hazard: `prepare()` mirrors this tree 
into the slot and the slot's own\n43: edge-runtime container serves what was mirrored. The live adapter therefore 
manages no process.\n44: \n45: **What is NOT yet proved by (a) and is left to the live adapter's own build:** that a 
request the\n46: platform ADMITS reaches this branch's function body. The 401 above is refused before the 
function\n47: runs. The live adapter's first authenticated call is what establishes that, and it is checked 
there.\n48: \n49: ### (b) Does the slot stack serve a mail catcher, and at which port?\n50: \n51: **ANSWER: yes. 
Mailpit v1.30.2. For slot 1 it is `http://127.0.0.1:55324`, and the endpoint is\n52: reported by the slot's OWN status 
rather than computed.**\n53: \n54: Evidence — `supabase status -o json` through the repo's CLI seam 
(`runSupabaseCli(slotTarget(1), …)`)\n55: reports:\n56: \n57: ```\n58: MAILPIT_URL  = http://127.0.0.1:55324\n59: 
INBUCKET_URL = http://127.0.0.1:55324\n60: ```\n61: \n62: and the catcher answers:\n63: \n64: ```\n65: GET 
http://127.0.0.1:55324/api/v1/info      -> 200 {\"Version\":\"v1.30.2\", …}\n66: GET 
http://127.0.0.1:55324/api/v1/messages  -> 200 {\"total\":0, …,\"messages\":[]}\n67: GET 
http://127.0.0.1:55324/api/v1/mailbox/probe -> 404   (the Inbucket shape; absent)\n68: ```\n69: \n70: 55324 is 
`[local_smtp] port = 54324` in this tree's `supabase/config.toml` moved by the slot\n71: overlay's +1000-per-slot 
listener rule. **The live capability reads `MAILPIT_URL` off the status the\n72: runner already proved, never the 
+1000 arithmetic** — the arithmetic is the pool's business and a\n73: second copy of it here would be the 
two-statements-one-fact defect.\n74: \n75: The API shape agrees with what AI4DEV-59's and AI4DEV-60's transcripts 
measured on the personal\n76: stack (\"mail catcher shape, measured: mailpit ... the Inbucket shape answered 404\"), 
so the recipe\n77: those transcripts carry transfers unchanged.\n78: \n79: ### (c) What is the accepted range for 
`auth.jwt_expiry`, and what value does D12 pin?\n80: \n81: **ANSWER: the pinned CLI accepts every low value probed, 
and the running GoTrue honours 120 s\n82: end to end. D12 pins `auth.jwt_expiry = 120`.**\n83: \n84: Evidence, in two 
steps.\n85: \n86: 1. **Parse-time.** Slot 1's config was rewritten with each candidate and `supabase status -o 
json`\n87:    was run against it through the CLI seam (a read-only command that parses the file). The slot's\n88:    
config text was restored afterwards.\n89: \n90:    ```\n91:    jwt_expiry=1    exit=0  no complaint\n92:    
jwt_expiry=30   exit=0  no complaint\n93:    jwt_expiry=60   exit=0  no complaint\n94:    jwt_expiry=120  exit=0  no 
complaint\n95:    jwt_expiry=300  exit=0  no complaint\n96:    ```\n97: \n98:    **This measures only that the CLI 
does not REFUSE the value.** It is not evidence that GoTrue\n99:    honours it, and it is written down separately for 
that reason.\n100: \n101: 2. **Run-time — the load-bearing half.** Slot 1 was restarted with `auth.jwt_expiry = 120`, 
a\n102:    confirmed user was created with operator authority, and a password grant was read:\n103: \n104:    
```\n105:    password grant -> 200 ; expires_in=120 ; the access token's own exp-iat = 120\n106:    ```\n107: \n108:   
 So the value reaches the container and the issued token really carries it.\n109: \n110: **Why 120 and not lower.** 
AI4DEV-60's transcript used a TRANSIENT `jwt_expiry = 5`, which suited a\n111: script that slept 10 seconds. D12 makes 
the value STANDING, so every id in the manifest runs under\n112: it, and a 5-second access token would expire inside 
ordinary test steps and turn unrelated ids red.\n113: 120 s is long enough that no body except the two that WANT an 
expiry ever meets one, and short\n114: enough that those two wait about two minutes. The cost is the one D1 already 
accepted.\n115: \n116: ### (d) Is supabase-js `autoRefreshToken` rotation deterministically observable under that 
expiry?\n117: \n118: **ANSWER: yes. Rotation was observed with NO explicit refresh call, and access continued 
afterwards.**\n119: \n120: Evidence, against slot 1 at `jwt_expiry = 120`, with\n121: `createClient(url, anon, { auth: 
{ autoRefreshToken: true, persistSession: false } })`:\n122: \n123: ```\n124: supabase-js sign-in -> ok ; token 
exp-iat=120\n125: --- waiting for an automatic refresh, with NO explicit refresh call\n126: AUTO-REFRESH OBSERVED 
after 30s ; events=INITIAL_SESSION,TOKEN_REFRESHED\n127: continued access with the rotated token -> 200\n128: 
```\n129: \n130: The observable is the pair: the `TOKEN_REFRESHED` event, and `getSession()` handing back an 
access\n131: token that is not the one sign-in returned. Neither is a self-report by the harness — both come 
from\n132: the client library, and the rotated token is then spent against `/auth/v1/user`.\n133: \n134: **Timing, 
stated exactly.** Rotation happened about 30 seconds into a 120-second token, not at its\n135: expiry: supabase-js 
runs an auto-refresh tick and refreshes while the token still has life. That is\n136: what makes it DETERMINISTIC 
rather than a race — the body does not have to wait out the expiry, it\n137: has to wait out one tick. The integration 
body waits with a bounded poll and fails loudly if no\n138: rotation appears.\n139: \n140: **One reading in the probe 
transcript that must not be misread**: the probe also spent the ORIGINAL\n141: token after the rotation and got 200. 
That is correct and is not a defect — at 30 seconds the\n142: original token had 90 seconds of life left. It is NOT 
evidence about what an expired token does;\n143: AT-001.12's expiry arm measures that separately, by waiting past the 
full 120 s.\n144: \n145: ### Slot 1's state after Part A\n146: \n147: The probe restored slot 1's config TEXT but left 
