here, one named after each provenance, and a capability's label\n5:  * was simply the one you called. Flipping the 
five stand-in call sites in `index.ts` to the other\n6:  * factory emptied the stand-in ledger, and `registry.ts` — 
which refuses ANY stubbed capability\n7:  * above the loop tier — then had nothing to refuse. A one-word edit that 
reads like a routine\n8:  * promotion turned the closing gate green against a reference adapter.\n9:  *\n10:  * So a 
capability is now built by ONE constructor that takes a name, a value, and any evidence the\n11:  * caller holds that 
a witness cannot derive. A witness registered for that name returns a verdict —\n12:  * or throws. There are THREE 
outcomes, and the third one is the whole point:\n13:  *\n14:  *   stand-in  — the witness found the seam that makes 
this a substitute, and says which seam.\n15:  *   real      — the witness has positive grounds, and says what they 
are.\n16:  *   REFUSAL   — the witness cannot classify the value, so it refuses to classify it.\n17:  *\n18:  * \"I 
found no stand-in seam\" is NEVER evidence of real backing. A `clock.controlled` value with its\n19:  * `advance` 
stripped is unclassifiable, not real; classifying it real would be the deleted\n20:  * real-labelling factory reached 
through a different door — exactly the door this file exists to\n21:  * close.\n22:  *\n23:  * The same rule covers 
the name itself: a capability name no witness has been registered for is an\n24:  * error at construction, naming the 
name. Not defaulted to real, and not quietly defaulted to\n25:  * stand-in either. `index.ts` and `registry.ts` have 
both written the reason down already — a guard\n26:  * that switches itself off when an entry is absent is the hole 
again, arriving through the door\n27:  * marked convenience.\n28:  */\n29: \n30: const CAPABILITY = 
Symbol('at-capability');\n31: \n32: export type CapabilityProvenance = 'real' | 'stand-in';\n33: \n34: /** A witness 
returns one of these, or it throws. There is no `null` and there is no default. */\n35: export type CapabilityVerdict 
=\n36:   | { readonly kind: 'stand-in'; readonly reason: string }\n37:   | { readonly kind: 'real'; readonly evidence: 
string };\n38: \n39: /**\n40:  * The one place a caller contributes anything, and it is deliberately narrow: facts a 
witness\n41:  * cannot derive from the value in front of it. Only `oracles.judge` needs any of it — the running\n42:  
* tier and the judge transport's `kind` brand, both of which `oracles.ts` already guards and\n43:  * refuses in both 
mismatched directions before it ever gets here.\n44:  */\n45: export type CapabilityEvidence = {\n46:   readonly 
tier?: string;\n47:   readonly transport?: string;\n48: };\n49: \n50: /* 
------------------------------------------------------------------ the live attestation brand */\n51: \n52: /**\n53:  
* POSITIVE GROUNDS ARE A ROUND TRIP THAT HAPPENED, NEVER A SHAPE THAT LOOKS RIGHT.\n54:  *\n55:  * 
`localStackProblems()` in `runner.ts` checks that a URL points at the loopback address on the\n56:  * slot's port and 
that a key decodes as a local development JWT. Every one of those is fabricable by\n57:  * a caller with a text editor 
and no database answering anywhere — they GUARD the founder's personal\n58:  * stack, which is what they were written 
for, and they establish nothing positive at all. A witness\n59:  * that read them as grounds would grant `real` to 
four plausible strings.\n60:  *\n61:  * So a live capability carries an ATTESTATION: an object the harness's own live 
constructors stamp\n62:  * onto the value AFTER a round trip that could only have succeeded against the prepared slot. 
The\n63:  * round trip is `attestation.ts`'s — `prepare()` mints a nonce, writes it into the slot database\n64:  * 
after the reset, and the child reads it back THROUGH the coordinates it was handed. \"These\n65:  * coordinates 
answered with this run's runner-minted value\" is the grounds; nothing weaker is.\n66:  *\n67:  * WHAT THIS FILE CAN 
AND CANNOT CHECK, said exactly, because a closure claim wider than the truth is\n68:  * the defect this file exists to 
remove. It has no I/O and performs no round trip. It checks that a\n69:  * value carries a well-formed attestation 
stamped FOR THAT CAPABILITY NAME, with non-empty evidence.\n70:  * The round trip itself is `attestation.ts`'s to 
perform and to refuse. What that buys is the same\n71:  * thing the two-route partition buys elsewhere: a name cannot 
be granted `real` through a door that\n72:  * asks nothing, and the one door that does ask is a single, reviewed 
function.\n73:  *\n74:  * THE SYMBOL IS EXPORTED, so a determined caller can import it and stamp an object — exactly 
as a\n75:  * determined caller could import any constructor. That is the same line `suite-adapters.ts` draws:\n76:  * 
the threat model is an honest mistake nothing can notice, not an author set on defeating the\n77:  * design, who has 
to write something at least as deliberate as a cast.\n78:  */\n79: export const ATTESTATION = 
Symbol('at-live-attestation');\n80: \n81: /**\n82:  * The brand a SLOT attestation carries. It is stamped onto the 
attestation OBJECT ITSELF, so that\n83:  * \"an object with an `evidence` string on it\" — which any caller can write 
— is not the same thing\n84:  * as an attestation, and the live constructors below can tell the two apart with one 
call.\n85:  */\n86: export const SLOT_ATTESTATION_BRAND = 'slot';\n87: \n88: export type LiveAttestation = {\n89:   
/** what was proved, in words a transcript can carry — never a credential */\n90:   readonly evidence: string;\n91:   
/** WHICH capability name this attestation was minted for; a brand for another name is refused */\n92:   readonly 
constructedFor: string;\n93: };\n94: \n95: /** Stamp an attestation onto a live value. Non-enumerable, so it never 
travels into a JSON dump. */\n96: export function stampAttestation<T extends object>(value: T, attestation: 
LiveAttestation): T {\n97:   if (!attestation.evidence.trim()) throw new Error('a live attestation requires non-empty 
evidence');\n98:   if (!attestation.constructedFor.trim()) throw new Error('a live attestation must name the 
capability it was minted for');\n99:   Object.defineProperty(value, ATTESTATION, {\n100:     value: Object.freeze({ 
evidence: attestation.evidence, constructedFor: attestation.constructedFor }),\n101:     enumerable: false,\n102:     
writable: false,\n103:     configurable: false,\n104:   });\n105:   return value;\n106: }\n107: \n108: /** The 
attestation this value carries FOR THIS NAME, or null. A brand for another name is null. */\n109: export function 
attestationOf(value: unknown, constructedFor: string): LiveAttestation | null {\n110:   if (value === null || (typeof 
value !== 'object' && typeof value !== 'function')) return null;\n111:   const carried = (value as Record<symbol, 
unknown>)[ATTESTATION];\n112:   if (carried === null || typeof carried !== 'object') return null;\n113:   const { 
evidence, constructedFor: brandedFor } = carried as Partial<LiveAttestation>;\n114:   if (typeof evidence !== 'string' 
|| evidence.trim() === '') return null;\n115:   if (brandedFor !== constructedFor) return null;\n116:   return { 
evidence, constructedFor };\n117: }\n118: \n119: export interface Capability<T> {\n120:   readonly [CAPABILITY]: 
true;\n121:   readonly name: string;\n122:   readonly provenance: CapabilityProvenance;\n123:   /** the witness's own 
words, non-null EXACTLY when `provenance` is 'stand-in' */\n124:   readonly standInReason: string | null;\n125:   /** 
the witness's own words, non-null EXACTLY when `provenance` is 'real' */\n126:   readonly realEvidence: string | 
null;\n127:   readonly value: T;\n128: }\n129: \n130: type CapabilityWitness = (value: unknown, evidence: 
CapabilityEvidence) => CapabilityVerdict;\n131: \n132: /**\n133:  * CALLABILITY THROUGH THE PROTOTYPE CHAIN, never 
own-property presence. `ControlledClock` is a\n134:  * class and `advance` is a method on its prototype, so 
`Object.hasOwn(clock, 'advance')` is FALSE\n135:  * for the very object the harness builds — an own-property witness 
would refuse today's clock on\n136:  * its first run. Plain property access walks the chain; `typeof` is what asks the 
real question,\n137:  * which is whether the seam can be CALLED.\n138:  */\n139: function callable(value: unknown, 
...path: readonly string[]): boolean {\n140:   let cursor: unknown = value;\n141:   for (const step of path) {\n142:   
  if (cursor === null || (typeof cursor !== 'object' && typeof cursor !== 'function')) return false;\n143:     cursor 
= (cursor as Record<string, unknown>)[step];\n144:   }\n145:   return typeof cursor === 'function';\n146: }\n147: 
\n148: const REFUSAL_DOCTRINE =\n149:   'Finding no stand-in seam is not evidence of real backing — a witness that 
cannot classify a value refuses it.';\n150: \n151: /**\n152:  * THE THREE NAME-SCOPED DECLARATIONS, said plainly 
instead of dressed up as checks.\n153:  *\n154:  * `config.registry`, `sentinels.planted` and `faults.injection` 
inspect nothing about their value,\n155:  * and that is honest rather than lazy: the thing each of them would be a 
stand-in FOR does not\n156:  * exist anywhere else. `atconfig.ts` IS the registry of pinned values; the marker store 
IS the\n157:  * article; the fault router IS the article. `stubbedCapabilities()` reports what the HARNESS\n158:  * 
substituted, and nothing here is substituted — marking them stand-in would tell an\n159:  * integration-tier run the 
gate is grading a substitute, which is a lie in the other direction, and\n160:  * would bar this identical machinery 
from the tier that is the closing gate.\n161:  *\n162:  * Writing them as a shape test would make them look like 
measurements. They are decisions about a\n163:  * name, they are scoped to that name, and a reader is entitled to know 
