/**\n372:  * Serialize every destructive run against one stack. The key is project id + api port because\n373:  * that 
pair IS the stack's identity: a second checkout carrying the same `project_id` shares the\n374:  * same Docker 
containers and the same ports, so its `at:verify` would reset this run's database\n375:  * out from under it. Mirrors 
`Acquire-WorkLock` in loop/work/work-lib.ps1 — exclusive create,\n376:  * holder recorded, stale takeover.\n377:  
*\n378:  * TAKEOVER IS THE WHOLE DIFFICULTY. Two processes can both read the same leftover lock and both\n379:  * 
conclude it is stale; if each then removed it and created its own, the second removal would take\n380:  * out the 
FIRST one's brand-new live lock and both would run — the precise failure this exists to\n381:  * prevent. Moving the 
stale file aside with an atomic rename does NOT fix that: the winner\n382:  * repopulates the path microseconds later, 
so the loser's rename succeeds against a LIVE lock,\n383:  * which is just as destructive as unlinking it. (Proved 
with a two-contender race, not reasoned\n384:  * about; the race is in runner.selftest.ts.)\n385:  *\n386:  * So the 
takeover DECISION is serialized by a second exclusive-create lock — the gate. Only one\n387:  * process is ever inside 
it, and it re-reads the holder INSIDE, so:\n388:  *   - no other process can be taking over concurrently (the gate is 
exclusive);\n389:  *   - no ordinary claim can have slipped in, because the stale file occupies the path 
continuously\n390:  *     until the gate holder removes it, and an exclusive create only succeeds on a free 
path;\n391:  *   - if a third process wins the claim in the instant after removal, the gate holder's own create\n392:  
*     simply fails and it restarts, having deleted nothing further.\n393:  * A gate stranded by a killed process is 
cleared on age. The section lasts milliseconds, so a live\n394:  * holder never reaches that threshold, and clearing 
one wrongly degrades to two takers in the\n395:  * section — today's behaviour, not worse.\n396:  */\n397: export 
