HEAD:loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10239:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:2:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290388882,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_FIjuOluyW2NhJc9NRS7f2224","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\plan.md"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\plan.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # AI4DEV-59 (email verification, unverified-write gate) — PLAN\n2: \n3: **Sitting 1 of the item: PLAN. Ruled by the `orchestrator` definition on fable (claude-fable-5,\n4: effort xhigh).**\n5: \n6: **Sitting 2 (DRAFT, same definition and model) ruled on gate 1's four findings and amended this\n7: plan. The rulings are section 7. The amended text below is what gets built; the pre-amendment\n8: text is commit `46b7485`.**\n9: \n10: **Sitting 3 (FIX AND GOAL, same definition and model) ruled on gate 2's twelve findings — nine\n11: from seat A, three from seat B, two convergent pairs. The rulings are section 8. That sitting\n12: amended step 2's mirror-binding sentence and step 5's done-list; the pre-amendment text is\n13: commit `ac33db1`.**\n14: \n15: **Chain, derived from the branch**\n16: (`nirdrang/ai4dev-59-email-verification-and-the-gate-on-unverified-writing-d2l1`):\n17: AI4DEV-59 (email verification, unverified-write gate) → AI4DEV-52 (verification, sessions and\n18: reset container) → AI4DEV-50 (auth dev-tree root) → AI4PM-19 (the authentication requirement).\n19: No `attr:` label on any of them; product work with an evidence gate above it.\n20: \n21: **Manifest:** `loop/decomp/req-001.md`, deliverable D2 leaf L1, revision `0579425`:\n22: *\"verification flow (all email-capable account types) + unverified-write gate on Discovery\n23: messages · verify: AT-001.09,10 · blocked-by: D1.L1\"*. The D1 container is Done on the board\n24: (its two leaves merged as pull requests #47 and #48), so the blocker is cleared.\n25: \n26: **Acceptance ids this leaf owns:** AT-001.09, AT-001.10.\n27: **Acceptance text (read-only for this item):** `.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`\n28: lines 20–21. Line 22 matters too: **AT-001.11 is retired** — *\"verification-link\n29: expiry/single-use/resend semantics are not stated in REQ-001\"* — so no assertion in this item\n30: may claim those semantics.\n31: \n32: **Ratified product text behind the gate:** the PRD makes email verification *\"the floor for any\n33: Discovery message\"* and says vetting is *\"never the Discovery wall\"* (decision-8,\n34: `.taskmaster/docs/prd-new.md` line 787; also lines 155 and 159, and\n35: `.taskmaster/docs/prd-mvp.md` line 157).\n36: \n37: **Verify command, pinned:** `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect`. The fixture\n38: adapter is a stand-in capped at the loop tier (its own header, `_fixture.ts` lines 7–10;\n39: `adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts`), CI has no database, and the\n40: requirement's integration-tier done contract remains AI4PM-19's gate, not this leaf's.\n41: \n42: ---\n43: \n44: ## 1. What I verified in the tree before deciding anything\n45: \n46: | claim | verdict | evidence (pointer) |\n47: |---|---|---|\n48: | Both ids are declared red, pending on this leaf | true | `tests/at/expected/req-001.json` lines 15–16; stubs at `tests/at/suites/req-001/b-verification-and-sessions.test.ts` lines 13 and 15, each `notLanded(LEAF.D2_L1)` |\n49: | The config flip is reserved for this leaf | true | `supabase/config.toml` line 243 `enable_confirmations = false`; the b-file header (lines 4–7) says turning it on \"is the verification leaf's own change\" |\n50: | A local mail catcher is enabled | true | `supabase/config.toml` lines 105–108: `[local_smtp]` enabled, web interface on port 54324 |\n51: | The local stack limits auth emails to 2 per hour | true (as configured) | `supabase/config.toml` line 216, `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent = 2`; whether it binds with the local test mailer is measured in step 5, not assumed |\n52: | No Discovery product code exists anywhere | true | grep `discovery` over `supabase/`, `src/`, `tests/` — only docs, a harness rubric for another requirement, and this suite's own stub title; the future route is mapped to a `discovery-message` edge function in `.taskmaster/docs/migration-nextjs-to-tanstack-VERIFIED.md` lines 103–105 |\n53: | The decision-module pattern is established: shipped judgements, fixture storage | true | `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts` header; `_fixture.ts` header and its imports (lines 45–62) |\n54: | The suite already models \"the hook a later leaf must call\" | true | `hasPlatformAcknowledgment`'s contract comment, `_contract.ts` lines 244–260 — enforcement it cannot build is named as the hook the later leaf calls |\n55: | The caller-fact-through-shipped-extractor pattern is ratified | true | `_fixture.ts` lines 208–225 (gate-2 ruling R3 of the GitHub leaf): the fixture renders the canonical GoTrue user shape and derives the fact through the shipped extractor |\n56: | `_pending.ts` header counts will go stale | true | `tests/at/suites/req-001/_pending.ts` lines 3 and 9 say 30 pending / seven written; after this item it is 28 / nine |\n57: | CI runs typecheck, harness self-tests, at:check and at:verify (loop, --expect) per suite, plus the ownership and reference guards | true | `.github/workflows/ci.yml` |\n58: | GoTrue with confirmations on issues no session at signup and refuses sign-in until the address is confirmed | **unverified-runtime-claim** | expected GoTrue behaviour; measured, with exact refusal text captured, in step 5 — nothing in this plan silently depends on it before then |\n59: \n60: ## 2. Decisions\n61: \n62: ### D-A — \"Verified\" is Supabase Auth's own fact. No migration, no new column.\n63: The observable behind AT-001.09 is `email_confirmed_at` on the auth user — GoTrue's field, set\n64: by GoTrue when the emailed link is used. Nothing this leaf ships writes it, `public.accounts`\n65: gains no column, and this item ships **no migration**. Consequence: there is no schema, RLS or\n66: privilege surface to prove here; the live proof reads `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` as\n67: operator evidence only.\n68: \n69: ### D-B — The config flip, and one pre-authorized relief valve\n70: `[auth.email] enable_confirmations` flips to `true` in `supabase/config.toml` — the change the\n71: b-file header explicitly reserves for this leaf. The comment beside it is updated to say what it\n72: changes and which leaf owns it. **Pre-authorized amendment:** if step 5's registrations starve\n73: on the 2-per-hour `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent` limit (429 or absent mail), the executor raises\n74: that value (e.g. to 100) in the same file, restarts the stack, and records the raise in the\n75: transcript. This is local-stack configuration; no hosted deployment exists.\n76: \n77: ### D-C — The gate is a shipped decision module: `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`\n78: A new module under the same two constraints as `accounts.ts` (relative imports only, no Deno\n79: global, no I/O; its type coverage is its import from the acceptance program). Two exports:\n80: \n81: - `emailVerifiedFromUser(user: unknown): boolean` — judges the GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` response\n82:   shape: a non-empty string `email_confirmed_at` means verified; **anything else — missing\n83:   field, null, non-string, malformed object — means unverified.** Fail closed.\n84: - `discoveryMessageAllowed(caller: { emailVerified: boolean }): Decision<'verified'>` —\n85:   `Decision` imported from `./accounts.ts`. The ONLY allow is `caller?.emailVerified === true`;\n86:   a missing or malformed caller argument refuses (the same fail-closed posture, for the same\n87:   measured reason: edge entry points have no type-checker). The refusal reason names email\n88:   verification as what is missing AND as the remedy, in words AT-001.10's assertion can match.\n89: \n90: The gate is **type-blind**: decision-8 makes verification the floor for ANY Discovery message.\n91: AT-001.10's unverified-NGO subject is an instance of that rule, not its scope, and a gate that\n92: read the account type would be shipping a distinction no ratified text draws.\n93: \n94: Why a new module rather than growing `accounts.ts`: that file's header scopes it to the account\n95: deliverable's decisions; `github.ts` already set the several-small-modules precedent; and this\n96: rule's future caller is the Discovery send route, not signup.\n97: \n98: ### D-D — No Discovery surface ships. The gate is the hook the route must call.\n99: The Discovery message route belongs to REQ-002/004 (the migration doc maps it to a future\n100: `discovery-message` edge function). Building an edge function or a table for it here would be\n101: another requirement's surface invented early. What this leaf ships is the **decision** that\n102: route must consult — exactly the pattern the suite already ratifies for\n103: `hasPlatformAcknowledgment` (\"this is the hook the leaf that lands project creation must call\").\n104: The module header and section 4 both state it plainly: **the gate has no deployed caller yet**,\n105: and no green from this item may be read as \"Discovery messaging is gated in production.\"\n106: \n107: At the loop tier the SUT gains a `sendDiscoveryMessage` operation and the fixture is the\n108: stand-in surface: it renders the caller's GoTrue user shape from stored Auth state, derives the\n109: verified fact through the SHIPPED `emailVerifiedFromUser`, consults the SHIPPED\n110: `discoveryMessageAllowed`, refuses with the gate's own reason and **writes nothing**, or on\n111: allow records the message in fixture storage. Every judgement is the shipped module's; the\n112: storage is a Map like all the rest.\n113: \n114: ### D-E — Fixture and contract: mirror Auth's verification state, judge nothing\n115: `_fixture.ts`: `AuthUser` gains `emailConfirmedAt: string | null` and\n116: `verificationLink: string | null`. An email/password registration starts unconfirmed and mints\n117: a link value (the fixture's stand-in for the emailed link the mail catcher would hold). A\n118: provider registration (Google/GitHub) starts confirmed — the expected GoTrue behaviour when the\n119: provider vouches for the address. **This mirror is UNBOUND by this item** (gate-1 ruling [1],\n120: section 7): no OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, so no live provider session\n121: is obtainable — the same recorded gap that leaves the OAuth handshake itself unproved at every\n122: tier, stated in the fixture header since the predecessor. No test this item reads a provider\n123: user's verified state; both new bodies register by email/password. If the prediction is wrong —\n124: a real provider user arriving unconfirmed — the shipped gate fails closed: it refuses, never\n125: allows. The fixture header's mirror section states all of this; the mirror is bound by the\n126: first item that ships a real provider-path consumer.\n127: \n128: `_contract.ts`: `AccountsSut` gains five members —\n129: - `emailVerified(accountId): Promise<boolean>` — read-back, derived through the shipped\n130:   `emailVerifiedFromUser` over the rendered GoTrue user shape (the R3 pattern: the extractor\n131:   sits on the tested path, exactly as the future route's caller resolution would).\n132: - `emailedVerificationLink(email): Promise<string | null>` — the emailed verification link for\n133:   the address, or null when none was emailed.\n134: - `useVerificationLink(link): Promise<{ ok: boolean }>` — using an emailed link confirms the\n135:   address; a never-issued link returns `ok: false` and flips nothing. **No expiry, no\n136:   single-use, no resend is modelled or asserted — those are retired AT-001.11's semantics,\n137:   deliberately unstated in REQ-001.** The never-issued negative is not AT-001.11 ground: a\n138:   link-shaped string that verifies an account it never belonged to would make \"using the link\n139:   flips it\" meaningless, so the negative guards this test's own oracle.\n140: - `sendDiscoveryMessage(session, body: string): Promise<{ ok: true } | { ok: false; reason:\n141:   string }>` — per D-D. A caller with no completed account gets the fixture's bookkeeping\n142:   refusal, same shape as `createOrganization`'s.\n143: - `discoveryMessagesBy(accountId): Promise<string[]>` — read-back, because a block whose write\n144:   happened anyway is not a block.\n145: \n146: `registerWithEmailPassword` keeps returning a `Session`. The fixture's `Session` has always\n147: been an identity handle, not an access token — session ISSUANCE (and with it \"an unconfirmed\n148: user holds no session\") is D2.L2's subject. The honest gap this leaves is stated in section 4\n149: and in the test comments, not hidden: at the loop tier a completed-but-unverified account is\n150: constructible; on the live stack the public path cannot reach it (step 5 measures that).\n151: \n152: ### D-F — AT-001.09's body: parameterized over both types, surface `ui`\n153: For EACH of `ngo` and `volunteer` (the two email-capable public types —\n154: `PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES`; the platform admin is provisioned, not signed up, and is outside\n155: this criterion's \"can register by email\"):\n156: 1. register with email/password → `emailVerified` is **false** — the fresh signup is\n157:    email-unverified.\n158: 2. complete signup (NGO with an organisation name; volunteer after `linkGithubIdentity`, the\n159:    precondition AT-001.04 owns) → the account row carries its type — which makes \"either\n160:    account type\" a fact of the run, not a label.\n161: 3. still `emailVerified` false — completion does not verify.\n162: 4. negative: `useVerificationLink('never-issued-link')` → `ok: false`, and `emailVerified`\n163:    still false.\n164: 5. `emailedVerificationLink(email)` → non-null; `useVerificationLink(link)` → `ok: true`;\n165:    `emailVerified` → **true** — using the emailed link is what flips it.\n166: 6. the account row still carries the same type — verification re-types nothing.\n167: \n168: Surface mark `ui`: the verification screen is one of the four auth screens the manifest's\n169: wiring leaf names (*\"signup, sign-in, verification, reset\"*), so D2.LW must find this id to\n170: re-run `--wired`.\n171: \n172: Ordering note, stated so the review can attack it rather than discover it: the test completes\n173: signup BEFORE verifying, so that the typed account is observably unverified — the criterion's\n174: \"the account … is email-unverified until the link is used\" read with \"account\" meaning the\n175: typed account. On the live stack with confirmations on the public order is verify-then-complete\n176: (no session exists before confirmation); step 5 drives THAT order against the real stack. The\n177: two orders together cover both readings; neither contradicts any criterion.\n178: \n179: ### D-G — AT-001.10's body: the discriminating pair, surface `backend`\n180: 1. register an NGO by email/password and complete signup **without verifying** (reachable at\n181:    the loop tier per D-E; the body's comment states plainly that the live public path cannot\n182:    reach this state and why the gate exists anyway — decision-8 makes the floor the write\n183:    path's own rule, not a property borrowed from the session layer, and non-public paths and\n184:    configuration history can produce exactly this state).\n185: 2. `sendDiscoveryMessage` → refused; the reason matches `/verif/i` and `/email/i` —\n186:    verification named as the remedy; `discoveryMessagesBy` is empty — the blocked attempt\n187:    wrote nothing.\n188: 3. the discriminating control: use the emailed verification link → `emailVerified` true → the\n189:    SAME send now succeeds and `discoveryMessagesBy` records it. The remedy is proved by being\n190:    the exact change that unblocks, and a refuse-everybody gate fails this half.\n191: \n192: Surface mark `backend`: the Discovery composer is REQ-002/004's surface; it is not one of the\n193: four auth screens, and the wiring leaf has nothing of it to re-run.\n194: \n195: ### D-H — Suite bookkeeping, all in the same change\n196: - `tests/at/expected/req-001.json`: AT-001.09 and AT-001.10 move from `red` to `green` — 9\n197:   green / 28 declared red.\n198: - `_pending.ts`: `D2_L1` is removed from `LEAF` (the orphan rule — its last two users become\n199:   real bodies), and the header's counts and \"seven are written\" sentence are corrected —\n200:   after this item they are false statements of fact, the audit's first box.\n201: - The b-file header is rewritten: its `enable_confirmations` sentence becomes false the moment\n202:   this leaf lands. `LEAF`/`notLanded` imports stay — four D2.L2 stubs still use them.\n203: - `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/pending-ledger.txt`: 28 lines, one per remaining pending id, each\n204:   naming a deliverable-and-leaf that appears in `loop/decomp/req-001.md`; this leaf's ids\n205:   absent.\n206: \n207: ### D-I — One review slice\n208: Estimated diff outside `loop/items/`: ~70 lines shared module, ~10 config, ~60 contract, ~90\n209: fixture, ~140 suite bodies and headers, ~50 shape selftest (gate-1 ruling [4]), ~15 declaration\n210: and pending bookkeeping — roughly 450.\n211: Far under the 1200-line trigger the previous two leaves used. **One slice**; both pinned\n212: draft-code readers read the whole diff.\n213: \n214: ## 3. Steps, each with its own done-criterion\n215: \n216: Item evidence lives in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/`. Every path repository-relative. The executor\n217: commits one commit per step (the parking rule).\n218: \n219: **Step 0 — baseline.** `bun run typecheck`, `bun run at:selftest`, `bun run at:check req-001`,\n220: `bun run at:check req-016`, `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect`,\n221: `bun run at:verify req-016 --tier loop --expect`.\n222: → done: all exit 0, transcript in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/baseline.txt` (req-001 showing 7 green /\n223: 30 declared red). A red baseline is reported, not worked around.\n224: \n225: **Step 1 — the shipped gate.** `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts` per D-C, header\n226: naming the hook contract (D-D) and the fail-closed posture.\n227: → done: `bun run typecheck` exits 0 with the module imported by the acceptance program (via the\n228: fixture); the refusal reason names email verification as the remedy; and a direct shape test —\n229: `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` (gate-1 ruling [4], section 7) — proves the\n230: fail-closed promise: `emailVerifiedFromUser` returns false for a missing field, `null`, an\n231: empty string, a number, an object value, and a non-object user, and true only for a non-empty\n232: string; `discoveryMessageAllowed` refuses a missing and a malformed caller in the same file.\n233: The vitest include `harness/**/*.selftest.ts` and the `at:selftest` filter pick it up with zero\n234: script or CI change — verified against `tests/at/vitest.config.ts` line 16 and the\n235: `package.json` script. Its header states why a shipped module's shape test rides the selftest\n236: lane: the promise cannot arise through the fixture, and this lane is what CI already runs.\n237: \n238: **Step 2 — contract and fixture.** The D-E extensions, judgements delegated per D-C/D-D.\n239: → done (rewritten by gate-1 ruling [3], section 7 — the earlier criterion was unsatisfiable as\n240: written): `bun run typecheck` exits 0; both PRODUCT judgements — the verified-fact extraction\n241: and the Discovery gate — come only from the shipped `verification.ts`, with no second copy of\n242: either rule in the fixture; and every VENDOR MIRROR this leaf adds is named in the fixture\n243: header's mirror section with what binds it: link issuance at email signup is bound by step 5\n244: (a)–(b); a never-issued link returning `ok: false` is bound by step 5 (b2) — the tampered-link\n245: negative added by gate-2 rulings [A2]/[B1], section 8, because the earlier \"(a)–(d)\" wording\n246: claimed a binding no check measured; a used link setting `emailConfirmedAt` is bound by step 5\n247: (b) and (d); a provider registration starting confirmed is declared UNBOUND, per the D-E\n248: amendment; and the provisioned platform admin starting CONFIRMED mirrors the recorded\n249: provisioning recipe (gate-2 ruling [A8], section 8), read by nothing and live-bound by the\n250: first item that reads an admin's verified state.\n251: \n252: **Step 3 — the two real test bodies, and the bookkeeping.** D-F, D-G, D-H.\n253: → done: `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect` exits 0 with exactly 9 passed and 28\n254: declared red; `bun run at:check req-001` clean; no comment in any touched file still states\n255: that .09/.10 are pending, that the config flip is reserved, or a stale count.\n256: \n257: **Step 4 — the config flip, and the stack with it.** D-B in `supabase/config.toml`; restart the\n258: local stack so auth picks it up.\n259: → done: `bun run db:reset` (or stop/start) completes; `bunx supabase status` shows the stack up;\n260: transcript in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/stack-up.txt`. Two failed attempts → stop and report.\n261: \n262: **Step 5 — the live proof.** `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` against the live stack,\n263: transcript in `proof-local.txt`. Evidence-gathering, run once, never a guarding test. Secrets\n264: redacted exactly as the predecessor transcripts redact them.\n265: → done, all of:\n266:   (a) an email/password signup under the flipped config returns **no session**, and the\n267:   operator read on the database (port 54322) shows `auth.users.email_confirmed_at IS NULL` —\n268:   the unverified-runtime-claims from section 1 measured, with exact wire responses captured;\n269:   (b) the mail catcher on port 54324 holds the confirmation email for that address, and the\n270:   verification link is extracted from it — the catcher's API shape MEASURED first, not assumed;\n271:   (b2) a tampered variant of that link — its token altered, so a token GoTrue never issued —\n272:   is followed BEFORE the real one, and `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` stays NULL: the\n273:   never-issued-link mirror measured rather than inferred (gate-2 rulings [A2]/[B1], section 8);\n274:   no expiry, single-use or resend semantics are touched — the token followed was never issued.\n275:   If only the operator-minted fallback link exists, (b2) runs against a tampered variant of THAT\n276:   link with a note naming the source — a mutated operator-minted token is equally never-issued —\n277:   and it skips only when no link exists at all (sitting-3 ruling on the executor's report);\n278:   (c) sign-in BEFORE confirmation is refused with a CONFIRMATION-SPECIFIC refusal — a 4xx whose\n279:   body names the unconfirmed state (expected GoTrue error code `email_not_confirmed`), captured\n280:   verbatim; a 429 or a 5xx is NOT this refusal and fails the check (gate-2 ruling [A3], section\n281:   8). The discriminator is read from the in-memory body, because the transcript redactor's\n282:   name-pattern also blanks `error_code`, and the note surfaces it through a safe extraction;\n283:   (d) using the link (HTTP GET) flips `email_confirmed_at` to non-null; sign-in now succeeds;\n284:   and the raw `/auth/v1/user` response of the signed-in user, fed to the SHIPPED\n285:   `emailVerifiedFromUser`, returns true — the extractor bound to GoTrue's real serialisation\n286:   (its false half is exercised at the loop tier; an unconfirmed user has no session to read\n287:   `/auth/v1/user` with, and the transcript says so rather than dressing it up);\n288:   (e) both email-capable types flow END TO END under the flipped config (gate-1 ruling [2],\n289:   section 7 — the flip changes the auth context beneath BOTH completion paths, so the\n290:   predecessor's completion evidence predates it and is re-proved, not cited): a second address\n291:   repeats (a)–(d); the NGO address completes signup through the deployed `complete-signup`\n292:   edge function AFTER confirmation; the volunteer address, after confirmation and sign-in,\n293:   gains a GitHub identity by the predecessor's mechanism — the operator-authority insert into\n294:   `auth.identities` (`fabricateGithubIdentity` in the predecessor's proof script, flip-\n295:   independent because it is a database write, not an auth flow) — then completes as a\n296:   volunteer through the same deployed function, and the account row and imported profile are\n297:   read back. The volunteer's \"repeats (a)–(d)\" claim has teeth (gate-2 rulings [A3]/[A4],\n298:   section 8): its link MUST come from the catcher (`linkSource === 'emailed'`) and its\n299:   pre-confirmation sign-in refusal MUST be the same confirmation-specific one as (c);\n300:   (f) if the mailer rate limit starves (b), the D-B relief valve fires and the transcript\n301:   records it.\n302: \n303: **Step 6 — the whole verify surface, green together.** The step-0 list re-run.\n304: → done: all exit 0 in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/verify-final.txt`; req-016 unchanged from baseline.\n305: \n306: ## 4. Expected verification state, per acceptance-test id\n307: \n308: At `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect` when this item is done: **AT-001.01–.07,\n309: .09, .10 green (9), the other 28 P0 ids red as `{\"kind\":\"pending\",\"phase\":\"sut-missing\"}`**,\n310: exit 0.\n311: \n312: | id | proved at loop tier (CI) | proved on the live stack (step 5, one machine) | **not proved by this item** |\n313: |---|---|---|---|\n314: | AT-001.09 | the flow shape against the fixture's Auth mirror: both public types start email-unverified, a never-issued link flips nothing, the emailed link flips the account to verified, the type survives; the shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser` judges the rendered user shape on every read | the real substance: GoTrue issues no session at signup, the confirmation email exists and its link flips `email_confirmed_at`, sign-in is refused before and succeeds after, the shipped extractor answers true against GoTrue's real serialisation | email delivery anywhere but the local mail catcher; any screen (D2.LW's, and Lovable's territory); link expiry / single-use / resend semantics — retired AT-001.11, deliberately unasserted; GoTrue's serialisation of provider-vouched confirmation — the fixture's provider-confirmed start is an unbound vendor mirror (D-E amendment) |\n315: | AT-001.10 | the SHIPPED gate refuses an unverified account's Discovery send with verification named as the remedy and writes nothing, and the SAME send succeeds once verified — the discriminating pair | the auth-layer fact: an unconfirmed email/password user cannot authenticate at all, refusal text captured — on the live stack the block sits upstream of the gate | **enforcement at a deployed Discovery route — none exists.** The route is REQ-002/004's; the gate is the hook it must call, stated in the module header. No green here may be read as \"Discovery messaging is gated in production\" |\n316: | AT-001.01–.07 | unchanged claims, bodies untouched | (e) re-proves BOTH public email paths end to end under the flipped config — NGO completion, and volunteer GitHub-link-then-completion; everything else is the predecessors' record | unchanged from the predecessors' tables |\n317: \n318: **What the green does and does not claim** (repeated in the merge ruling): claims — the two\n319: tests exist, execute, open worlds and assert; the shipped decision module `verification.ts` —\n320: byte for byte the code a future route imports — behaves as the two criteria require, and fails\n321: closed; the config flip is real and the live verification round trip works on one machine.\n322: Does not claim — that any deployed route enforces the gate (none exists to), that hosted email\n323: delivery works, that any screen exists, or anything about session expiry, revocation, refresh\n324: or password reset (D2.L2's ids, declared red).\n325: \n326: ## 5. Seen, deliberately not touched\n327: \n328: - **The a-file's seven green tests.** No criterion makes completion require verification, and\n329:   the fixture does not model session issuance, so no existing body changes. The live-stack\n330:   consequence of the flip (no session until confirmed) is proved as (e), not simulated at loop.\n331: - **Session semantics** — expiry, revocation, refresh, reset: D2.L2's four ids, still\n332:   `notLanded(LEAF.D2_L2)`.\n333: - **The wiring leaf's screens**, including the verify CTA the PRD describes — D2.LW, and\n334:   `src/` is the other ownership territory besides.\n335: - **Retired AT-001.11 semantics** — nothing modelled, nothing asserted (D-E).\n336: - **The two filed items under the auth root** — the default-privilege residue on the four auth\n337:   tables, and the volunteer GitHub-unlink enforcement — other items' work.\n338: - The acceptance file and the manifest — read-only here; changes go through `/doc-sync`.\n339: - `AGENTS.md` staleness — pre-existing, filed two items ago, still not this item's.\n340: \n341: ## 6. Risks that could force a mid-flight change\n342: \n343: 1. **GoTrue's confirmations-on behaviour differs from the expectation** — a session at signup,\n344:    sign-in not refused, or a different `/auth/v1/user` serialisation. Step 5 measures all\n345:    three; if any differs, the plan is amended in the open, never silently thinned. The gate\n346:    module is unaffected either way (it judges a shape, and its fail-closed answer to a missing\n347:    field is the point).\n348: 2. **The mail catcher's API shape varies across CLI versions.** The executor measures the\n349:    endpoint before asserting (b). Fallback if the catcher cannot yield the link: GoTrue's admin\n350:    generate-link endpoint mints an equivalent verification link with operator authority — and\n351:    the transcript then says the EMAILED link was not what was followed, narrowing claim (b)\n352:    honestly instead of dressing it up.\n353: 3. **The email rate limit starves the proof.** The D-B relief valve is pre-authorized; the\n354:    transcript records the raise.\n355: 4. **The flip breaks an existing live flow.** Step 5(e) exists precisely to catch the plausible\n356:    ones (email/password signup → completion, for both public types). Anything else it surfaces is reported with the\n357:    evidence, classified, and ruled — not patched silently.\n358: 5. **The two bodies cannot be written without inventing Discovery semantics.** The send\n359:    operation carries an opaque string and nothing else; if anything richer turns out to be\n360:    needed, stop and report — richer semantics belong to REQ-002/004.\n361: \n362: ## 7. Gate 1 rulings (sitting 2, DRAFT — fable, claude-fable-5, effort xhigh)\n363: \n364: The gate had one reader. Its raw output is\n365: `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/gate1-sol-output.md`; the distillate beside it matches the raw\n366: count (4 = 4). Every claim below is quoted verbatim from the raw output.\n367: \n368: **[1] severity high — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.**\n369: Claim: \"The plan treats Google/GitHub users as email-confirmed without a ratified product\n370: ruling and incorrectly says step 5 verifies their GoTrue serialization, although that step\n371: exercises only email/password users.\"\n372: The second half is right and the plan text was false as written: step 5 has no OAuth flow, so\n373: it binds nothing about provider users. That sentence is removed (D-E amendment). The proposed\n374: remedy — raw `/auth/v1/user` responses from real Google and GitHub sessions — is not\n375: obtainable: no OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, the same recorded gap that\n376: leaves the OAuth handshake itself unproved at every tier (the fixture header has carried that\n377: statement since the predecessor item; this narrowing rides on that recorded gap rather than\n378: filing a duplicate). The first half needs no new product ruling: decision D-A already defines\n379: \"verified\" as GoTrue's own fact, so the fixture's provider-confirmed start is a prediction of\n380: the vendor, not a product choice this plan makes. No test this item reads a provider user's\n381: verified state, and if the prediction is wrong the shipped gate fails closed — a real\n382: unconfirmed provider user is refused, never allowed. The fix as built: the mirror is declared\n383: UNBOUND in D-E, in the fixture header's mirror section, and in section 4's not-proved column.\n384: \n385: **[2] severity medium — ACCEPT.**\n386: Claim: \"Step 5 claims both email-capable account types flow, but only the NGO address\n387: completes signup; the second address repeats type-blind Auth operations and never becomes a\n388: volunteer account.\"\n389: Correct, and the reviewer's reasoning is adopted whole: the config flip changes the auth\n390: context beneath both completion paths, so the predecessor's volunteer evidence — produced\n391: with confirmations off — predates the flip and no longer covers it. Step 5(e) is rewritten:\n392: the second address confirms, signs in, gains a GitHub identity through the predecessor's\n393: operator-authority insert into `auth.identities`, completes as a volunteer through the\n394: deployed function, and the account row and imported profile are read back.\n395: \n396: **[3] severity medium — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.**\n397: Claim: \"Step 2's done-criterion that every fixture rule comes from a shipped module is\n398: impossible because the planned fixture itself decides link issuance, link validity,\n399: confirmation mutation, and provider auto-confirmation.\"\n400: Correct that the criterion was unsatisfiable read literally — the fixture necessarily mirrors\n401: vendor behaviour, and its own header already keeps a named mirror category. The remedy is not\n402: to drop the discipline but to state the real one: step 2's criterion is rewritten so that the\n403: two PRODUCT judgements come only from the shipped `verification.ts`, and every vendor mirror\n404: this leaf adds is named in the header's mirror section with what binds it. Three of the four\n405: named behaviours are bound by step 5 (a)–(d) on the live stack; provider auto-confirmation is\n406: declared UNBOUND per ruling [1] — bound \"to live evidence\" is met where live evidence is\n407: obtainable and honestly declined where it is not.\n408: \n409: **[4] severity medium — ACCEPT.**\n410: Claim: \"The promised fail-closed handling of missing, non-string, and malformed\n411: `email_confirmed_at` values has no planned oracle because the tests exercise only `null` and a\n412: valid timestamp string.\"\n413: Correct — a promise without an oracle is exactly what the audit would flag as an untrue stated\n414: fact, and weakening the promise would invert the gate's point. The fix: a direct shape test,\n415: `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts`, added to step 1's done-criterion,\n416: covering the malformed shapes for `emailVerifiedFromUser` and the malformed-caller refusals\n417: for `discoveryMessageAllowed`. Placement verified before ruling: the vitest include\n418: `harness/**/*.selftest.ts` (`tests/at/vitest.config.ts` line 16) and the `at:selftest` filter\n419: pick the file up with zero script or CI change.\n420: \n421: No ruling removes work, so no removal-verification conditions exist. Nothing contradicts\n422: ratified text and nothing grows scope; there is no founder question.\n423: \n424: ## 8. Gate 2 rulings (sitting 3, FIX AND GOAL — fable, claude-fable-5, effort xhigh)\n425: \n426: The gate was a panel of two, each blind to the other. Seat A: nine findings, raw output\n427: `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/gate2-terra-output.md`, distillate beside it (9 = 9). Seat B:\n428: three findings, raw output `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/gate2-flash-output.md`, distillate\n429: beside it (3 = 3). Seat B also names seven verified-sound areas in its raw output — the\n430: fail-closed oracle coverage, the fixture discipline, AT-001.10's discriminating pair,\n431: AT-001.09's ordering, the bookkeeping counts, the refactor's neutrality, and the selftest-lane\n432: placement — recorded here as evidence, not as findings. Every claim below is quoted verbatim\n433: from the seat's raw output. TWO CONVERGENT PAIRS — [A2]+[B1] and [A6]+[B2] — two blind seats\n434: independently finding the same defect, the strongest signal a panel gives; each pair is one\n435: defect with one fix. **Twelve findings, twelve accepted (three of them fixed differently), zero\n436: rejected — ten distinct defects.**\n437: \n438: **[A2] + [B1] — CONVERGENT. ACCEPT.**\n439: Seat A (medium): \"The fixture calls the never-issued-link mirror live-bound even though the\n440: proof never follows or observes an unissued link.\"\n441: Seat B (low): \"Mirror 2 — 'a link that was never issued confirms nothing' — is labeled\n442: **BOUND** by the live proof, but none of proof checks (a)–(d) ever attempts a never-issued\n443: link, so nothing in the named evidence measures the negative.\"\n444: Both are right: mirror 2's BOUND label cited evidence that measured only the positive half, and\n445: the step-2 criterion's \"(a)–(d)\" wording carried the same overstatement into this plan. Seat\n446: A's proposed instrument is adopted — a mutated issued link is a token GoTrue never issued, and\n447: following it asserts nothing about expiry, single use or resend (retired AT-001.11 stays\n448: untouched). The fix: new proof check (b2) follows a tampered variant of the emailed link BEFORE\n449: the real one and requires `email_confirmed_at` still NULL; mirror 2's header entry cites (b2);\n450: step 2's criterion is amended (see section 3). The record was false as written and is corrected\n451: in the open, not thinned. Interpretation ruled after the build, upheld: (b2) skips only when NO\n452: link exists at all; when only the operator-minted fallback yields one, it runs against a\n453: tampered variant of that link with a note naming the source, because a mutated operator-minted\n454: token is equally a token GoTrue never issued. On the real run the link was emailed, so the\n455: question was moot.\n456: \n457: **[A1] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (medium): \"The malformed-caller selftest checks only `ok` and never\n458: verifies that those refusals name email verification as the remedy.\"\n459: Verified: `shipped-verification.selftest.ts` lines 96–114 assert only `.ok`. Today the module\n460: has ONE refusal literal covering every non-allow path, so the promise holds — but its oracle\n461: does not reach the malformed-caller paths, and a later split of the refusal paths would keep\n462: this selftest green while breaking the module header's all-refusals promise. Fix: assert\n463: `/verif/i` and `/email/i` on the reason of every malformed-caller refusal in that test.\n464: \n465: **[A3] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (high): \"Check (c) treats any HTTP status of 400 or greater as the\n466: expected pre-confirmation refusal and does not assert a confirmation-specific response.\"\n467: Verified: `ngoTrip.signInBefore.status >= 400` — a 429 or a 500 would record as PASS while\n468: confirmation enforced nothing. Fix: (c) passes only on a 4xx (never 5xx, never 429) whose body\n469: names the unconfirmed state — expected GoTrue error code `email_not_confirmed`, matched against\n470: the IN-MEMORY body because the transcript redactor's `code$` pattern blanks `error_code`; the\n471: note surfaces the discriminator through a safe extraction (an enum value, not a credential).\n472: If the live text differs, the check FAILS, the verbatim capture shows the real discriminator,\n473: and the executor pins that exact discriminator and re-runs — measurement, not assumption. The\n474: same tightening applies to the volunteer's pre-confirmation sign-in inside (e).\n475: \n476: **[A4] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (high): \"Check (e) never requires the volunteer round trip to use an\n477: emailed verification link, despite claiming it repeats check (b).\"\n478: Verified: (e)'s conjunction never reads `volunteerTrip.linkSource`, so the admin-generated\n479: fallback could pass (e) while the claim \"repeats (a)–(d)\" silently narrowed. The NGO half\n480: cannot do this — (b) fails with a NARROWED note on the fallback — so the volunteer half gets\n481: the same teeth: (e) requires `volunteerTrip.linkSource === 'emailed'`.\n482: \n483: **[A5] — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY, with a verification condition.** Seat A (medium): \"The\n484: stale-worktree probe accepts any non-404 response as the current `complete-signup` function.\"\n485: The fact is right — the probe fails only on 404 or unreachable, and a stale mount answers 401.\n486: The proposed remedy — a verifiable revision/source marker in the mounted artifact — buys\n487: revision-binding that (e)'s evidence does not depend on: this branch changes nothing under\n488: `supabase/functions/` except the NEW `_shared/verification.ts`, which `complete-signup` does\n489: not import, so any live `complete-signup` is byte-for-byte the code this branch carries.\n490: **VERIFY FIRST, before relying on that:** the executor confirms with\n491: `git diff main...HEAD --stat -- supabase/functions/` and by reading `complete-signup`'s\n492: imports. If the condition fails, this ruling's fixed-differently route is VOID — stop and\n493: report, and the full revision-binding remedy applies. If it holds: the probe's comment states\n494: the revision-independence argument, and `stack-up.txt` records the `functions serve` launch\n495: (command and cwd) as the operator-side binding of the mount to this worktree.\n496: \n497: **[A6] + [B2] — CONVERGENT. ACCEPT, at seat A's severity (high).**\n498: Seat A: \"The redirect redactor strips only URL fragments and logs credential-bearing query\n499: parameters unchanged.\"\n500: Seat B: \"The header promises 'NO KEY IS WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AND NONE IS PRINTED BY IT', but\n501: the followed redirect's `location` is printed after only fragment-stripping\n502: (`withoutFragment`), and `redact()` returns non-object values — including non-JSON response\n503: bodies — verbatim.\"\n504: Both right, and seat B widens the surface to raw string bodies. One fix, three parts: (i) every\n505: query-parameter VALUE in a printed location header is redacted, names kept — a token-hash or\n506: `?code=` redirect can no longer land in a transcript; (ii) `redact()` scrubs strings too —\n507: JWT-shaped substrings (`eyJ…`) and `name=value` pairs whose name matches the SENSITIVE pattern\n508: are replaced; (iii) the transcript is INSPECTED for credential residue before it is committed —\n509: the run this sitting performs is that inspection.\n510: \n511: **[A7] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (medium): \"The updated fixture header falsely says `verification.ts`\n512: is imported by edge functions and that every accept/refusal below comes from shipped modules.\"\n513: Verified: no deployed function imports `verification.ts` — that is decision D-D's own point —\n514: and `sendDiscoveryMessage`'s no-completed-account refusal is fixture bookkeeping, not a shipped\n515: judgement. This diff rewrote that header paragraph, so the falsehood is this item's to fix.\n516: Fix: the PROVED paragraph names `verification.ts` as the module the FUTURE Discovery route must\n517: import — today imported only by this suite and its shape selftest — and scopes the\n518: accept/refusal sentence to the PRODUCT judgements, naming the fixture's bookkeeping\n519: precondition refusals as the exception.\n520: \n521: **[A8] — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** Seat A (low): \"The new mirror makes a provisioned\n522: platform admin unconfirmed by reusing email registration, despite the repository's real\n523: provisioning flow creating that user with `email_confirm: true`.\"\n524: Premise verified in the tree: the predecessor's proof script provisions the admin through\n525: `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with `email_confirm: true` — the only provisioning recipe this\n526: repository records — so the fixture's admin starting unconfirmed contradicts the repository's\n527: own record, and the header note presents that state as natural rather than as contradicted.\n528: The proposed remedy — a live admin-creation check in step 5 — buys a live measurement for a\n529: state NOTHING reads; disproportionate. Fixed differently: `provisionPlatformAdmin` marks its\n530: auth user confirmed at provisioning and mints no verification link (admin creation sends no\n531: email); the header's \"ONE CONSEQUENCE\" paragraph now says the provisioned admin starts\n532: CONFIRMED, mirroring the recorded recipe, that nothing reads this state, and that it is\n533: live-bound by the first item that reads an admin's verified state. The runtime half stays\n534: honestly unbound: the predecessor's transcript ran with confirmations OFF, so it does not\n535: measure the column under the flip — which is why the mirror is labeled by its recipe, never\n536: called live-bound.\n537: \n538: **[A9] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (low): \"The PR body still says the branch is in the plan phase and\n539: that code comes after plan review, although this commit contains the draft implementation under\n540: code review.\"\n541: True — `pr-body.md`'s own text promised \"This body will be brought up to date as the item\n542: moves\" and was not. Fix: the status paragraph states the current truth (draft built, dual code\n543: review ruled, fixes applied, read-only audit and CI ahead of merge); a mechanical syncs the\n544: GitHub pull-request body from the file, as handed, after the close push.\n545: \n546: **[B3] — ACCEPT.** Seat B (low): \"The rewritten header says 'The other four are section C's',\n547: but AT-001.38 (wrong-password rejection) sits in **section B** of the acceptance file\n548: (`at-req-001.md` line 23, under '## B. Email verification'); only .12/.13/.14 are section C's.\"\n549: Verified against the acceptance file — exactly as claimed. One-clause fix in the b-file\n550: header: the other four belong to the session-and-reset leaf (D2.L2), three of them section\n551: C's, the wrong-password id section B's.\n552: \n553: **Removal check:** no ruling removes work. [A5]'s fixed-differently declines a proposed\n554: ADDITION and carries its verification condition above. Nothing contradicts ratified text and\n555: nothing grows scope; there is no founder question.\n556: \n557: **Post-goal record notes (sitting 3).** [A5]'s verify-first condition HELD: the only change\n558: under `supabase/functions/` is the new `_shared/verification.ts`, and `complete-signup` does\n559: not import it — measured by the executor before any fix. Section 1's deferred rate-limit\n560: question is ANSWERED by measurement: the CLI did not push `email_sent = 2` into the auth\n561: container (the container carried `GOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000`, recorded in\n562: `stack-up.txt` beside the reading), so the file's cap did not bind the run; check (f) passed\n563: honestly and the D-B relief valve did not fire. The proof script's rate-limit comment was\n564: amended in the open so no stated fact contradicts that measurement. Executor invocations this\n565: sitting: two of the permitted three (the goal, reached on its first attempt; the one-comment\n566: truth amendment).\n\n(End of file - total 566 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# AI4DEV-59 (email verification, unverified-write gate) — PLAN\n\n**Sitting 1 of the item: PLAN. Ruled by the `orchestrator` definition on fable (claude-fable-5,\neffort xhigh).**\n\n**Sitting 2 (DRAFT, same definition and model) ruled on gate 1's four findings and amended this\nplan. The rulings are section 7. The amended text below is what gets built; the pre-amendment\ntext is commit `46b7485`.**\n\n**Sitting 3 (FIX AND GOAL, same definition and model) ruled on gate 2's twelve findings — nine\nfrom seat A, three from seat B, two convergent pairs. The rulings are section 8. That sitting\namended step 2's mirror-binding sentence and step 5's done-list; the pre-amendment text is\ncommit `ac33db1`.**\n\n**Chain, derived from the branch**\n(`nirdrang/ai4dev-59-email-verification-and-the-gate-on-unverified-writing-d2l1`):\nAI4DEV-59 (email verification, unverified-write gate) → AI4DEV-52 (verification, sessions and\nreset container) → AI4DEV-50 (auth dev-tree root) → AI4PM-19 (the authentication requirement).\nNo `attr:` label on any of them; product work with an evidence gate above it.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\plan.md","text":"# AI4DEV-59 (email verification, unverified-write gate) — PLAN\n\n**Sitting 1 of the item: PLAN. Ruled by the `orchestrator` definition on fable (claude-fable-5,\neffort xhigh).**\n\n**Sitting 2 (DRAFT, same definition and model) ruled on gate 1's four findings and amended this\nplan. The rulings are section 7. The amended text below is what gets built; the pre-amendment\ntext is commit `46b7485`.**\n\n**Sitting 3 (FIX AND GOAL, same definition and model) ruled on gate 2's twelve findings — nine\nfrom seat A, three from seat B, two convergent pairs. The rulings are section 8. That sitting\namended step 2's mirror-binding sentence and step 5's done-list; the pre-amendment text is\ncommit `ac33db1`.**\n\n**Chain, derived from the branch**\n(`nirdrang/ai4dev-59-email-verification-and-the-gate-on-unverified-writing-d2l1`):\nAI4DEV-59 (email verification, unverified-write gate) → AI4DEV-52 (verification, sessions and\nreset container) → AI4DEV-50 (auth dev-tree root) → AI4PM-19 (the authentication requirement).\nNo `attr:` label on any of them; product work with an evidence gate above it.\n\n**Manifest:** `loop/decomp/req-001.md`, deliverable D2 leaf L1, revision `0579425`:\n*\"verification flow (all email-capable account types) + unverified-write gate on Discovery\nmessages · verify: AT-001.09,10 · blocked-by: D1.L1\"*. The D1 container is Done on the board\n(its two leaves merged as pull requests #47 and #48), so the blocker is cleared.\n\n**Acceptance ids this leaf owns:** AT-001.09, AT-001.10.\n**Acceptance text (read-only for this item):** `.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`\nlines 20–21. Line 22 matters too: **AT-001.11 is retired** — *\"verification-link\nexpiry/single-use/resend semantics are not stated in REQ-001\"* — so no assertion in this item\nmay claim those semantics.\n\n**Ratified product text behind the gate:** the PRD makes email verification *\"the floor for any\nDiscovery message\"* and says vetting is *\"never the Discovery wall\"* (decision-8,\n`.taskmaster/docs/prd-new.md` line 787; also lines 155 and 159, and\n`.taskmaster/docs/prd-mvp.md` line 157).\n\n**Verify command, pinned:** `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect`. The fixture\nadapter is a stand-in capped at the loop tier (its own header, `_fixture.ts` lines 7–10;\n`adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts`), CI has no database, and the\nrequirement's integration-tier done contract remains AI4PM-19's gate, not this leaf's.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What I verified in the tree before deciding anything\n\n| claim | verdict | evidence (pointer) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Both ids are declared red, pending on this leaf | true | `tests/at/expected/req-001.json` lines 15–16; stubs at `tests/at/suites/req-001/b-verification-and-sessions.test.ts` lines 13 and 15, each `notLanded(LEAF.D2_L1)` |\n| The config flip is reserved for this leaf | true | `supabase/config.toml` line 243 `enable_confirmations = false`; the b-file header (lines 4–7) says turning it on \"is the verification leaf's own change\" |\n| A local mail catcher is enabled | true | `supabase/config.toml` lines 105–108: `[local_smtp]` enabled, web interface on port 54324 |\n| The local stack limits auth emails to 2 per hour | true (as configured) | `supabase/config.toml` line 216, `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent = 2`; whether it binds with the local test mailer is measured in step 5, not assumed |\n| No Discovery product code exists anywhere | true | grep `discovery` over `supabase/`, `src/`, `tests/` — only docs, a harness rubric for another requirement, and this suite's own stub title; the future route is mapped to a `discovery-message` edge function in `.taskmaster/docs/migration-nextjs-to-tanstack-VERIFIED.md` lines 103–105 |\n| The decision-module pattern is established: shipped judgements, fixture storage | true | `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts` header; `_fixture.ts` header and its imports (lines 45–62) |\n| The suite already models \"the hook a later leaf must call\" | true | `hasPlatformAcknowledgment`'s contract comment, `_contract.ts` lines 244–260 — enforcement it cannot build is named as the hook the later leaf calls |\n| The caller-fact-through-shipped-extractor pattern is ratified | true | `_fixture.ts` lines 208–225 (gate-2 ruling R3 of the GitHub leaf): the fixture renders the canonical GoTrue user shape and derives the fact through the shipped extractor |\n| `_pending.ts` header counts will go stale | true | `tests/at/suites/req-001/_pending.ts` lines 3 and 9 say 30 pending / seven written; after this item it is 28 / nine |\n| CI runs typecheck, harness self-tests, at:check and at:verify (loop, --expect) per suite, plus the ownership and reference guards | true | `.github/workflows/ci.yml` |\n| GoTrue with confirmations on issues no session at signup and refuses sign-in until the address is confirmed | **unverified-runtime-claim** | expected GoTrue behaviour; measured, with exact refusal text captured, in step 5 — nothing in this plan silently depends on it before then |\n\n## 2. Decisions\n\n### D-A — \"Verified\" is Supabase Auth's own fact. No migration, no new column.\nThe observable behind AT-001.09 is `email_confirmed_at` on the auth user — GoTrue's field, set\nby GoTrue when the emailed link is used. Nothing this leaf ships writes it, `public.accounts`\ngains no column, and this item ships **no migration**. Consequence: there is no schema, RLS or\nprivilege surface to prove here; the live proof reads `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` as\noperator evidence only.\n\n### D-B — The config flip, and one pre-authorized relief valve\n`[auth.email] enable_confirmations` flips to `true` in `supabase/config.toml` — the change the\nb-file header explicitly reserves for this leaf. The comment beside it is updated to say what it\nchanges and which leaf owns it. **Pre-authorized amendment:** if step 5's registrations starve\non the 2-per-hour `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent` limit (429 or absent mail), the executor raises\nthat value (e.g. to 100) in the same file, restarts the stack, and records the raise in the\ntranscript. This is local-stack configuration; no hosted deployment exists.\n\n### D-C — The gate is a shipped decision module: `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`\nA new module under the same two constraints as `accounts.ts` (relative imports only, no Deno\nglobal, no I/O; its type coverage is its import from the acceptance program). Two exports:\n\n- `emailVerifiedFromUser(user: unknown): boolean` — judges the GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` response\n  shape: a non-empty string `email_confirmed_at` means verified; **anything else — missing\n  field, null, non-string, malformed object — means unverified.** Fail closed.\n- `discoveryMessageAllowed(caller: { emailVerified: boolean }): Decision<'verified'>` —\n  `Decision` imported from `./accounts.ts`. The ONLY allow is `caller?.emailVerified === true`;\n  a missing or malformed caller argument refuses (the same fail-closed posture, for the same\n  measured reason: edge entry points have no type-checker). The refusal reason names email\n  verification as what is missing AND as the remedy, in words AT-001.10's assertion can match.\n\nThe gate is **type-blind**: decision-8 makes verification the floor for ANY Discovery message.\nAT-001.10's unverified-NGO subject is an instance of that rule, not its scope, and a gate that\nread the account type would be shipping a distinction no ratified text draws.\n\nWhy a new module rather than growing `accounts.ts`: that file's header scopes it to the account\ndeliverable's decisions; `github.ts` already set the several-small-modules precedent; and this\nrule's future caller is the Discovery send route, not signup.\n\n### D-D — No Discovery surface ships. The gate is the hook the route must call.\nThe Discovery message route belongs to REQ-002/004 (the migration doc maps it to a future\n`discovery-message` edge function). Building an edge function or a table for it here would be\nanother requirement's surface invented early. What this leaf ships is the **decision** that\nroute must consult — exactly the pattern the suite already ratifies for\n`hasPlatformAcknowledgment` (\"this is the hook the leaf that lands project creation must call\").\nThe module header and section 4 both state it plainly: **the gate has no deployed caller yet**,\nand no green from this item may be read as \"Discovery messaging is gated in production.\"\n\nAt the loop tier the SUT gains a `sendDiscoveryMessage` operation and the fixture is the\nstand-in surface: it renders the caller's GoTrue user shape from stored Auth state, derives the\nverified fact through the SHIPPED `emailVerifiedFromUser`, consults the SHIPPED\n`discoveryMessageAllowed`, refuses with the gate's own reason and **writes nothing**, or on\nallow records the message in fixture storage. Every judgement is the shipped module's; the\nstorage is a Map like all the rest.\n\n### D-E — Fixture and contract: mirror Auth's verification state, judge nothing\n`_fixture.ts`: `AuthUser` gains `emailConfirmedAt: string | null` and\n`verificationLink: string | null`. An email/password registration starts unconfirmed and mints\na link value (the fixture's stand-in for the emailed link the mail catcher would hold). A\nprovider registration (Google/GitHub) starts confirmed — the expected GoTrue behaviour when the\nprovider vouches for the address. **This mirror is UNBOUND by this item** (gate-1 ruling [1],\nsection 7): no OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, so no live provider session\nis obtainable — the same recorded gap that leaves the OAuth handshake itself unproved at every\ntier, stated in the fixture header since the predecessor. No test this item reads a provider\nuser's verified state; both new bodies register by email/password. If the prediction is wrong —\na real provider user arriving unconfirmed — the shipped gate fails closed: it refuses, never\nallows. The fixture header's mirror section states all of this; the mirror is bound by the\nfirst item that ships a real provider-path consumer.\n\n`_contract.ts`: `AccountsSut` gains five members —\n- `emailVerified(accountId): Promise<boolean>` — read-back, derived through the shipped\n  `emailVerifiedFromUser` over the rendered GoTrue user shape (the R3 pattern: the extractor\n  sits on the tested path, exactly as the future route's caller resolution would).\n- `emailedVerificationLink(email): Promise<string | null>` — the emailed verification link for\n  the address, or null when none was emailed.\n- `useVerificationLink(link): Promise<{ ok: boolean }>` — using an emailed link confirms the\n  address; a never-issued link returns `ok: false` and flips nothing. **No expiry, no\n  single-use, no resend is modelled or asserted — those are retired AT-001.11's semantics,\n  deliberately unstated in REQ-001.** The never-issued negative is not AT-001.11 ground: a\n  link-shaped string that verifies an account it never belonged to would make \"using the link\n  flips it\" meaningless, so the negative guards this test's own oracle.\n- `sendDiscoveryMessage(session, body: string): Promise<{ ok: true } | { ok: false; reason:\n  string }>` — per D-D. A caller with no completed account gets the fixture's bookkeeping\n  refusal, same shape as `createOrganization`'s.\n- `discoveryMessagesBy(accountId): Promise<string[]>` — read-back, because a block whose write\n  happened anyway is not a block.\n\n`registerWithEmailPassword` keeps returning a `Session`. The fixture's `Session` has always\nbeen an identity handle, not an access token — session ISSUANCE (and with it \"an unconfirmed\nuser holds no session\") is D2.L2's subject. The honest gap this leaves is stated in section 4\nand in the test comments, not hidden: at the loop tier a completed-but-unverified account is\nconstructible; on the live stack the public path cannot reach it (step 5 measures that).\n\n### D-F — AT-001.09's body: parameterized over both types, surface `ui`\nFor EACH of `ngo` and `volunteer` (the two email-capable public types —\n`PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES`; the platform admin is provisioned, not signed up, and is outside\nthis criterion's \"can register by email\"):\n1. register with email/password → `emailVerified` is **false** — the fresh signup is\n   email-unverified.\n2. complete signup (NGO with an organisation name; volunteer after `linkGithubIdentity`, the\n   precondition AT-001.04 owns) → the account row carries its type — which makes \"either\n   account type\" a fact of the run, not a label.\n3. still `emailVerified` false — completion does not verify.\n4. negative: `useVerificationLink('never-issued-link')` → `ok: false`, and `emailVerified`\n   still false.\n5. `emailedVerificationLink(email)` → non-null; `useVerificationLink(link)` → `ok: true`;\n   `emailVerified` → **true** — using the emailed link is what flips it.\n6. the account row still carries the same type — verification re-types nothing.\n\nSurface mark `ui`: the verification screen is one of the four auth screens the manifest's\nwiring leaf names (*\"signup, sign-in, verification, reset\"*), so D2.LW must find this id to\nre-run `--wired`.\n\nOrdering note, stated so the review can attack it rather than discover it: the test completes\nsignup BEFORE verifying, so that the typed account is observably unverified — the criterion's\n\"the account … is email-unverified until the link is used\" read with \"account\" meaning the\ntyped account. On the live stack with confirmations on the public order is verify-then-complete\n(no session exists before confirmation); step 5 drives THAT order against the real stack. The\ntwo orders together cover both readings; neither contradicts any criterion.\n\n### D-G — AT-001.10's body: the discriminating pair, surface `backend`\n1. register an NGO by email/password and complete signup **without verifying** (reachable at\n   the loop tier per D-E; the body's comment states plainly that the live public path cannot\n   reach this state and why the gate exists anyway — decision-8 makes the floor the write\n   path's own rule, not a property borrowed from the session layer, and non-public paths and\n   configuration history can produce exactly this state).\n2. `sendDiscoveryMessage` → refused; the reason matches `/verif/i` and `/email/i` —\n   verification named as the remedy; `discoveryMessagesBy` is empty — the blocked attempt\n   wrote nothing.\n3. the discriminating control: use the emailed verification link → `emailVerified` true → the\n   SAME send now succeeds and `discoveryMessagesBy` records it. The remedy is proved by being\n   the exact change that unblocks, and a refuse-everybody gate fails this half.\n\nSurface mark `backend`: the Discovery composer is REQ-002/004's surface; it is not one of the\nfour auth screens, and the wiring leaf has nothing of it to re-run.\n\n### D-H — Suite bookkeeping, all in the same change\n- `tests/at/expected/req-001.json`: AT-001.09 and AT-001.10 move from `red` to `green` — 9\n  green / 28 declared red.\n- `_pending.ts`: `D2_L1` is removed from `LEAF` (the orphan rule — its last two users become\n  real bodies), and the header's counts and \"seven are written\" sentence are corrected —\n  after this item they are false statements of fact, the audit's first box.\n- The b-file header is rewritten: its `enable_confirmations` sentence becomes false the moment\n  this leaf lands. `LEAF`/`notLanded` imports stay — four D2.L2 stubs still use them.\n- `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/pending-ledger.txt`: 28 lines, one per remaining pending id, each\n  naming a deliverable-and-leaf that appears in `loop/decomp/req-001.md`; this leaf's ids\n  absent.\n\n### D-I — One review slice\nEstimated diff outside `loop/items/`: ~70 lines shared module, ~10 config, ~60 contract, ~90\nfixture, ~140 suite bodies and headers, ~50 shape selftest (gate-1 ruling [4]), ~15 declaration\nand pending bookkeeping — roughly 450.\nFar under the 1200-line trigger the previous two leaves used. **One slice**; both pinned\ndraft-code readers read the whole diff.\n\n## 3. Steps, each with its own done-criterion\n\nItem evidence lives in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/`. Every path repository-relative. The executor\ncommits one commit per step (the parking rule).\n\n**Step 0 — baseline.** `bun run typecheck`, `bun run at:selftest`, `bun run at:check req-001`,\n`bun run at:check req-016`, `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect`,\n`bun run at:verify req-016 --tier loop --expect`.\n→ done: all exit 0, transcript in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/baseline.txt` (req-001 showing 7 green /\n30 declared red). A red baseline is reported, not worked around.\n\n**Step 1 — the shipped gate.** `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts` per D-C, header\nnaming the hook contract (D-D) and the fail-closed posture.\n→ done: `bun run typecheck` exits 0 with the module imported by the acceptance program (via the\nfixture); the refusal reason names email verification as the remedy; and a direct shape test —\n`tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` (gate-1 ruling [4], section 7) — proves the\nfail-closed promise: `emailVerifiedFromUser` returns false for a missing field, `null`, an\nempty string, a number, an object value, and a non-object user, and true only for a non-empty\nstring; `discoveryMessageAllowed` refuses a missing and a malformed caller in the same file.\nThe vitest include `harness/**/*.selftest.ts` and the `at:selftest` filter pick it up with zero\nscript or CI change — verified against `tests/at/vitest.config.ts` line 16 and the\n`package.json` script. Its header states why a shipped module's shape test rides the selftest\nlane: the promise cannot arise through the fixture, and this lane is what CI already runs.\n\n**Step 2 — contract and fixture.** The D-E extensions, judgements delegated per D-C/D-D.\n→ done (rewritten by gate-1 ruling [3], section 7 — the earlier criterion was unsatisfiable as\nwritten): `bun run typecheck` exits 0; both PRODUCT judgements — the verified-fact extraction\nand the Discovery gate — come only from the shipped `verification.ts`, with no second copy of\neither rule in the fixture; and every VENDOR MIRROR this leaf adds is named in the fixture\nheader's mirror section with what binds it: link issuance at email signup is bound by step 5\n(a)–(b); a never-issued link returning `ok: false` is bound by step 5 (b2) — the tampered-link\nnegative added by gate-2 rulings [A2]/[B1], section 8, because the earlier \"(a)–(d)\" wording\nclaimed a binding no check measured; a used link setting `emailConfirmedAt` is bound by step 5\n(b) and (d); a provider registration starting confirmed is declared UNBOUND, per the D-E\namendment; and the provisioned platform admin starting CONFIRMED mirrors the recorded\nprovisioning recipe (gate-2 ruling [A8], section 8), read by nothing and live-bound by the\nfirst item that reads an admin's verified state.\n\n**Step 3 — the two real test bodies, and the bookkeeping.** D-F, D-G, D-H.\n→ done: `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect` exits 0 with exactly 9 passed and 28\ndeclared red; `bun run at:check req-001` clean; no comment in any touched file still states\nthat .09/.10 are pending, that the config flip is reserved, or a stale count.\n\n**Step 4 — the config flip, and the stack with it.** D-B in `supabase/config.toml`; restart the\nlocal stack so auth picks it up.\n→ done: `bun run db:reset` (or stop/start) completes; `bunx supabase status` shows the stack up;\ntranscript in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/stack-up.txt`. Two failed attempts → stop and report.\n\n**Step 5 — the live proof.** `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` against the live stack,\ntranscript in `proof-local.txt`. Evidence-gathering, run once, never a guarding test. Secrets\nredacted exactly as the predecessor transcripts redact them.\n→ done, all of:\n  (a) an email/password signup under the flipped config returns **no session**, and the\n  operator read on the database (port 54322) shows `auth.users.email_confirmed_at IS NULL` —\n  the unverified-runtime-claims from section 1 measured, with exact wire responses captured;\n  (b) the mail catcher on port 54324 holds the confirmation email for that address, and the\n  verification link is extracted from it — the catcher's API shape MEASURED first, not assumed;\n  (b2) a tampered variant of that link — its token altered, so a token GoTrue never issued —\n  is followed BEFORE the real one, and `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` stays NULL: the\n  never-issued-link mirror measured rather than inferred (gate-2 rulings [A2]/[B1], section 8);\n  no expiry, single-use or resend semantics are touched — the token followed was never issued.\n  If only the operator-minted fallback link exists, (b2) runs against a tampered variant of THAT\n  link with a note naming the source — a mutated operator-minted token is equally never-issued —\n  and it skips only when no link exists at all (sitting-3 ruling on the executor's report);\n  (c) sign-in BEFORE confirmation is refused with a CONFIRMATION-SPECIFIC refusal — a 4xx whose\n  body names the unconfirmed state (expected GoTrue error code `email_not_confirmed`), captured\n  verbatim; a 429 or a 5xx is NOT this refusal and fails the check (gate-2 ruling [A3], section\n  8). The discriminator is read from the in-memory body, because the transcript redactor's\n  name-pattern also blanks `error_code`, and the note surfaces it through a safe extraction;\n  (d) using the link (HTTP GET) flips `email_confirmed_at` to non-null; sign-in now succeeds;\n  and the raw `/auth/v1/user` response of the signed-in user, fed to the SHIPPED\n  `emailVerifiedFromUser`, returns true — the extractor bound to GoTrue's real serialisation\n  (its false half is exercised at the loop tier; an unconfirmed user has no session to read\n  `/auth/v1/user` with, and the transcript says so rather than dressing it up);\n  (e) both email-capable types flow END TO END under the flipped config (gate-1 ruling [2],\n  section 7 — the flip changes the auth context beneath BOTH completion paths, so the\n  predecessor's completion evidence predates it and is re-proved, not cited): a second address\n  repeats (a)–(d); the NGO address completes signup through the deployed `complete-signup`\n  edge function AFTER confirmation; the volunteer address, after confirmation and sign-in,\n  gains a GitHub identity by the predecessor's mechanism — the operator-authority insert into\n  `auth.identities` (`fabricateGithubIdentity` in the predecessor's proof script, flip-\n  independent because it is a database write, not an auth flow) — then completes as a\n  volunteer through the same deployed function, and the account row and imported profile are\n  read back. The volunteer's \"repeats (a)–(d)\" claim has teeth (gate-2 rulings [A3]/[A4],\n  section 8): its link MUST come from the catcher (`linkSource === 'emailed'`) and its\n  pre-confirmation sign-in refusal MUST be the same confirmation-specific one as (c);\n  (f) if the mailer rate limit starves (b), the D-B relief valve fires and the transcript\n  records it.\n\n**Step 6 — the whole verify surface, green together.** The step-0 list re-run.\n→ done: all exit 0 in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/verify-final.txt`; req-016 unchanged from baseline.\n\n## 4. Expected verification state, per acceptance-test id\n\nAt `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect` when this item is done: **AT-001.01–.07,\n.09, .10 green (9), the other 28 P0 ids red as `{\"kind\":\"pending\",\"phase\":\"sut-missing\"}`**,\nexit 0.\n\n| id | proved at loop tier (CI) | proved on the live stack (step 5, one machine) | **not proved by this item** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AT-001.09 | the flow shape against the fixture's Auth mirror: both public types start email-unverified, a never-issued link flips nothing, the emailed link flips the account to verified, the type survives; the shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser` judges the rendered user shape on every read | the real substance: GoTrue issues no session at signup, the confirmation email exists and its link flips `email_confirmed_at`, sign-in is refused before and succeeds after, the shipped extractor answers true against GoTrue's real serialisation | email delivery anywhere but the local mail catcher; any screen (D2.LW's, and Lovable's territory); link expiry / single-use / resend semantics — retired AT-001.11, deliberately unasserted; GoTrue's serialisation of provider-vouched confirmation — the fixture's provider-confirmed start is an unbound vendor mirror (D-E amendment) |\n| AT-001.10 | the SHIPPED gate refuses an unverified account's Discovery send with verification named as the remedy and writes nothing, and the SAME send succeeds once verified — the discriminating pair | the auth-layer fact: an unconfirmed email/password user cannot authenticate at all, refusal text captured — on the live stack the block sits upstream of the gate | **enforcement at a deployed Discovery route — none exists.** The route is REQ-002/004's; the gate is the hook it must call, stated in the module header. No green here may be read as \"Discovery messaging is gated in production\" |\n| AT-001.01–.07 | unchanged claims, bodies untouched | (e) re-proves BOTH public email paths end to end under the flipped config — NGO completion, and volunteer GitHub-link-then-completion; everything else is the predecessors' record | unchanged from the predecessors' tables |\n\n**What the green does and does not claim** (repeated in the merge ruling): claims — the two\ntests exist, execute, open worlds and assert; the shipped decision module `verification.ts` —\nbyte for byte the code a future route imports — behaves as the two criteria require, and fails\nclosed; the config flip is real and the live verification round trip works on one machine.\nDoes not claim — that any deployed route enforces the gate (none exists to), that hosted email\ndelivery works, that any screen exists, or anything about session expiry, revocation, refresh\nor password reset (D2.L2's ids, declared red).\n\n## 5. Seen, deliberately not touched\n\n- **The a-file's seven green tests.** No criterion makes completion require verification, and\n  the fixture does not model session issuance, so no existing body changes. The live-stack\n  consequence of the flip (no session until confirmed) is proved as (e), not simulated at loop.\n- **Session semantics** — expiry, revocation, refresh, reset: D2.L2's four ids, still\n  `notLanded(LEAF.D2_L2)`.\n- **The wiring leaf's screens**, including the verify CTA the PRD describes — D2.LW, and\n  `src/` is the other ownership territory besides.\n- **Retired AT-001.11 semantics** — nothing modelled, nothing asserted (D-E).\n- **The two filed items under the auth root** — the default-privilege residue on the four auth\n  tables, and the volunteer GitHub-unlink enforcement — other items' work.\n- The acceptance file and the manifest — read-only here; changes go through `/doc-sync`.\n- `AGENTS.md` staleness — pre-existing, filed two items ago, still not this item's.\n\n## 6. Risks that could force a mid-flight change\n\n1. **GoTrue's confirmations-on behaviour differs from the expectation** — a session at signup,\n   sign-in not refused, or a different `/auth/v1/user` serialisation. Step 5 measures all\n   three; if any differs, the plan is amended in the open, never silently thinned. The gate\n   module is unaffected either way (it judges a shape, and its fail-closed answer to a missing\n   field is the point).\n2. **The mail catcher's API shape varies across CLI versions.** The executor measures the\n   endpoint before asserting (b). Fallback if the catcher cannot yield the link: GoTrue's admin\n   generate-link endpoint mints an equivalent verification link with operator authority — and\n   the transcript then says the EMAILED link was not what was followed, narrowing claim (b)\n   honestly instead of dressing it up.\n3. **The email rate limit starves the proof.** The D-B relief valve is pre-authorized; the\n   transcript records the raise.\n4. **The flip breaks an existing live flow.** Step 5(e) exists precisely to catch the plausible\n   ones (email/password signup → completion, for both public types). Anything else it surfaces is reported with the\n   evidence, classified, and ruled — not patched silently.\n5. **The two bodies cannot be written without inventing Discovery semantics.** The send\n   operation carries an opaque string and nothing else; if anything richer turns out to be\n   needed, stop and report — richer semantics belong to REQ-002/004.\n\n## 7. Gate 1 rulings (sitting 2, DRAFT — fable, claude-fable-5, effort xhigh)\n\nThe gate had one reader. Its raw output is\n`loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/gate1-sol-output.md`; the distillate beside it matches the raw\ncount (4 = 4). Every claim below is quoted verbatim from the raw output.\n\n**[1] severity high — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.**\nClaim: \"The plan treats Google/GitHub users as email-confirmed without a ratified product\nruling and incorrectly says step 5 verifies their GoTrue serialization, although that step\nexercises only email/password users.\"\nThe second half is right and the plan text was false as written: step 5 has no OAuth flow, so\nit binds nothing about provider users. That sentence is removed (D-E amendment). The proposed\nremedy — raw `/auth/v1/user` responses from real Google and GitHub sessions — is not\nobtainable: no OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, the same recorded gap that\nleaves the OAuth handshake itself unproved at every tier (the fixture header has carried that\nstatement since the predecessor item; this narrowing rides on that recorded gap rather than\nfiling a duplicate). The first half needs no new product ruling: decision D-A already defines\n\"verified\" as GoTrue's own fact, so the fixture's provider-confirmed start is a prediction of\nthe vendor, not a product choice this plan makes. No test this item reads a provider user's\nverified state, and if the prediction is wrong the shipped gate fails closed — a real\nunconfirmed provider user is refused, never allowed. The fix as built: the mirror is declared\nUNBOUND in D-E, in the fixture header's mirror section, and in section 4's not-proved column.\n\n**[2] severity medium — ACCEPT.**\nClaim: \"Step 5 claims both email-capable account types flow, but only the NGO address\ncompletes signup; the second address repeats type-blind Auth operations and never becomes a\nvolunteer account.\"\nCorrect, and the reviewer's reasoning is adopted whole: the config flip changes the auth\ncontext beneath both completion paths, so the predecessor's volunteer evidence — produced\nwith confirmations off — predates the flip and no longer covers it. Step 5(e) is rewritten:\nthe second address confirms, signs in, gains a GitHub identity through the predecessor's\noperator-authority insert into `auth.identities`, completes as a volunteer through the\ndeployed function, and the account row and imported profile are read back.\n\n**[3] severity medium — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.**\nClaim: \"Step 2's done-criterion that every fixture rule comes from a shipped module is\nimpossible because the planned fixture itself decides link issuance, link validity,\nconfirmation mutation, and provider auto-confirmation.\"\nCorrect that the criterion was unsatisfiable read literally — the fixture necessarily mirrors\nvendor behaviour, and its own header already keeps a named mirror category. The remedy is not\nto drop the discipline but to state the real one: step 2's criterion is rewritten so that the\ntwo PRODUCT judgements come only from the shipped `verification.ts`, and every vendor mirror\nthis leaf adds is named in the header's mirror section with what binds it. Three of the four\nnamed behaviours are bound by step 5 (a)–(d) on the live stack; provider auto-confirmation is\ndeclared UNBOUND per ruling [1] — bound \"to live evidence\" is met where live evidence is\nobtainable and honestly declined where it is not.\n\n**[4] severity medium — ACCEPT.**\nClaim: \"The promised fail-closed handling of missing, non-string, and malformed\n`email_confirmed_at` values has no planned oracle because the tests exercise only `null` and a\nvalid timestamp string.\"\nCorrect — a promise without an oracle is exactly what the audit would flag as an untrue stated\nfact, and weakening the promise would invert the gate's point. The fix: a direct shape test,\n`tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts`, added to step 1's done-criterion,\ncovering the malformed shapes for `emailVerifiedFromUser` and the malformed-caller refusals\nfor `discoveryMessageAllowed`. Placement verified before ruling: the vitest include\n`harness/**/*.selftest.ts` (`tests/at/vitest.config.ts` line 16) and the `at:selftest` filter\npick the file up with zero script or CI change.\n\nNo ruling removes work, so no removal-verification conditions exist. Nothing contradicts\nratified text and nothing grows scope; there is no founder question.\n\n## 8. Gate 2 rulings (sitting 3, FIX AND GOAL — fable, claude-fable-5, effort xhigh)\n\nThe gate was a panel of two, each blind to the other. Seat A: nine findings, raw output\n`loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/gate2-terra-output.md`, distillate beside it (9 = 9). Seat B:\nthree findings, raw output `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/gate2-flash-output.md`, distillate\nbeside it (3 = 3). Seat B also names seven verified-sound areas in its raw output — the\nfail-closed oracle coverage, the fixture discipline, AT-001.10's discriminating pair,\nAT-001.09's ordering, the bookkeeping counts, the refactor's neutrality, and the selftest-lane\nplacement — recorded here as evidence, not as findings. Every claim below is quoted verbatim\nfrom the seat's raw output. TWO CONVERGENT PAIRS — [A2]+[B1] and [A6]+[B2] — two blind seats\nindependently finding the same defect, the strongest signal a panel gives; each pair is one\ndefect with one fix. **Twelve findings, twelve accepted (three of them fixed differently), zero\nrejected — ten distinct defects.**\n\n**[A2] + [B1] — CONVERGENT. ACCEPT.**\nSeat A (medium): \"The fixture calls the never-issued-link mirror live-bound even though the\nproof never follows or observes an unissued link.\"\nSeat B (low): \"Mirror 2 — 'a link that was never issued confirms nothing' — is labeled\n**BOUND** by the live proof, but none of proof checks (a)–(d) ever attempts a never-issued\nlink, so nothing in the named evidence measures the negative.\"\nBoth are right: mirror 2's BOUND label cited evidence that measured only the positive half, and\nthe step-2 criterion's \"(a)–(d)\" wording carried the same overstatement into this plan. Seat\nA's proposed instrument is adopted — a mutated issued link is a token GoTrue never issued, and\nfollowing it asserts nothing about expiry, single use or resend (retired AT-001.11 stays\nuntouched). The fix: new proof check (b2) follows a tampered variant of the emailed link BEFORE\nthe real one and requires `email_confirmed_at` still NULL; mirror 2's header entry cites (b2);\nstep 2's criterion is amended (see section 3). The record was false as written and is corrected\nin the open, not thinned. Interpretation ruled after the build, upheld: (b2) skips only when NO\nlink exists at all; when only the operator-minted fallback yields one, it runs against a\ntampered variant of that link with a note naming the source, because a mutated operator-minted\ntoken is equally a token GoTrue never issued. On the real run the link was emailed, so the\nquestion was moot.\n\n**[A1] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (medium): \"The malformed-caller selftest checks only `ok` and never\nverifies that those refusals name email verification as the remedy.\"\nVerified: `shipped-verification.selftest.ts` lines 96–114 assert only `.ok`. Today the module\nhas ONE refusal literal covering every non-allow path, so the promise holds — but its oracle\ndoes not reach the malformed-caller paths, and a later split of the refusal paths would keep\nthis selftest green while breaking the module header's all-refusals promise. Fix: assert\n`/verif/i` and `/email/i` on the reason of every malformed-caller refusal in that test.\n\n**[A3] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (high): \"Check (c) treats any HTTP status of 400 or greater as the\nexpected pre-confirmation refusal and does not assert a confirmation-specific response.\"\nVerified: `ngoTrip.signInBefore.status >= 400` — a 429 or a 500 would record as PASS while\nconfirmation enforced nothing. Fix: (c) passes only on a 4xx (never 5xx, never 429) whose body\nnames the unconfirmed state — expected GoTrue error code `email_not_confirmed`, matched against\nthe IN-MEMORY body because the transcript redactor's `code$` pattern blanks `error_code`; the\nnote surfaces the discriminator through a safe extraction (an enum value, not a credential).\nIf the live text differs, the check FAILS, the verbatim capture shows the real discriminator,\nand the executor pins that exact discriminator and re-runs — measurement, not assumption. The\nsame tightening applies to the volunteer's pre-confirmation sign-in inside (e).\n\n**[A4] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (high): \"Check (e) never requires the volunteer round trip to use an\nemailed verification link, despite claiming it repeats check (b).\"\nVerified: (e)'s conjunction never reads `volunteerTrip.linkSource`, so the admin-generated\nfallback could pass (e) while the claim \"repeats (a)–(d)\" silently narrowed. The NGO half\ncannot do this — (b) fails with a NARROWED note on the fallback — so the volunteer half gets\nthe same teeth: (e) requires `volunteerTrip.linkSource === 'emailed'`.\n\n**[A5] — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY, with a verification condition.** Seat A (medium): \"The\nstale-worktree probe accepts any non-404 response as the current `complete-signup` function.\"\nThe fact is right — the probe fails only on 404 or unreachable, and a stale mount answers 401.\nThe proposed remedy — a verifiable revision/source marker in the mounted artifact — buys\nrevision-binding that (e)'s evidence does not depend on: this branch changes nothing under\n`supabase/functions/` except the NEW `_shared/verification.ts`, which `complete-signup` does\nnot import, so any live `complete-signup` is byte-for-byte the code this branch carries.\n**VERIFY FIRST, before relying on that:** the executor confirms with\n`git diff main...HEAD --stat -- supabase/functions/` and by reading `complete-signup`'s\nimports. If the condition fails, this ruling's fixed-differently route is VOID — stop and\nreport, and the full revision-binding remedy applies. If it holds: the probe's comment states\nthe revision-independence argument, and `stack-up.txt` records the `functions serve` launch\n(command and cwd) as the operator-side binding of the mount to this worktree.\n\n**[A6] + [B2] — CONVERGENT. ACCEPT, at seat A's severity (high).**\nSeat A: \"The redirect redactor strips only URL fragments and logs credential-bearing query\nparameters unchanged.\"\nSeat B: \"The header promises 'NO KEY IS WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AND NONE IS PRINTED BY IT', but\nthe followed redirect's `location` is printed after only fragment-stripping\n(`withoutFragment`), and `redact()` returns non-object values — including non-JSON response\nbodies — verbatim.\"\nBoth right, and seat B widens the surface to raw string bodies. One fix, three parts: (i) every\nquery-parameter VALUE in a printed location header is redacted, names kept — a token-hash or\n`?code=` redirect can no longer land in a transcript; (ii) `redact()` scrubs strings too —\nJWT-shaped substrings (`eyJ…`) and `name=value` pairs whose name matches the SENSITIVE pattern\nare replaced; (iii) the transcript is INSPECTED for credential residue before it is committed —\nthe run this sitting performs is that inspection.\n\n**[A7] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (medium): \"The updated fixture header falsely says `verification.ts`\nis imported by edge functions and that every accept/refusal below comes from shipped modules.\"\nVerified: no deployed function imports `verification.ts` — that is decision D-D's own point —\nand `sendDiscoveryMessage`'s no-completed-account refusal is fixture bookkeeping, not a shipped\njudgement. This diff rewrote that header paragraph, so the falsehood is this item's to fix.\nFix: the PROVED paragraph names `verification.ts` as the module the FUTURE Discovery route must\nimport — today imported only by this suite and its shape selftest — and scopes the\naccept/refusal sentence to the PRODUCT judgements, naming the fixture's bookkeeping\nprecondition refusals as the exception.\n\n**[A8] — ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** Seat A (low): \"The new mirror makes a provisioned\nplatform admin unconfirmed by reusing email registration, despite the repository's real\nprovisioning flow creating that user with `email_confirm: true`.\"\nPremise verified in the tree: the predecessor's proof script provisions the admin through\n`POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with `email_confirm: true` — the only provisioning recipe this\nrepository records — so the fixture's admin starting unconfirmed contradicts the repository's\nown record, and the header note presents that state as natural rather than as contradicted.\nThe proposed remedy — a live admin-creation check in step 5 — buys a live measurement for a\nstate NOTHING reads; disproportionate. Fixed differently: `provisionPlatformAdmin` marks its\nauth user confirmed at provisioning and mints no verification link (admin creation sends no\nemail); the header's \"ONE CONSEQUENCE\" paragraph now says the provisioned admin starts\nCONFIRMED, mirroring the recorded recipe, that nothing reads this state, and that it is\nlive-bound by the first item that reads an admin's verified state. The runtime half stays\nhonestly unbound: the predecessor's transcript ran with confirmations OFF, so it does not\nmeasure the column under the flip — which is why the mirror is labeled by its recipe, never\ncalled live-bound.\n\n**[A9] — ACCEPT.** Seat A (low): \"The PR body still says the branch is in the plan phase and\nthat code comes after plan review, although this commit contains the draft implementation under\ncode review.\"\nTrue — `pr-body.md`'s own text promised \"This body will be brought up to date as the item\nmoves\" and was not. Fix: the status paragraph states the current truth (draft built, dual code\nreview ruled, fixes applied, read-only audit and CI ahead of merge); a mechanical syncs the\nGitHub pull-request body from the file, as handed, after the close push.\n\n**[B3] — ACCEPT.** Seat B (low): \"The rewritten header says 'The other four are section C's',\nbut AT-001.38 (wrong-password rejection) sits in **section B** of the acceptance file\n(`at-req-001.md` line 23, under '## B. Email verification'); only .12/.13/.14 are section C's.\"\nVerified against the acceptance file — exactly as claimed. One-clause fix in the b-file\nheader: the other four belong to the session-and-reset leaf (D2.L2), three of them section\nC's, the wrong-password id section B's.\n\n**Removal check:** no ruling removes work. [A5]'s fixed-differently declines a proposed\nADDITION and carries its verification condition above. Nothing contradicts ratified text and\nnothing grows scope; there is no founder question.\n\n**Post-goal record notes (sitting 3).** [A5]'s verify-first condition HELD: the only change\nunder `supabase/functions/` is the new `_shared/verification.ts`, and `complete-signup` does\nnot import it — measured by the executor before any fix. Section 1's deferred rate-limit\nquestion is ANSWERED by measurement: the CLI did not push `email_sent = 2` into the auth\ncontainer (the container carried `GOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000`, recorded in\n`stack-up.txt` beside the reading), so the file's cap did not bind the run; check (f) passed\nhonestly and the D-B relief valve did not fire. The proof script's rate-limit comment was\namended in the open so no stated fact contradicts that measurement. Executor invocations this\nsitting: two of the permitted three (the goal, reached on its first attempt; the one-comment\ntruth amendment).","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":566,"totalLines":566,"truncated":false}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\plan.md","time":{"start":1786290388854,"end":1786290388874}},"id":"prt_fe7343e78001SckhbdPOw23yMc","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe7342274001LsA5UmnoZvxp7a"}}
HEAD:loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10241:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:6:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290395229,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_R88P3oJepUbm7eva1K6l7065","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\pr-body.md"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\pr-body.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # AI4DEV-59 — Email verification, and the gate on unverified writing\n2: \n3: Deliverable D2 leaf L1 of the authentication requirement (`loop/decomp/req-001.md`, revision\n4: `0579425`): the email-verification flow for every email-capable account type, and the gate that\n5: stops an email-unverified account writing Discovery messages.\n6: \n7: **Acceptance ids this leaf owns:** AT-001.09, AT-001.10\n8: (`.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`). It builds on the accounts leaf and the GitHub\n9: leaf (pull requests #47 and #48), which landed signup, sign-in and the account types this flow\n10: verifies.\n11: \n12: **Status: built, reviewed twice, fixes applied.** The plan is at\n13: `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/plan.md`. Two external review gates have run and both are ruled. The plan\n14: gate raised four findings; the draft-code gate was a panel of two blind seats and raised twelve.\n15: All sixteen are accepted — several fixed differently from the remedy the reviewer proposed — and\n16: every disposition, with the finding quoted verbatim, is in the plan's rulings sections 7 and 8.\n17: The fixes are applied and the live-stack proof is run. Still ahead of merge: the read-only audit,\n18: and continuous integration green on the exact head. The merge ruling — what was built, every\n19: review finding and its disposition, and exactly what the green does and does not claim, pinned to\n20: the head it licenses — will be posted on this pull request before merge.\n21: \n22: **The shape, in one paragraph.** \"Verified\" is Supabase Auth's own fact\n23: (`email_confirmed_at`); this item ships no migration. The local auth config turns email\n24: confirmations on — the change the suite's own header reserves for this leaf. The gate ships as\n25: a shared decision module (`supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`) that the future\n26: Discovery send route must call; no Discovery surface is built here, and no green from this item\n27: may be read as \"Discovery messaging is gated in production\". The two acceptance tests go from\n28: declared-pending stubs to real bodies at the loop tier (9 green / 28 declared red for the\n29: requirement), and a live local-stack transcript carries the evidence the loop tier cannot\n30: reach: the real confirmation email, the link flipping the account to verified, and sign-in\n31: refused before confirmation.\n\n(End of file - total 31 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# AI4DEV-59 — Email verification, and the gate on unverified writing\n\nDeliverable D2 leaf L1 of the authentication requirement (`loop/decomp/req-001.md`, revision\n`0579425`): the email-verification flow for every email-capable account type, and the gate that\nstops an email-unverified account writing Discovery messages.\n\n**Acceptance ids this leaf owns:** AT-001.09, AT-001.10\n(`.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`). It builds on the accounts leaf and the GitHub\nleaf (pull requests #47 and #48), which landed signup, sign-in and the account types this flow\nverifies.\n\n**Status: built, reviewed twice, fixes applied.** The plan is at\n`loop/items/AI4DEV-59/plan.md`. Two external review gates have run and both are ruled. The plan\ngate raised four findings; the draft-code gate was a panel of two blind seats and raised twelve.\nAll sixteen are accepted — several fixed differently from the remedy the reviewer proposed — and\nevery disposition, with the finding quoted verbatim, is in the plan's rulings sections 7 and 8.\nThe fixes are applied and the live-stack proof is run. Still ahead of merge: the read-only audit,\nand continuous integration green on the exact head. The merge ruling — what was built, every\nreview finding and its disposition, and exactly what the green does and does not claim, pinned to\nthe head it licenses — will be posted on this pull request before merge.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\pr-body.md","text":"# AI4DEV-59 — Email verification, and the gate on unverified writing\n\nDeliverable D2 leaf L1 of the authentication requirement (`loop/decomp/req-001.md`, revision\n`0579425`): the email-verification flow for every email-capable account type, and the gate that\nstops an email-unverified account writing Discovery messages.\n\n**Acceptance ids this leaf owns:** AT-001.09, AT-001.10\n(`.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`). It builds on the accounts leaf and the GitHub\nleaf (pull requests #47 and #48), which landed signup, sign-in and the account types this flow\nverifies.\n\n**Status: built, reviewed twice, fixes applied.** The plan is at\n`loop/items/AI4DEV-59/plan.md`. Two external review gates have run and both are ruled. The plan\ngate raised four findings; the draft-code gate was a panel of two blind seats and raised twelve.\nAll sixteen are accepted — several fixed differently from the remedy the reviewer proposed — and\nevery disposition, with the finding quoted verbatim, is in the plan's rulings sections 7 and 8.\nThe fixes are applied and the live-stack proof is run. Still ahead of merge: the read-only audit,\nand continuous integration green on the exact head. The merge ruling — what was built, every\nreview finding and its disposition, and exactly what the green does and does not claim, pinned to\nthe head it licenses — will be posted on this pull request before merge.\n\n**The shape, in one paragraph.** \"Verified\" is Supabase Auth's own fact\n(`email_confirmed_at`); this item ships no migration. The local auth config turns email\nconfirmations on — the change the suite's own header reserves for this leaf. The gate ships as\na shared decision module (`supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`) that the future\nDiscovery send route must call; no Discovery surface is built here, and no green from this item\nmay be read as \"Discovery messaging is gated in production\". The two acceptance tests go from\ndeclared-pending stubs to real bodies at the loop tier (9 green / 28 declared red for the\nrequirement), and a live local-stack transcript carries the evidence the loop tier cannot\nreach: the real confirmation email, the link flipping the account to verified, and sign-in\nrefused before confirmation.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":31,"totalLines":31,"truncated":false}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\pr-body.md","time":{"start":1786290395215,"end":1786290395227}},"id":"prt_fe734574f001XgTvvMeUPTceDP","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe73445270019yfmL1VZxizM0z"}}
HEAD:loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10242:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:7:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290395510,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_8Z5BrHUISz4UC4COq7Ge9582","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\proof-local.ts"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\proof-local.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /**\n2:  * THE LOCAL PROOF for AI4DEV-59 — the only evidence in this item about the real Supabase Auth\n3:  * under `enable_confirmations = true`, the real confirmation email, and the real\n4:  * `/auth/v1/user` serialisation the shipped extractor has to read.\n5:  *\n6:  *   bun loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts\n7:  *\n8:  * Preconditions, and the script refuses rather than guesses if any is missing:\n9:  *   1. `bun run db:start` — the stack is up, WITH this item's `supabase/config.toml`. The flip to\n10:  *      `enable_confirmations = true` is read at Auth start-up, so a stack started before the flip\n11:  *      is running the old setting and every check below would measure the wrong system.\n12:  *   2. `bun run db:reset` — run it immediately before this script. The checks assert row counts.\n13:  *   3. `bunx supabase functions serve` — running in another terminal, FROM THIS WORKTREE, WITHOUT\n14:  *      --no-verify-jwt. The directory matters and is not pedantry: the edge runtime container\n15:  *      mounts whichever project directory launched it, and this machine was once found serving a\n16:  *      worktree that had since been deleted. A stale mount answers preflights perfectly while\n17:  *      running somebody else's code. The script probes the function before using it.\n18:  *\n19:  * IT LIVES IN THE ITEM RECORD AND NOT IN `tests/`, DELIBERATELY, for the reason the predecessor's\n20:  * copy of this file gives: it is evidence-gathering. It runs once, on one machine, by hand, and\n21:  * its transcript is the artifact. Under `tests/` it would look like something that guards the\n22:  * tree, and it guards nothing — nothing runs it again and CI has no database.\n23:  *\n24:  * WHAT THE LOOP-TIER SUITE ALREADY PROVES, so this does not duplicate it: that the shipped\n25:  * decisions in `_shared/verification.ts` behave as AT-001.09 and AT-001.10 require, including the\n26:  * fail-closed answers, which `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` drives directly.\n27:  * What NOTHING BUT THIS SCRIPT touches: that GoTrue issues no session at signup with confirmations\n28:  * on, that it refuses sign-in until the address is confirmed, that a confirmation email really\n29:  * arrives and its link really flips `email_confirmed_at`, and that the shipped extractor answers\n30:  * true against GoTrue's REAL serialisation rather than against the fixture's prediction of it.\n31:  *\n32:  * ============================================================================================\n33:  * NO CHECK HERE EXERCISES `discoveryMessageAllowed`, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.\n34:  * ============================================================================================\n35:  *\n36:  * There is no Discovery route on this stack or on any stack — it is REQ-002/004's, and this leaf\n37:  * ships the decision that route must call rather than the route. So there is nothing live to send\n38:  * a Discovery message to. The gate's oracle is the loop-tier body for AT-001.10 plus the direct\n39:  * shape selftest, and this transcript says so instead of leaving a reader to assume a live check\n40:  * happened. What this script DOES add on that subject is check (c): on the live stack an\n41:  * unconfirmed user cannot authenticate at all, so the auth layer blocks upstream of the gate.\n42:  *\n43:  * NO KEY IS WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AND NONE IS PRINTED BY IT. Everything is read from\n44:  * `bunx supabase status -o json` at run time. Redaction is DESIGNED IN rather than applied\n45:  * afterwards, and it covers THREE surfaces, because gate 2 found the first version covering only\n46:  * one:\n47:  *\n48:  *   1. BODIES. `redact()` below walks every object body before it reaches the transcript and\n49:  *      replaces the value under any credential-shaped key name.\n50:  *   2. STRINGS, including a body that is not JSON at all. `redact()` also scrubs JWT-shaped\n51:  *      substrings and `name=value` pairs whose name is credential-shaped, so a raw string answer\n52:  *      cannot carry a token through verbatim.\n53:  *   3. THE REDIRECT LOCATION. `redactedLocation()` cuts the URL fragment — that fragment is where\n54:  *      GoTrue puts the access token it mints when a link is followed — AND replaces EVERY\n55:  *      query-parameter VALUE, keeping the parameter names. A `?token=` or `?code=` redirect can\n56:  *      therefore not land in a file that gets pushed.\n57:  *\n58:  * GitHub push protection has already refused one push in this project for a transcript that\n59:  * captured a status command verbatim. The transcript is ALSO inspected for credential residue by\n60:  * hand before it is committed; that inspection is recorded in the item's report.\n61:  */\n62: \n63: import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n64: import { SQL } from 'bun';\n65: // THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR, IMPORTED RATHER THAN MIRRORED — the whole point of check (d). The\n66: // question this script exists to settle is whether the function that ships answers correctly\n67: // about a REAL GoTrue response, so re-implementing the read here would settle nothing.\n68: import { emailVerifiedFromUser } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';\n69: // The stub import fixture, for the volunteer completion read-back in (e) — the same reason the\n70: // predecessor imports it: comparing the stored profile against the shipped judgement shows the\n71: // values travelled from the edge function into the transaction unchanged, where \"the row is\n72: // non-empty\" would pass over plausible-looking rubbish.\n73: import { stubGithubStatsFor } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts';\n74: \n75: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ reporting machinery */\n76: \n77: type Outcome = 'pass' | 'fail' | 'skip';\n78: type Check = { id: string; title: string; outcome: Outcome; note: string };\n79: \n80: const results: Check[] = [];\n81: \n82: function record(id: string, title: string, passed: boolean, note: string): void {\n83:   results.push({ id, title, outcome: passed ? 'pass' : 'fail', note });\n84:   console.log(`${passed ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}  (${id}) ${title}\\n        ${note}`);\n85: }\n86: \n87: /**\n88:  * A check that could not be attempted. Never a pass and never a failure — a skip says the evidence\n89:  * is MISSING, which is a different statement from the claim being false, and both differ from the\n90:  * claim being proved.\n91:  */\n92: function skip(id: string, title: string, why: string): void {\n93:   results.push({ id, title, outcome: 'skip', note: `SKIPPED — ${why}` });\n94:   console.log(`SKIP  (${id}) ${title}\\n        ${why}`);\n95: }\n96: \n97: function fail(message: string): never {\n98:   console.error(`\\nPRECONDITION FAILED: ${message}`);\n99:   process.exit(2);\n100: }\n101: \n102: /**\n103:  * EVERY BODY GOES THROUGH THIS BEFORE IT REACHES THE TRANSCRIPT.\n104:  *\n105:  * The fields that carry secrets are known and few — GoTrue answers with `access_token`,\n106:  * `refresh_token`, `provider_token` and friends — but the rule here is by NAME PATTERN rather than\n107:  * by a list, because a list is a thing to forget to extend. A value under any key whose name looks\n108:  * like a credential is replaced; the KEY still appears, so the transcript still shows that the\n109:  * field was present, which is usually the fact a check needs.\n110:  *\n111:  * It returns a STRING, not an object, so there is no path by which an unredacted value reaches the\n112:  * output through some later `JSON.stringify`.\n113:  */\n114: const SENSITIVE = /token|secret|password|apikey|api_key|jwt|nonce|otp|code$/i;\n115: \n116: /**\n117:  * A STRING SCRUBBED OF CREDENTIALS, for the surface `redact()`'s key walk cannot see.\n118:  *\n119:  * A key walk protects `{\"access_token\": \"...\"}`. It protects nothing inside a value — and GoTrue\n120:  * answers with raw strings on some paths, while any body at all can carry a link with a token in\n121:  * its query. So two shapes are replaced here:\n122:  *\n123:  *   - A JWT. Three base64url runs separated by dots, beginning `eyJ`, which is what a JSON header\n124:  *     always encodes to. Every access token, refresh token and service key in this stack is one.\n125:  *   - A `name=value` pair whose NAME is credential-shaped by the same pattern the key walk uses.\n126:  *     The name survives, so the transcript still shows which parameter was present.\n127:  */\n128: function scrubString(text: string): string {\n129:   return text\n130:     .replace(/eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/g, '<redacted-jwt>')\n131:     .replace(/([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)=([^&\\s\"'<>]+)/g, (whole, name: string) =>\n132:       SENSITIVE.test(name) ? `${name}=<redacted>` : whole,\n133:     );\n134: }\n135: \n136: function redact(value: unknown): string {\n137:   const walk = (node: unknown): unknown => {\n138:     if (Array.isArray(node)) return node.map(walk);\n139:     if (node !== null && typeof node === 'object') {\n140:       const out: Record<string, unknown> = {};\n141:       for (const [key, inner] of Object.entries(node as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n142:         out[key] = SENSITIVE.test(key) ? '<redacted>' : walk(inner);\n143:       }\n144:       return out;\n145:     }\n146:     // A STRING VALUE IS SCRUBBED TOO. This is the half gate 2 found missing: a body that is not\n147:     // JSON reaches here as one long string, and a key walk never looks inside it.\n148:     if (typeof node === 'string') return scrubString(node);\n149:     return node;\n150:   };\n151:   try {\n152:     return JSON.stringify(walk(value));\n153:   } catch {\n154:     return '<unserialisable>';\n155:   }\n156: }\n157: \n158: /**\n159:  * A URL SAFE TO PRINT: no fragment, and no query-parameter VALUES.\n160:  *\n161:  * The fragment is where GoTrue puts the minted access token. The QUERY is where it puts the\n162:  * token hash, the `code`, and the error description — and printing those unchanged was the defect\n163:  * two blind reviewers found independently. Parameter NAMES are kept, because which parameters a\n164:  * redirect carried is usually the fact a check needs, and a name is not a credential.\n165:  */\n166: function redactedLocation(url: string): string {\n167:   if (url === '') return '';\n168:   const hash = url.indexOf('#');\n169:   const fragmentNote = hash < 0 ? '' : '#<redacted-fragment>';\n170:   const withoutHash = hash < 0 ? url : url.slice(0, hash);\n171: \n172:   const question = withoutHash.indexOf('?');\n173:   if (question < 0) return `${withoutHash}${fragmentNote}`;\n174: \n175:   const query = withoutHash\n176:     .slice(question + 1)\n177:     .split('&')\n178:     .map((pair) => {\n179:       const equals = pair.indexOf('=');\n180:       // A bare flag has no value to redact.\n181:       return equals < 0 ? pair : `${pair.slice(0, equals)}=<redacted>`;\n182:     })\n183:     .join('&');\n184:   return `${withoutHash.slice(0, question)}?${query}${fragmentNote}`;\n185: }\n186: \n187: /* --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the environment */\n188: \n189: const status = await (async () => {\n190:   const proc = Bun.spawnSync(['bunx', 'supabase', 'status', '-o', 'json'], { stdout: 'pipe', stderr: 'pipe' });\n191:   const text = new TextDecoder().decode(proc.stdout);\n192:   const start = text.indexOf('{');\n193:   // The raw text is NOT included in this message, deliberately: it is the whole status payload and\n194:   // it carries key material. A failure here says what to do, not what it read.\n195:   if (start < 0) fail('could not read `supabase status -o json` — is the stack up? (bun run db:start)');\n196:   return JSON.parse(text.slice(start)) as Record<string, string>;\n197: })();\n198: \n199: const API_URL = status.API_URL ?? fail('supabase status reported no API_URL');\n200: const DB_URL = status.DB_URL ?? fail('supabase status reported no DB_URL');\n201: /** The browser's key. Everything a signed-out or signed-in visitor could do uses this and no other. */\n202: const PUBLIC_KEY = status.ANON_KEY ?? status.PUBLISHABLE_KEY ?? fail('supabase status reported no client key');\n203: /** The server's key. Used ONLY where the check is about an authority the public never holds. */\n204: const SECRET_KEY = status.SERVICE_ROLE_KEY ?? status.SECRET_KEY ?? fail('supabase status reported no service key');\n205: /**\n206:  * The local mail catcher. Its port is 54324 in `supabase/config.toml`, and its API SHAPE is\n207:  * measured below rather than assumed — see `discoverMailCatcher`.\n208:  */\n209: const MAIL_URL = status.MAILPIT_URL ?? status.INBUCKET_URL ?? 'http://127.0.0.1:54324';\n210: \n211: const sql = new SQL(DB_URL);\n212: \n213: const TEXT_VERSION = 'tos-2026-01+promise-2026-01';\n214: const PASSWORD = 'correct horse battery staple';\n215: const CLIENT_IP = '203.0.113.7';\n216: const VOLUNTEER_HANDLE = 'octoverifier';\n217: /** Unique per run, so a second run against a database that was not reset still reads honestly. */\n218: const RUN = Date.now().toString(36);\n219: const address = (local: string) => `${local}+${RUN}@example.test`;\n220: \n221: /**\n222:  * The configured per-hour cap on auth emails, read from the file rather than remembered.\n223:  *\n224:  * This run sends TWO confirmation emails — one per email-capable account type — and the checked-in\n225:  * value is 2, so the file's cap sits EXACTLY on what this run sends — which is why check (f)\n226:  * exists and why the plan pre-authorized raising it.\n227:  *\n228:  * MEASURED ON THE FIRST RUN: the CLI did NOT push this file value into the auth container — the\n229:  * container carried GOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000, recorded in stack-up.txt beside the\n230:  * reading — so the file's cap did not bind that run. Check (f) still reports the file's value AND\n231:  * whether any mailer refusal appeared, which together are the honest pair: the transcript shows\n232:  * what the file claims and what the mailer actually did.\n233:  */\n234: const CONFIGURED_EMAIL_RATE_LIMIT = (() => {\n235:   try {\n236:     const toml = readFileSync(new URL('../../../supabase/config.toml', import.meta.url), 'utf8');\n237:     return /^\\s*email_sent\\s*=\\s*(\\d+)/m.exec(toml)?.[1] ?? '<not found>';\n238:   } catch {\n239:     return '<unreadable>';\n240:   }\n241: })();\n242: \n243: /**\n244:  * Anything that looks like the mailer refusing to send. Check (f) reports on this, so a starved\n245:  * run can never look like a clean one.\n246:  */\n247: const rateLimitEvidence: string[] = [];\n248: \n249: function noteIfRateLimited(where: string, httpStatus: number, body: unknown): void {\n250:   const text = typeof body === 'string' ? body : JSON.stringify(body ?? null);\n251:   if (httpStatus === 429 || /rate.?limit|over_email_send/i.test(text)) {\n252:     rateLimitEvidence.push(`${where}: HTTP ${httpStatus} ${redact(body)}`);\n253:   }\n254: }\n255: \n256: /* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Auth calls */\n257: \n258: type Wire = { status: number; body: unknown };\n259: \n260: async function authPost(path: string, body: unknown, key = PUBLIC_KEY): Promise<Wire> {\n261:   const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}${path}`, {\n262:     method: 'POST',\n263:     headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', apikey: key, Authorization: `Bearer ${key}` },\n264:     body: JSON.stringify(body),\n265:   });\n266:   const text = await response.text();\n267:   let parsed: unknown = text;\n268:   try {\n269:     parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n270:   } catch {\n271:     // A non-JSON answer is itself evidence; it is kept as the raw string.\n272:   }\n273:   return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n274: }\n275: \n276: /**\n277:  * Register an email/password user. WITH CONFIRMATIONS ON THIS MUST RETURN NO SESSION, which is\n278:  * check (a)'s first half, so the caller inspects the wire response rather than being handed a\n279:  * tidy session object.\n280:  *\n281:  * THE USER ID IS TAKEN FROM THE RESPONSE WHEN IT IS THERE, AND FROM THE DATABASE WHEN IT IS NOT.\n282:  * GoTrue's confirmations-on signup response has varied across versions — some return the full user\n283:  * record, some return an obfuscated one to avoid leaking whether an address already exists. Which\n284:  * one this stack does is recorded rather than assumed, and neither answer is a failure: the id is\n285:  * an operator read either way.\n286:  */\n287: async function signUp(email: string): Promise<{ wire: Wire; userId: string; idFrom: 'response' | 'database' }> {\n288:   const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/signup', { email, password: PASSWORD });\n289:   noteIfRateLimited(`signup ${email}`, wire.status, wire.body);\n290:   if (wire.status >= 400) fail(`sign-up for ${email} failed (HTTP ${wire.status}): ${redact(wire.body)}`);\n291: \n292:   const body = wire.body as { id?: string; user?: { id?: string } } | null;\n293:   const fromResponse = body?.user?.id ?? body?.id;\n294:   if (typeof fromResponse === 'string' && fromResponse !== '') {\n295:     return { wire, userId: fromResponse, idFrom: 'response' };\n296:   }\n297:   const rows = await sql`select id from auth.users where email = ${email}`;\n298:   if (rows.length !== 1) fail(`sign-up for ${email} left ${rows.length} rows in auth.users — expected exactly 1`);\n299:   return { wire, userId: String(rows[0].id), idFrom: 'database' };\n300: }\n301: \n302: /** Sign in with a password. Returns the wire answer AND the token, which is never printed. */\n303: async function signIn(email: string): Promise<{ wire: Wire; accessToken: string | null }> {\n304:   const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/token?grant_type=password', { email, password: PASSWORD });\n305:   const token = (wire.body as { access_token?: string } | null)?.access_token;\n306:   return { wire, accessToken: typeof token === 'string' && token !== '' ? token : null };\n307: }\n308: \n309: /**\n310:  * GoTrue's error code for \"this address is not confirmed yet\".\n311:  *\n312:  * It is the DISCRIMINATOR check (c) needs, and it is pinned as a constant so the transcript states\n313:  * which value was expected. If the live stack answers a different code, the check FAILS and the\n314:  * verbatim capture beside it shows the real one — measurement, never assumption.\n315:  */\n316: const CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE = 'email_not_confirmed';\n317: \n318: /**\n319:  * IS THIS THE CONFIRMATION REFUSAL, or merely A refusal — the distinction gate 2 found missing.\n320:  *\n321:  * `status >= 400` alone passes on a 429 from the mailer's rate limiter and on a 500 from a broken\n322:  * stack, and either would record \"confirmation is enforced\" while confirmation enforced nothing.\n323:  * So a pass needs BOTH halves:\n324:  *\n325:  *   - a 4xx that is NOT 429 — a client refusal, not a throttle and not a server fault;\n326:  *   - a body that NAMES the unconfirmed state — the pinned error code, or `confirm` in the\n327:  *     message, which is the weaker initial predicate the ruling allows.\n328:  *\n329:  * IT READS THE IN-MEMORY BODY, NOT THE PRINTED ONE, and that is load-bearing: the transcript\n330:  * redactor's key pattern ends with `code$`, so `error_code` is blanked in everything it prints.\n331:  * Judging the printed text would judge `<redacted>`. The discriminator is surfaced separately\n332:  * below through a safe extraction — an error code is an enum value, not a credential.\n333:  */\n334: function unconfirmedSignInRefusal(wire: Wire): { ok: boolean; discriminator: string } {\n335:   const body =\n336:     wire.body !== null && typeof wire.body === 'object'\n337:       ? (wire.body as Record<string, unknown>)\n338:       : {};\n339:   const errorCode = typeof body.error_code === 'string' ? body.error_code : '';\n340:   const message = [body.msg, body.message, body.error_description, body.error, wire.body]\n341:     .filter((part): part is string => typeof part === 'string')\n342:     .join(' | ');\n343: \n344:   const statusIsClientRefusal = wire.status >= 400 && wire.status < 500 && wire.status !== 429;\n345:   const bodyNamesTheState = errorCode === CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE || /confirm/i.test(message);\n346: \n347:   return {\n348:     ok: statusIsClientRefusal && bodyNamesTheState,\n349:     discriminator:\n350:       `HTTP ${wire.status} · error_code=${errorCode === '' ? '<absent>' : errorCode}` +\n351:       ` (expected ${CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE}) · message=${JSON.stringify(scrubString(message) || '<absent>')}`,\n352:   };\n353: }\n354: \n355: /**\n356:  * THE SAME LINK WITH A TOKEN GOTRUE NEVER ISSUED — check (b2)'s instrument.\n357:  *\n358:  * The mirror under test says a link that was never issued confirms nothing, and following a\n359:  * MUTATED token is how that is measured without touching any semantics REQ-001 does not state.\n360:  * The token this returns was never minted, so following it says nothing about expiry, about single\n361:  * use, or about resend — retired AT-001.11 stays untouched. Every other part of the link, the\n362:  * `type` and the redirect included, is left exactly as GoTrue wrote it, so the only difference\n363:  * between this request and the real one is the token itself.\n364:  *\n365:  * The mutation shifts digits and letters inside their own alphabet, which keeps the token the same\n366:  * length and shape. A token that changed shape could be refused for being malformed rather than\n367:  * for being unissued, and the check would then measure input validation instead of the mirror.\n368:  */\n369: function tamperVerificationToken(link: string): { tampered: string; parameter: string } | null {\n370:   let url: URL;\n371:   try {\n372:     url = new URL(link);\n373:   } catch {\n374:     return null;\n375:   }\n376:   const parameter = ['token', 'token_hash', 'confirmation_token'].find((name) => {\n377:     const value = url.searchParams.get(name);\n378:     return typeof value === 'string' && value !== '';\n379:   });\n380:   if (parameter === undefined) return null;\n381: \n382:   const original = url.searchParams.get(parameter) as string;\n383:   const mutated = original.replace(/[0-9a-zA-Z]/g, (character) => {\n384:     if (character >= '0' && character <= '9') return String((Number(character) + 5) % 10);\n385:     const base = character >= 'a' ? 'a'.charCodeAt(0) : 'A'.charCodeAt(0);\n386:     return String.fromCharCode(base + ((character.charCodeAt(0) - base + 13) % 26));\n387:   });\n388:   if (mutated === original) return null;\n389: \n390:   url.searchParams.set(parameter, mutated);\n391:   return { tampered: url.toString(), parameter };\n392: }\n393: \n394: /** `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` read as the operator, on port 54322 — not through any API. */\n395: async function confirmedAt(userId: string): Promise<string | null> {\n396:   const rows = await sql`select email_confirmed_at from auth.users where id = ${userId}::uuid`;\n397:   const value = rows[0]?.email_confirmed_at ?? null;\n398:   return value === null ? null : String(value instanceof Date ? value.toISOString() : value);\n399: }\n400: \n401: /**\n402:  * THE POST-LINK AUTH STATE FOR GITHUB, FABRICATED BY OPERATOR AUTHORITY — carried over from the\n403:  * predecessor item unchanged, including the correction that cost it real time.\n404:  *\n405:  * Linking a GitHub identity is an OAuth consent round trip: a person at github.com and a redirect\n406:  * no script can drive. What linking LEAVES BEHIND is a row in `auth.identities`, and that row is\n407:  * what the shipped code reads. So the row is written directly, as the operator. IT IS\n408:  * FLIP-INDEPENDENT — a database write, not an auth flow — which is why (e) can reuse it under the\n409:  * new setting without re-proving it.\n410:  *\n411:  * `created_at`, `updated_at` and `last_sign_in_at` are NULLABLE in the table, so a row without them\n412:  * inserts happily; but GoTrue reads identities into a Go struct with non-pointer timestamps, so\n413:  * `GET /auth/v1/user` then answers 500 for that user, and the whole thing looks exactly like an\n414:  * expired token rather than like a malformed row three layers away. So they are filled here.\n415:  */\n416: async function fabricateGithubIdentity(userId: string, handle: string): Promise<void> {\n417:   await sql`\n418:     insert into auth.identities (id, user_id, provider, provider_id, identity_data,\n419:                                  created_at, updated_at, last_sign_in_at)\n420:     values (\n421:       gen_random_uuid(),\n422:       ${userId}::uuid,\n423:       'github',\n424:       ${`gh-${handle}-${RUN}`},\n425:       -- The casts are explicit because jsonb_build_object takes \"any\", so PostgreSQL has nothing to\n426:       -- infer a parameter's type from and refuses the statement outright rather than guessing.\n427:       jsonb_build_object('sub', ${userId}::text, 'user_name', ${handle}::text, 'provider_id', ${`gh-${handle}-${RUN}`}::text),\n428:       now(), now(), now()\n429:     )`;\n430: }\n431: \n432: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ the edge function */\n433: \n434: async function callFunction(name: string, accessToken: string, body: unknown): Promise<Wire> {\n435:   const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/functions/v1/${name}`, {\n436:     method: 'POST',\n437:     headers: {\n438:       'content-type': 'application/json',\n439:       apikey: PUBLIC_KEY,\n440:       Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`,\n441:       'x-forwarded-for': CLIENT_IP,\n442:     },\n443:     body: JSON.stringify(body),\n444:   });\n445:   const text = await response.text();\n446:   let parsed: unknown = text;\n447:   try {\n448:     parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n449:   } catch {\n450:     /* kept raw */\n451:   }\n452:   return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n453: }\n454: \n455: // The stale-mount hazard the header names: probe the function before any check depends on it. An\n456: // unauthenticated POST must be REFUSED by something, and a connection error is a different failure\n457: // from a refusal. Only the second means the function is being served.\n458: //\n459: // THE PROBE GUARDS REACHABILITY, NOT REVISION, AND (e)'s EVIDENCE DOES NOT NEED IT TO.\n460: // A reviewer read this as a hole: a stale mount serving an older worktree answers 401, so any\n461: // non-404 passes and the probe cannot tell which REVISION of `complete-signup` replied. The fact is\n462: // right; the consequence is not, and the reason was verified in the tree before this comment was\n463: // written. This branch changes NOTHING under `supabase/functions/` except the NEW\n464: // `_shared/verification.ts` (`git diff main...HEAD --stat -- supabase/functions/`: one file, 167\n465: // insertions), and `complete-signup/index.ts` imports only `_shared/accounts.ts`,\n466: // `_shared/github.ts` and `_shared/edge.ts` — never `verification.ts`. So every revision of\n467: // `complete-signup` this branch could be compared against is byte for byte the same code, and the\n468: // mount's revision is not load-bearing for anything (e) claims.\n469: // The operator side of the binding is recorded rather than inferred:\n470: // `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/stack-up.txt` carries the exact `bunx supabase functions serve` command and\n471: // the directory it ran in.\n472: await (async () => {\n473:   try {\n474:     const probe = await fetch(`${API_URL}/functions/v1/complete-signup`, {\n475:       method: 'POST',\n476:       headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', apikey: PUBLIC_KEY },\n477:       body: '{}',\n478:     });\n479:     if (probe.status === 404) {\n480:       fail('the edge runtime answered 404 for complete-signup — `bunx supabase functions serve` is not serving THIS worktree');\n481:     }\n482:   } catch (error) {\n483:     fail(\n484:       'could not reach the edge runtime at all — run `bunx supabase functions serve` from THIS worktree ' +\n485:         `(${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)})`,\n486:     );\n487:   }\n488: })();\n489: \n490: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------- the mail catcher, MEASURED */\n491: \n492: type CatcherKind = 'mailpit' | 'inbucket' | 'unknown';\n493: \n494: /**\n495:  * WHICH MAIL CATCHER IS BEHIND PORT 54324, established by asking it rather than by remembering.\n496:  *\n497:  * The Supabase CLI has shipped Inbucket and, more recently, Mailpit, and their APIs are not the\n498:  * same shape. The plan's risk 2 is exactly this. So both are probed, BOTH ANSWERS GO IN THE\n499:  * TRANSCRIPT, and the extraction below branches on what actually replied. An assumption here would\n500:  * fail as \"no confirmation email arrived\", which is indistinguishable from the mailer being\n501:  * rate-limited — the single most misleading way this script could go wrong.\n502:  */\n503: async function discoverMailCatcher(): Promise<{ kind: CatcherKind; evidence: string }> {\n504:   const probe = async (path: string): Promise<string> => {\n505:     try {\n506:       const response = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}${path}`);\n507:       const text = (await response.text()).slice(0, 200);\n508:       return `GET ${path} -> HTTP ${response.status} ${JSON.stringify(text)}`;\n509:     } catch (error) {\n510:       return `GET ${path} -> unreachable (${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)})`;\n511:     }\n512:   };\n513: \n514:   const mailpit = await probe('/api/v1/info');\n515:   const inbucket = await probe('/api/v1/mailbox/probe');\n516:   const evidence = `catcher at ${MAIL_URL} · ${mailpit} · ${inbucket}`;\n517: \n518:   if (/HTTP 200/.test(mailpit)) return { kind: 'mailpit', evidence };\n519:   if (/HTTP 200/.test(inbucket)) return { kind: 'inbucket', evidence };\n520:   return { kind: 'unknown', evidence };\n521: }\n522: \n523: const catcher = await discoverMailCatcher();\n524: console.log(`\\nmail catcher shape, measured: ${catcher.kind}\\n        ${catcher.evidence}\\n`);\n525: \n526: /**\n527:  * Every URL in the message that looks like GoTrue's verify endpoint.\n528:  *\n529:  * `&amp;` IS DECODED FIRST, and that is not cosmetic: the confirmation email's HTML part escapes\n530:  * the ampersands, so a link lifted straight out of it carries `&amp;type=signup` and GoTrue reads\n531:  * the parameter name as `amp;type`. The failure looks like a rejected link rather than like a\n532:  * mangled one.\n533:  */\n534: function verifyLinksIn(text: string): string[] {\n535:   const decoded = text.replace(/&amp;/g, '&');\n536:   return decoded.match(/https?:\\/\\/[^\\s\"'<>)\\]]+\\/auth\\/v1\\/verify[^\\s\"'<>)\\]]*/g) ?? [];\n537: }\n538: \n539: async function emailedVerificationLink(email: string): Promise<{ link: string | null; evidence: string }> {\n540:   const local = email.split('@')[0];\n541:   try {\n542:     if (catcher.kind === 'mailpit') {\n543:       const list = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/search?query=${encodeURIComponent(`to:${email}`)}&limit=20`);\n544:       const listed = (await list.json()) as { messages?: Array<{ ID?: string }> };\n545:       const id = listed.messages?.[0]?.ID;\n546:       if (!id) return { link: null, evidence: `mailpit search for ${email} returned no message` };\n547:       const message = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/message/${id}`);\n548:       const parsed = (await message.json()) as { Text?: string; HTML?: string };\n549:       const links = verifyLinksIn(`${parsed.Text ?? ''}\\n${parsed.HTML ?? ''}`);\n550:       return {\n551:         link: links[0] ?? null,\n552:         evidence: `mailpit held ${listed.messages?.length ?? 0} message(s) for ${email}; ${links.length} verify link(s) found`,\n553:       };\n554:     }\n555:     if (catcher.kind === 'inbucket') {\n556:       const list = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/mailbox/${encodeURIComponent(local)}`);\n557:       const listed = (await list.json()) as Array<{ id?: string }>;\n558:       const id = listed[0]?.id;\n559:       if (!id) return { link: null, evidence: `inbucket mailbox ${local} was empty` };\n560:       const message = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/mailbox/${encodeURIComponent(local)}/${id}`);\n561:       const parsed = (await message.json()) as { body?: { text?: string; html?: string } };\n562:       const links = verifyLinksIn(`${parsed.body?.text ?? ''}\\n${parsed.body?.html ?? ''}`);\n563:       return {\n564:         link: links[0] ?? null,\n565:         evidence: `inbucket mailbox ${local} held ${listed.length} message(s); ${links.length} verify link(s) found`,\n566:       };\n567:     }\n568:     return { link: null, evidence: `the catcher at ${MAIL_URL} matched no known API shape` };\n569:   } catch (error) {\n570:     return { link: null, evidence: `reading the catcher failed: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}` };\n571:   }\n572: }\n573: \n574: /**\n575:  * THE FALLBACK THE PLAN'S RISK 2 PRE-AUTHORIZES, and it NARROWS the claim rather than rescuing it.\n576:  *\n577:  * GoTrue's admin endpoint mints a verification link with operator authority. If the catcher cannot\n578:  * yield the emailed link, this is what gets followed — and the transcript then says, in the check's\n579:  * own note, that the link followed was NOT the emailed one. That is a smaller claim, stated as a\n580:  * smaller claim. It is never used when the catcher works.\n581:  */\n582: async function adminGeneratedLink(email: string): Promise<{ link: string | null; evidence: string }> {\n583:   const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/admin/generate_link', { type: 'signup', email, password: PASSWORD }, SECRET_KEY);\n584:   const link = (wire.body as { action_link?: string } | null)?.action_link ?? null;\n585:   return {\n586:     link: typeof link === 'string' && link !== '' ? link : null,\n587:     evidence: `admin generate_link -> HTTP ${wire.status} (action_link ${link ? 'present' : 'absent'})`,\n588:   };\n589: }\n590: \n591: /* ============================================================================================\n592:  * THE VERIFICATION ROUND TRIP — (a) through (d), as one function so the SECOND address in (e)\n593:  * repeats exactly the same measurement rather than an abbreviated version of it.\n594:  * ============================================================================================ */\n595: \n596: /**\n597:  * What following a tampered link did — or why it could not be followed at all.\n598:  *\n599:  * `attempted: false` is never a pass. Check (b2) SKIPS on it, because a check that could not run\n600:  * is a missing measurement rather than a satisfied one.\n601:  */\n602: type TamperedFollow = {\n603:   attempted: boolean;\n604:   why: string;\n605:   parameter: string;\n606:   status: number;\n607:   location: string;\n608:   confirmedAfter: string | null;\n609: };\n610: \n611: type RoundTrip = {\n612:   email: string;\n613:   userId: string;\n614:   idFrom: 'response' | 'database';\n615:   signupWire: Wire;\n616:   /** the whole point of (a): with confirmations on there must be NO token in the signup answer */\n617:   signupCarriedSession: boolean;\n618:   confirmedBefore: string | null;\n619:   linkSource: 'emailed' | 'admin-generated' | 'none';\n620:   linkEvidence: string;\n621:   signInBefore: Wire;\n622:   /** check (b2): the never-issued-token negative, measured BEFORE the real link is followed */\n623:   tampered: TamperedFollow;\n624:   followStatus: number;\n625:   followLocation: string;\n626:   confirmedAfter: string | null;\n627:   signInAfter: Wire;\n628:   accessToken: string | null;\n629:   rawUserWire: Wire;\n630:   shippedVerdict: boolean;\n631: };\n632: \n633: async function verificationRoundTrip(email: string): Promise<RoundTrip> {\n634:   const { wire: signupWire, userId, idFrom } = await signUp(email);\n635:   const signupBody = signupWire.body as { access_token?: unknown; session?: unknown } | null;\n636:   const signupCarriedSession =\n637:     typeof signupBody?.access_token === 'string' || (signupBody?.session !== null && signupBody?.session !== undefined);\n638: \n639:   const confirmedBefore = await confirmedAt(userId);\n640: \n641:   // (c) is measured BEFORE the link is followed, because afterwards the same call succeeds and the\n642:   // refusal would be unobtainable.\n643:   const signInBefore = (await signIn(email)).wire;\n644: \n645:   const fromCatcher = await emailedVerificationLink(email);\n646:   let link = fromCatcher.link;\n647:   let linkSource: RoundTrip['linkSource'] = link ? 'emailed' : 'none';\n648:   let linkEvidence = fromCatcher.evidence;\n649:   if (!link) {\n650:     const fallback = await adminGeneratedLink(email);\n651:     if (fallback.link) {\n652:       link = fallback.link;\n653:       linkSource = 'admin-generated';\n654:     }\n655:     linkEvidence = `${fromCatcher.evidence} · FALLBACK: ${fallback.evidence}`;\n656:   }\n657: \n658:   // (b2) THE NEVER-ISSUED TOKEN, FOLLOWED BEFORE THE REAL LINK.\n659:   //\n660:   // The order matters and is the whole design of this block: once the real link has confirmed the\n661:   // address, `email_confirmed_at` is non-null for every later read, and the negative becomes\n662:   // unmeasurable. So the tampered variant goes first, and the column is read straight afterwards.\n663:   const tampered: TamperedFollow = {\n664:     attempted: false,\n665:     why: '',\n666:     parameter: '',\n667:     status: 0,\n668:     location: '',\n669:     confirmedAfter: null,\n670:   };\n671:   if (!link) {\n672:     tampered.why = 'no verification link was obtained at all, so there was nothing to tamper with';\n673:   } else {\n674:     const mutation = tamperVerificationToken(link);\n675:     if (mutation === null) {\n676:       tampered.why = 'the link carries no non-empty token parameter this script knows how to mutate';\n677:     } else {\n678:       const followed = await fetch(mutation.tampered, { redirect: 'manual' });\n679:       tampered.attempted = true;\n680:       tampered.parameter = mutation.parameter;\n681:       tampered.status = followed.status;\n682:       tampered.location = redactedLocation(followed.headers.get('location') ?? '');\n683:       tampered.confirmedAfter = await confirmedAt(userId);\n684:     }\n685:   }\n686: \n687:   let followStatus = 0;\n688:   let followLocation = '';\n689:   if (link) {\n690:     const followed = await fetch(link, { redirect: 'manual' });\n691:     followStatus = followed.status;\n692:     // THE FRAGMENT IS CUT AND EVERY QUERY VALUE IS REPLACED. GoTrue redirects to the site URL with\n693:     // the freshly minted access token in the URL fragment, and this string goes into a file that\n694:     // gets pushed.\n695:     followLocation = redactedLocation(followed.headers.get('location') ?? '');\n696:   }\n697: \n698:   const confirmedAfter = await confirmedAt(userId);\n699:   const after = await signIn(email);\n700:   const rawUserWire: Wire = after.accessToken\n701:     ? await (async () => {\n702:         const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/auth/v1/user`, {\n703:           headers: { apikey: PUBLIC_KEY, Authorization: `Bearer ${after.accessToken}` },\n704:         });\n705:         const text = await response.text();\n706:         let parsed: unknown = text;\n707:         try {\n708:           parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n709:         } catch {\n710:           /* kept raw */\n711:         }\n712:         return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n713:       })()\n714:     : { status: 0, body: null };\n715: \n716:   return {\n717:     email,\n718:     userId,\n719:     idFrom,\n720:     signupWire,\n721:     signupCarriedSession,\n722:     confirmedBefore,\n723:     linkSource,\n724:     linkEvidence,\n725:     signInBefore,\n726:     tampered,\n727:     followStatus,\n728:     followLocation,\n729:     confirmedAfter,\n730:     signInAfter: after.wire,\n731:     accessToken: after.accessToken,\n732:     rawUserWire,\n733:     // THE SHIPPED FUNCTION, ON THE REAL BODY. This single line is what binds the extractor to\n734:     // GoTrue's actual serialisation; everything else in this script is the setup that makes it\n735:     // meaningful.\n736:     shippedVerdict: emailVerifiedFromUser(rawUserWire.body),\n737:   };\n738: }\n739: \n740: const ngoEmail = address('ngo-verify');\n741: const ngoTrip = await verificationRoundTrip(ngoEmail);\n742: \n743: /* =============================================================================================\n744:  * (a) SIGNUP UNDER THE FLIPPED CONFIG ISSUES NO SESSION, AND THE ADDRESS IS UNCONFIRMED.\n745:  *\n746:  * The first of the plan's three unverified-runtime-claims, measured rather than expected. Both\n747:  * halves matter: no token on the wire, AND `email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read as the operator on\n748:  * port 54322. The wire answer alone could be a client-library convention; the column is the fact.\n749:  * ============================================================================================= */\n750: \n751: record(\n752:   'a',\n753:   'an email/password signup under enable_confirmations = true returns NO session, and auth.users.email_confirmed_at is NULL',\n754:   ngoTrip.signupWire.status < 400 && ngoTrip.signupCarriedSession === false && ngoTrip.confirmedBefore === null,\n755:   `signup HTTP ${ngoTrip.signupWire.status} ${redact(ngoTrip.signupWire.body)}` +\n756:     ` · carried a session: ${ngoTrip.signupCarriedSession}` +\n757:     ` · user id read from the ${ngoTrip.idFrom}` +\n758:     ` · auth.users.email_confirmed_at = ${ngoTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'}`,\n759: );\n760: \n761: /* =============================================================================================\n762:  * (b) THE CONFIRMATION EMAIL EXISTS IN THE CATCHER, AND THE LINK COMES OUT OF IT.\n763:  *\n764:  * The catcher's API shape was measured above, not assumed — the evidence line says which shape\n765:  * answered. If the link came from the admin endpoint instead, this check says so IN ITS OWN NOTE\n766:  * and the claim is narrower: an equivalent link was followed, but not the emailed one.\n767:  *\n768:  * AN UNRECOGNISED CATCHER SKIPS RATHER THAN FAILS, and the distinction is the shared invariant\n769:  * about re-measuring a negative: a broken instrument and a true absence produce identical output.\n770:  * \"The mailbox reads empty\" is not evidence that no email was sent when the thing being asked is\n771:  * not the thing that answers. A skip says the evidence is missing, which is what is true.\n772:  * ============================================================================================= */\n773: \n774: if (catcher.kind === 'unknown') {\n775:   skip(\n776:     'b',\n777:     'the local mail catcher holds a confirmation email for the address, and the verification link is extracted from it',\n778:     `the catcher at ${MAIL_URL} matched no known API shape, so this check has no working instrument. ` +\n779:       `It is NOT evidence that no confirmation email was sent — a broken reader and an empty mailbox look the same. ` +\n780:       `Probe results: ${catcher.evidence}. Fix the reader, or note which catcher this CLI version ships, then re-run.`,\n781:   );\n782: } else {\n783:   record(\n784:     'b',\n785:     'the local mail catcher holds a confirmation email for the address, and the verification link is extracted from it',\n786:     ngoTrip.linkSource === 'emailed',\n787:     `catcher shape: ${catcher.kind} · ${ngoTrip.linkEvidence} · link source: ${ngoTrip.linkSource}` +\n788:       (ngoTrip.linkSource === 'admin-generated'\n789:         ? ' — NARROWED: the EMAILED link was not what was followed; an operator-minted equivalent was.'\n790:         : ''),\n791:   );\n792: }\n793: \n794: /* =============================================================================================\n795:  * (b2) A TOKEN GOTRUE NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.\n796:  *\n797:  * The fixture's mirror 2 says a link that was never issued has no confirming effect. Two blind\n798:  * reviewers found the same defect: that mirror was labelled BOUND by checks (a)–(d), and none of\n799:  * those checks ever follows an unissued link — the named evidence measured only the positive half.\n800:  * This check is the negative, measured.\n801:  *\n802:  * The instrument is the REAL link with its token mutated inside its own alphabet, followed BEFORE\n803:  * the real one. What it asserts is exactly one thing: `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` is still\n804:  * NULL afterwards. It asserts NOTHING about expiry, single use or resend — the token followed was\n805:  * never minted, so no lifetime and no use count is in play, and retired AT-001.11's semantics stay\n806:  * untouched.\n807:  *\n808:  * THE RESPONSE STATUS IS RECORDED BUT NOT ASSERTED ON. GoTrue may answer a redirect carrying an\n809:  * error, or a 4xx, and which one it picks is its own business. The column is the fact.\n810:  * ============================================================================================= */\n811: \n812: if (!ngoTrip.tampered.attempted) {\n813:   skip(\n814:     'b2',\n815:     'a verification link carrying a token GoTrue never issued confirms nothing',\n816:     `${ngoTrip.tampered.why}. Link source was \"${ngoTrip.linkSource}\" (${ngoTrip.linkEvidence}). ` +\n817:       'This is NOT evidence that a never-issued token confirms an address — it is evidence that the ' +\n818:       'negative could not be measured on this run.',\n819:   );\n820: } else {\n821:   record(\n822:     'b2',\n823:     'a verification link carrying a token GoTrue never issued confirms nothing',\n824:     ngoTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter === null,\n825:     `the \"${ngoTrip.tampered.parameter}\" parameter of the ${ngoTrip.linkSource} link was mutated inside its own alphabet` +\n826:       ` · following the tampered link answered HTTP ${ngoTrip.tampered.status} -> ${ngoTrip.tampered.location || '<no location header>'}` +\n827:       ` · auth.users.email_confirmed_at immediately afterwards = ${ngoTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n828:       ' · this says nothing about expiry, single use or resend: the token followed was never issued' +\n829:       (ngoTrip.linkSource === 'admin-generated'\n830:         ? ' · NARROWED: the link mutated was the operator-minted one, because the catcher yielded none.'\n831:         : ''),\n832:   );\n833: }\n834: \n835: /* =============================================================================================\n836:  * (c) SIGN-IN BEFORE CONFIRMATION IS REFUSED, WITH THE REFUSAL TEXT CAPTURED VERBATIM.\n837:  *\n838:  * The second unverified-runtime-claim. On the live stack this block sits UPSTREAM of the shipped\n839:  * gate: an unconfirmed user cannot authenticate at all, so it never reaches a write path. That is\n840:  * why the gate is not redundant rather than why it is — decision-8 makes verification the write\n841:  * path's own floor, and non-public paths do not pass through this refusal.\n842:  *\n843:  * THE REFUSAL MUST BE THE CONFIRMATION ONE, not any refusal. `status >= 400` was the first\n844:  * version's predicate and a reviewer showed what it accepts: a 429 from the mailer's rate limiter\n845:  * and a 500 from a broken stack both record as PASS while confirmation enforces nothing. See\n846:  * `unconfirmedSignInRefusal` above for the two halves a pass now needs.\n847:  * ============================================================================================= */\n848: \n849: const ngoRefusal = unconfirmedSignInRefusal(ngoTrip.signInBefore);\n850: \n851: record(\n852:   'c',\n853:   'sign-in BEFORE confirmation is refused by Auth itself, with a CONFIRMATION-SPECIFIC refusal (a 4xx that is not 429, naming the unconfirmed state)',\n854:   ngoRefusal.ok,\n855:   `sign-in ${ngoRefusal.discriminator}` +\n856:     ` · redacted body: ${redact(ngoTrip.signInBefore.body)} — captured verbatim, this is the refusal text` +\n857:     ' · the discriminator above is read from the in-memory body, because the redactor blanks error_code',\n858: );\n859: \n860: /* =============================================================================================\n861:  * (d) USING THE LINK FLIPS THE COLUMN, SIGN-IN THEN SUCCEEDS, AND THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR AGREES\n862:  *     WITH GOTRUE'S REAL SERIALISATION.\n863:  *\n864:  * The third unverified-runtime-claim and the one this whole script exists for. `emailVerifiedFromUser`\n865:  * is fed the RAW `/auth/v1/user` body — not a shape this script built — and must answer true.\n866:  *\n867:  * ITS FALSE HALF IS NOT EXERCISED HERE, and the transcript says so rather than dressing it up: an\n868:  * unconfirmed user holds no session, so there is no token with which to read `/auth/v1/user` for\n869:  * one. The false half is exercised at the loop tier and in the direct shape selftest.\n870:  * ============================================================================================= */\n871: \n872: record(\n873:   'd',\n874:   'following the verification link flips email_confirmed_at, sign-in then succeeds, and the SHIPPED emailVerifiedFromUser answers true on the real /auth/v1/user body',\n875:   ngoTrip.followStatus >= 300 &&\n876:     ngoTrip.followStatus < 400 &&\n877:     ngoTrip.confirmedAfter !== null &&\n878:     ngoTrip.signInAfter.status === 200 &&\n879:     ngoTrip.accessToken !== null &&\n880:     ngoTrip.rawUserWire.status === 200 &&\n881:     ngoTrip.shippedVerdict === true,\n882:   `link follow HTTP ${ngoTrip.followStatus} -> ${ngoTrip.followLocation || '<no location header>'}` +\n883:     ` · email_confirmed_at before=${ngoTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'} after=${ngoTrip.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n884:     ` · sign-in after HTTP ${ngoTrip.signInAfter.status} (access token present: ${ngoTrip.accessToken !== null})` +\n885:     ` · /auth/v1/user HTTP ${ngoTrip.rawUserWire.status} ${redact(ngoTrip.rawUserWire.body)}` +\n886:     ` · the SHIPPED emailVerifiedFromUser says: ${ngoTrip.shippedVerdict}` +\n887:     ' · the extractor\\'s FALSE half is NOT exercised here: an unconfirmed user holds no session to read /auth/v1/user with',\n888: );\n889: \n890: /* =============================================================================================\n891:  * (e) BOTH EMAIL-CAPABLE ACCOUNT TYPES FLOW END TO END UNDER THE FLIPPED CONFIG.\n892:  *\n893:  * WHY THE PREDECESSOR'S COMPLETION EVIDENCE IS NOT CITED HERE, which is gate-1 ruling [2] adopted\n894:  * whole: the flip changes the auth context beneath BOTH completion paths. That evidence was\n895:  * produced with confirmations OFF, so it predates this change and no longer covers it. Both paths\n896:  * are therefore re-proved rather than referenced.\n897:  *\n898:  * The NGO address, already confirmed by (a)–(d), completes through the DEPLOYED complete-signup\n899:  * edge function. The volunteer address repeats the whole round trip on its own, then gains a\n900:  * GitHub identity by the operator insert above — flip-independent, because it is a database write\n901:  * and not an auth flow — and completes as a volunteer through the same deployed function.\n902:  * ============================================================================================= */\n903: \n904: const ngoCompletion = ngoTrip.accessToken\n905:   ? await callFunction('complete-signup', ngoTrip.accessToken, {\n906:       accountType: 'ngo',\n907:       organizationName: 'Riverside Shelter',\n908:       acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n909:     })\n910:   : { status: 0, body: 'no access token — the NGO never signed in' };\n911: \n912: const ngoRows = await sql`\n913:   select a.account_type, o.name, m.role\n914:     from public.accounts a\n915:     left join public.org_memberships m on m.account_id = a.id\n916:     left join public.organizations o on o.id = m.org_id\n917:    where a.id = ${ngoTrip.userId}::uuid`;\n918: \n919: const volunteerEmail = address('volunteer-verify');\n920: const volunteerTrip = await verificationRoundTrip(volunteerEmail);\n921: \n922: if (volunteerTrip.accessToken) await fabricateGithubIdentity(volunteerTrip.userId, VOLUNTEER_HANDLE);\n923: \n924: const volunteerCompletion = volunteerTrip.accessToken\n925:   ? await callFunction('complete-signup', volunteerTrip.accessToken, {\n926:       accountType: 'volunteer',\n927:       acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n928:     })\n929:   : { status: 0, body: 'no access token — the volunteer never signed in' };\n930: \n931: const volunteerAccountRows = await sql`select account_type from public.accounts where id = ${volunteerTrip.userId}::uuid`;\n932: const volunteerProfileRows = await sql`\n933:   select github_handle, top_languages, repository_count, contribution_summary, imported_at\n934:     from public.volunteer_profiles where account_id = ${volunteerTrip.userId}::uuid`;\n935: const expectedStats = stubGithubStatsFor(VOLUNTEER_HANDLE);\n936: const volunteerProfile = volunteerProfileRows[0] as\n937:   | { github_handle: string; top_languages: string[]; repository_count: number; contribution_summary: string; imported_at: Date }\n938:   | undefined;\n939: \n940: // THE VOLUNTEER'S PRE-CONFIRMATION REFUSAL GETS THE SAME TEETH AS (c)'s, per the same ruling: a\n941: // claim that the volunteer \"repeats (a)-(d)\" is only true if it is measured the same way.\n942: const volunteerRefusal = unconfirmedSignInRefusal(volunteerTrip.signInBefore);\n943: \n944: record(\n945:   'e',\n946:   'both email-capable types flow end to end under the flipped config: the NGO completes after confirmation, and the volunteer confirms, links GitHub and completes with its profile populated',\n947:   // The NGO half.\n948:   ngoCompletion.status === 200 &&\n949:     ngoRows.length === 1 &&\n950:     ngoRows[0].account_type === 'ngo' &&\n951:     ngoRows[0].name === 'Riverside Shelter' &&\n952:     ngoRows[0].role === 'admin' &&\n953:     // The volunteer half: its OWN round trip repeated (a)-(d), then completion.\n954:     volunteerTrip.signupCarriedSession === false &&\n955:     volunteerTrip.confirmedBefore === null &&\n956:     // THE REFUSAL IS THE CONFIRMATION ONE, not merely a 4xx — the same predicate (c) uses.\n957:     volunteerRefusal.ok &&\n958:     // AND THE LINK CAME FROM THE CATCHER. Without this, the admin-minted fallback could carry the\n959:     // volunteer half while the claim \"repeats (b)\" quietly narrowed to something smaller. The NGO\n960:     // half already cannot do that, because (b) fails with a NARROWED note on the fallback.\n961:     volunteerTrip.linkSource === 'emailed' &&\n962:     volunteerTrip.confirmedAfter !== null &&\n963:     volunteerTrip.shippedVerdict === true &&\n964:     volunteerCompletion.status === 200 &&\n965:     volunteerAccountRows.length === 1 &&\n966:     volunteerAccountRows[0].account_type === 'volunteer' &&\n967:     volunteerProfileRows.length === 1 &&\n968:     volunteerProfile?.github_handle === VOLUNTEER_HANDLE &&\n969:     JSON.stringify(volunteerProfile?.top_languages) === JSON.stringify(expectedStats.topLanguages) &&\n970:     Number(volunteerProfile?.repository_count) === expectedStats.repositoryCount &&\n971:     volunteerProfile?.contribution_summary === expectedStats.contributionSummary,\n972:   `NGO completion HTTP ${ngoCompletion.status} ${redact(ngoCompletion.body)} · rows=${JSON.stringify(ngoRows)}\\n        ` +\n973:     `volunteer round trip: signup carried a session=${volunteerTrip.signupCarriedSession}` +\n974:     ` · confirmed before=${volunteerTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'} after=${volunteerTrip.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n975:     ` · sign-in before confirmation ${volunteerRefusal.discriminator}, redacted body ${redact(volunteerTrip.signInBefore.body)}` +\n976:     ` · link source ${volunteerTrip.linkSource} (${volunteerTrip.linkEvidence})` +\n977:     ` · its own tampered-token probe: attempted=${volunteerTrip.tampered.attempted}` +\n978:     ` email_confirmed_at afterwards=${volunteerTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n979:     ` · shipped emailVerifiedFromUser says ${volunteerTrip.shippedVerdict}\\n        ` +\n980:     `volunteer completion HTTP ${volunteerCompletion.status} ${redact(volunteerCompletion.body)}` +\n981:     ` · account=${JSON.stringify(volunteerAccountRows)} · stored profile=${JSON.stringify(volunteerProfileRows)}` +\n982:     ` · the shipped stub's judgement for \"${VOLUNTEER_HANDLE}\"=${JSON.stringify(expectedStats)}`,\n983: );\n984: \n985: /* =============================================================================================\n986:  * (f) THE MAILER RATE LIMIT — reported either way, so a starved run can never look like a clean\n987:  *     one.\n988:  *\n989:  * `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent` caps auth emails per hour, and this run sends exactly TWO\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-989. Use offset=990 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/**\n * THE LOCAL PROOF for AI4DEV-59 — the only evidence in this item about the real Supabase Auth\n * under `enable_confirmations = true`, the real confirmation email, and the real\n * `/auth/v1/user` serialisation the shipped extractor has to read.\n *\n *   bun loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts\n *\n * Preconditions, and the script refuses rather than guesses if any is missing:\n *   1. `bun run db:start` — the stack is up, WITH this item's `supabase/config.toml`. The flip to\n *      `enable_confirmations = true` is read at Auth start-up, so a stack started before the flip\n *      is running the old setting and every check below would measure the wrong system.\n *   2. `bun run db:reset` — run it immediately before this script. The checks assert row counts.\n *   3. `bunx supabase functions serve` — running in another terminal, FROM THIS WORKTREE, WITHOUT\n *      --no-verify-jwt. The directory matters and is not pedantry: the edge runtime container\n *      mounts whichever project directory launched it, and this machine was once found serving a\n *      worktree that had since been deleted. A stale mount answers preflights perfectly while\n *      running somebody else's code. The script probes the function before using it.\n *\n * IT LIVES IN THE ITEM RECORD AND NOT IN `tests/`, DELIBERATELY, for the reason the predecessor's\n * copy of this file gives: it is evidence-gathering. It runs once, on one machine, by hand, and","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\proof-local.ts","text":"/**\n * THE LOCAL PROOF for AI4DEV-59 — the only evidence in this item about the real Supabase Auth\n * under `enable_confirmations = true`, the real confirmation email, and the real\n * `/auth/v1/user` serialisation the shipped extractor has to read.\n *\n *   bun loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts\n *\n * Preconditions, and the script refuses rather than guesses if any is missing:\n *   1. `bun run db:start` — the stack is up, WITH this item's `supabase/config.toml`. The flip to\n *      `enable_confirmations = true` is read at Auth start-up, so a stack started before the flip\n *      is running the old setting and every check below would measure the wrong system.\n *   2. `bun run db:reset` — run it immediately before this script. The checks assert row counts.\n *   3. `bunx supabase functions serve` — running in another terminal, FROM THIS WORKTREE, WITHOUT\n *      --no-verify-jwt. The directory matters and is not pedantry: the edge runtime container\n *      mounts whichever project directory launched it, and this machine was once found serving a\n *      worktree that had since been deleted. A stale mount answers preflights perfectly while\n *      running somebody else's code. The script probes the function before using it.\n *\n * IT LIVES IN THE ITEM RECORD AND NOT IN `tests/`, DELIBERATELY, for the reason the predecessor's\n * copy of this file gives: it is evidence-gathering. It runs once, on one machine, by hand, and\n * its transcript is the artifact. Under `tests/` it would look like something that guards the\n * tree, and it guards nothing — nothing runs it again and CI has no database.\n *\n * WHAT THE LOOP-TIER SUITE ALREADY PROVES, so this does not duplicate it: that the shipped\n * decisions in `_shared/verification.ts` behave as AT-001.09 and AT-001.10 require, including the\n * fail-closed answers, which `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` drives directly.\n * What NOTHING BUT THIS SCRIPT touches: that GoTrue issues no session at signup with confirmations\n * on, that it refuses sign-in until the address is confirmed, that a confirmation email really\n * arrives and its link really flips `email_confirmed_at`, and that the shipped extractor answers\n * true against GoTrue's REAL serialisation rather than against the fixture's prediction of it.\n *\n * ============================================================================================\n * NO CHECK HERE EXERCISES `discoveryMessageAllowed`, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.\n * ============================================================================================\n *\n * There is no Discovery route on this stack or on any stack — it is REQ-002/004's, and this leaf\n * ships the decision that route must call rather than the route. So there is nothing live to send\n * a Discovery message to. The gate's oracle is the loop-tier body for AT-001.10 plus the direct\n * shape selftest, and this transcript says so instead of leaving a reader to assume a live check\n * happened. What this script DOES add on that subject is check (c): on the live stack an\n * unconfirmed user cannot authenticate at all, so the auth layer blocks upstream of the gate.\n *\n * NO KEY IS WRITTEN INTO THIS FILE AND NONE IS PRINTED BY IT. Everything is read from\n * `bunx supabase status -o json` at run time. Redaction is DESIGNED IN rather than applied\n * afterwards, and it covers THREE surfaces, because gate 2 found the first version covering only\n * one:\n *\n *   1. BODIES. `redact()` below walks every object body before it reaches the transcript and\n *      replaces the value under any credential-shaped key name.\n *   2. STRINGS, including a body that is not JSON at all. `redact()` also scrubs JWT-shaped\n *      substrings and `name=value` pairs whose name is credential-shaped, so a raw string answer\n *      cannot carry a token through verbatim.\n *   3. THE REDIRECT LOCATION. `redactedLocation()` cuts the URL fragment — that fragment is where\n *      GoTrue puts the access token it mints when a link is followed — AND replaces EVERY\n *      query-parameter VALUE, keeping the parameter names. A `?token=` or `?code=` redirect can\n *      therefore not land in a file that gets pushed.\n *\n * GitHub push protection has already refused one push in this project for a transcript that\n * captured a status command verbatim. The transcript is ALSO inspected for credential residue by\n * hand before it is committed; that inspection is recorded in the item's report.\n */\n\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { SQL } from 'bun';\n// THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR, IMPORTED RATHER THAN MIRRORED — the whole point of check (d). The\n// question this script exists to settle is whether the function that ships answers correctly\n// about a REAL GoTrue response, so re-implementing the read here would settle nothing.\nimport { emailVerifiedFromUser } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';\n// The stub import fixture, for the volunteer completion read-back in (e) — the same reason the\n// predecessor imports it: comparing the stored profile against the shipped judgement shows the\n// values travelled from the edge function into the transaction unchanged, where \"the row is\n// non-empty\" would pass over plausible-looking rubbish.\nimport { stubGithubStatsFor } from '../../../supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts';\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ reporting machinery */\n\ntype Outcome = 'pass' | 'fail' | 'skip';\ntype Check = { id: string; title: string; outcome: Outcome; note: string };\n\nconst results: Check[] = [];\n\nfunction record(id: string, title: string, passed: boolean, note: string): void {\n  results.push({ id, title, outcome: passed ? 'pass' : 'fail', note });\n  console.log(`${passed ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}  (${id}) ${title}\\n        ${note}`);\n}\n\n/**\n * A check that could not be attempted. Never a pass and never a failure — a skip says the evidence\n * is MISSING, which is a different statement from the claim being false, and both differ from the\n * claim being proved.\n */\nfunction skip(id: string, title: string, why: string): void {\n  results.push({ id, title, outcome: 'skip', note: `SKIPPED — ${why}` });\n  console.log(`SKIP  (${id}) ${title}\\n        ${why}`);\n}\n\nfunction fail(message: string): never {\n  console.error(`\\nPRECONDITION FAILED: ${message}`);\n  process.exit(2);\n}\n\n/**\n * EVERY BODY GOES THROUGH THIS BEFORE IT REACHES THE TRANSCRIPT.\n *\n * The fields that carry secrets are known and few — GoTrue answers with `access_token`,\n * `refresh_token`, `provider_token` and friends — but the rule here is by NAME PATTERN rather than\n * by a list, because a list is a thing to forget to extend. A value under any key whose name looks\n * like a credential is replaced; the KEY still appears, so the transcript still shows that the\n * field was present, which is usually the fact a check needs.\n *\n * It returns a STRING, not an object, so there is no path by which an unredacted value reaches the\n * output through some later `JSON.stringify`.\n */\nconst SENSITIVE = /token|secret|password|apikey|api_key|jwt|nonce|otp|code$/i;\n\n/**\n * A STRING SCRUBBED OF CREDENTIALS, for the surface `redact()`'s key walk cannot see.\n *\n * A key walk protects `{\"access_token\": \"...\"}`. It protects nothing inside a value — and GoTrue\n * answers with raw strings on some paths, while any body at all can carry a link with a token in\n * its query. So two shapes are replaced here:\n *\n *   - A JWT. Three base64url runs separated by dots, beginning `eyJ`, which is what a JSON header\n *     always encodes to. Every access token, refresh token and service key in this stack is one.\n *   - A `name=value` pair whose NAME is credential-shaped by the same pattern the key walk uses.\n *     The name survives, so the transcript still shows which parameter was present.\n */\nfunction scrubString(text: string): string {\n  return text\n    .replace(/eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/g, '<redacted-jwt>')\n    .replace(/([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)=([^&\\s\"'<>]+)/g, (whole, name: string) =>\n      SENSITIVE.test(name) ? `${name}=<redacted>` : whole,\n    );\n}\n\nfunction redact(value: unknown): string {\n  const walk = (node: unknown): unknown => {\n    if (Array.isArray(node)) return node.map(walk);\n    if (node !== null && typeof node === 'object') {\n      const out: Record<string, unknown> = {};\n      for (const [key, inner] of Object.entries(node as Record<string, unknown>)) {\n        out[key] = SENSITIVE.test(key) ? '<redacted>' : walk(inner);\n      }\n      return out;\n    }\n    // A STRING VALUE IS SCRUBBED TOO. This is the half gate 2 found missing: a body that is not\n    // JSON reaches here as one long string, and a key walk never looks inside it.\n    if (typeof node === 'string') return scrubString(node);\n    return node;\n  };\n  try {\n    return JSON.stringify(walk(value));\n  } catch {\n    return '<unserialisable>';\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * A URL SAFE TO PRINT: no fragment, and no query-parameter VALUES.\n *\n * The fragment is where GoTrue puts the minted access token. The QUERY is where it puts the\n * token hash, the `code`, and the error description — and printing those unchanged was the defect\n * two blind reviewers found independently. Parameter NAMES are kept, because which parameters a\n * redirect carried is usually the fact a check needs, and a name is not a credential.\n */\nfunction redactedLocation(url: string): string {\n  if (url === '') return '';\n  const hash = url.indexOf('#');\n  const fragmentNote = hash < 0 ? '' : '#<redacted-fragment>';\n  const withoutHash = hash < 0 ? url : url.slice(0, hash);\n\n  const question = withoutHash.indexOf('?');\n  if (question < 0) return `${withoutHash}${fragmentNote}`;\n\n  const query = withoutHash\n    .slice(question + 1)\n    .split('&')\n    .map((pair) => {\n      const equals = pair.indexOf('=');\n      // A bare flag has no value to redact.\n      return equals < 0 ? pair : `${pair.slice(0, equals)}=<redacted>`;\n    })\n    .join('&');\n  return `${withoutHash.slice(0, question)}?${query}${fragmentNote}`;\n}\n\n/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the environment */\n\nconst status = await (async () => {\n  const proc = Bun.spawnSync(['bunx', 'supabase', 'status', '-o', 'json'], { stdout: 'pipe', stderr: 'pipe' });\n  const text = new TextDecoder().decode(proc.stdout);\n  const start = text.indexOf('{');\n  // The raw text is NOT included in this message, deliberately: it is the whole status payload and\n  // it carries key material. A failure here says what to do, not what it read.\n  if (start < 0) fail('could not read `supabase status -o json` — is the stack up? (bun run db:start)');\n  return JSON.parse(text.slice(start)) as Record<string, string>;\n})();\n\nconst API_URL = status.API_URL ?? fail('supabase status reported no API_URL');\nconst DB_URL = status.DB_URL ?? fail('supabase status reported no DB_URL');\n/** The browser's key. Everything a signed-out or signed-in visitor could do uses this and no other. */\nconst PUBLIC_KEY = status.ANON_KEY ?? status.PUBLISHABLE_KEY ?? fail('supabase status reported no client key');\n/** The server's key. Used ONLY where the check is about an authority the public never holds. */\nconst SECRET_KEY = status.SERVICE_ROLE_KEY ?? status.SECRET_KEY ?? fail('supabase status reported no service key');\n/**\n * The local mail catcher. Its port is 54324 in `supabase/config.toml`, and its API SHAPE is\n * measured below rather than assumed — see `discoverMailCatcher`.\n */\nconst MAIL_URL = status.MAILPIT_URL ?? status.INBUCKET_URL ?? 'http://127.0.0.1:54324';\n\nconst sql = new SQL(DB_URL);\n\nconst TEXT_VERSION = 'tos-2026-01+promise-2026-01';\nconst PASSWORD = 'correct horse battery staple';\nconst CLIENT_IP = '203.0.113.7';\nconst VOLUNTEER_HANDLE = 'octoverifier';\n/** Unique per run, so a second run against a database that was not reset still reads honestly. */\nconst RUN = Date.now().toString(36);\nconst address = (local: string) => `${local}+${RUN}@example.test`;\n\n/**\n * The configured per-hour cap on auth emails, read from the file rather than remembered.\n *\n * This run sends TWO confirmation emails — one per email-capable account type — and the checked-in\n * value is 2, so the file's cap sits EXACTLY on what this run sends — which is why check (f)\n * exists and why the plan pre-authorized raising it.\n *\n * MEASURED ON THE FIRST RUN: the CLI did NOT push this file value into the auth container — the\n * container carried GOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000, recorded in stack-up.txt beside the\n * reading — so the file's cap did not bind that run. Check (f) still reports the file's value AND\n * whether any mailer refusal appeared, which together are the honest pair: the transcript shows\n * what the file claims and what the mailer actually did.\n */\nconst CONFIGURED_EMAIL_RATE_LIMIT = (() => {\n  try {\n    const toml = readFileSync(new URL('../../../supabase/config.toml', import.meta.url), 'utf8');\n    return /^\\s*email_sent\\s*=\\s*(\\d+)/m.exec(toml)?.[1] ?? '<not found>';\n  } catch {\n    return '<unreadable>';\n  }\n})();\n\n/**\n * Anything that looks like the mailer refusing to send. Check (f) reports on this, so a starved\n * run can never look like a clean one.\n */\nconst rateLimitEvidence: string[] = [];\n\nfunction noteIfRateLimited(where: string, httpStatus: number, body: unknown): void {\n  const text = typeof body === 'string' ? body : JSON.stringify(body ?? null);\n  if (httpStatus === 429 || /rate.?limit|over_email_send/i.test(text)) {\n    rateLimitEvidence.push(`${where}: HTTP ${httpStatus} ${redact(body)}`);\n  }\n}\n\n/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Auth calls */\n\ntype Wire = { status: number; body: unknown };\n\nasync function authPost(path: string, body: unknown, key = PUBLIC_KEY): Promise<Wire> {\n  const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}${path}`, {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', apikey: key, Authorization: `Bearer ${key}` },\n    body: JSON.stringify(body),\n  });\n  const text = await response.text();\n  let parsed: unknown = text;\n  try {\n    parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n  } catch {\n    // A non-JSON answer is itself evidence; it is kept as the raw string.\n  }\n  return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n}\n\n/**\n * Register an email/password user. WITH CONFIRMATIONS ON THIS MUST RETURN NO SESSION, which is\n * check (a)'s first half, so the caller inspects the wire response rather than being handed a\n * tidy session object.\n *\n * THE USER ID IS TAKEN FROM THE RESPONSE WHEN IT IS THERE, AND FROM THE DATABASE WHEN IT IS NOT.\n * GoTrue's confirmations-on signup response has varied across versions — some return the full user\n * record, some return an obfuscated one to avoid leaking whether an address already exists. Which\n * one this stack does is recorded rather than assumed, and neither answer is a failure: the id is\n * an operator read either way.\n */\nasync function signUp(email: string): Promise<{ wire: Wire; userId: string; idFrom: 'response' | 'database' }> {\n  const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/signup', { email, password: PASSWORD });\n  noteIfRateLimited(`signup ${email}`, wire.status, wire.body);\n  if (wire.status >= 400) fail(`sign-up for ${email} failed (HTTP ${wire.status}): ${redact(wire.body)}`);\n\n  const body = wire.body as { id?: string; user?: { id?: string } } | null;\n  const fromResponse = body?.user?.id ?? body?.id;\n  if (typeof fromResponse === 'string' && fromResponse !== '') {\n    return { wire, userId: fromResponse, idFrom: 'response' };\n  }\n  const rows = await sql`select id from auth.users where email = ${email}`;\n  if (rows.length !== 1) fail(`sign-up for ${email} left ${rows.length} rows in auth.users — expected exactly 1`);\n  return { wire, userId: String(rows[0].id), idFrom: 'database' };\n}\n\n/** Sign in with a password. Returns the wire answer AND the token, which is never printed. */\nasync function signIn(email: string): Promise<{ wire: Wire; accessToken: string | null }> {\n  const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/token?grant_type=password', { email, password: PASSWORD });\n  const token = (wire.body as { access_token?: string } | null)?.access_token;\n  return { wire, accessToken: typeof token === 'string' && token !== '' ? token : null };\n}\n\n/**\n * GoTrue's error code for \"this address is not confirmed yet\".\n *\n * It is the DISCRIMINATOR check (c) needs, and it is pinned as a constant so the transcript states\n * which value was expected. If the live stack answers a different code, the check FAILS and the\n * verbatim capture beside it shows the real one — measurement, never assumption.\n */\nconst CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE = 'email_not_confirmed';\n\n/**\n * IS THIS THE CONFIRMATION REFUSAL, or merely A refusal — the distinction gate 2 found missing.\n *\n * `status >= 400` alone passes on a 429 from the mailer's rate limiter and on a 500 from a broken\n * stack, and either would record \"confirmation is enforced\" while confirmation enforced nothing.\n * So a pass needs BOTH halves:\n *\n *   - a 4xx that is NOT 429 — a client refusal, not a throttle and not a server fault;\n *   - a body that NAMES the unconfirmed state — the pinned error code, or `confirm` in the\n *     message, which is the weaker initial predicate the ruling allows.\n *\n * IT READS THE IN-MEMORY BODY, NOT THE PRINTED ONE, and that is load-bearing: the transcript\n * redactor's key pattern ends with `code$`, so `error_code` is blanked in everything it prints.\n * Judging the printed text would judge `<redacted>`. The discriminator is surfaced separately\n * below through a safe extraction — an error code is an enum value, not a credential.\n */\nfunction unconfirmedSignInRefusal(wire: Wire): { ok: boolean; discriminator: string } {\n  const body =\n    wire.body !== null && typeof wire.body === 'object'\n      ? (wire.body as Record<string, unknown>)\n      : {};\n  const errorCode = typeof body.error_code === 'string' ? body.error_code : '';\n  const message = [body.msg, body.message, body.error_description, body.error, wire.body]\n    .filter((part): part is string => typeof part === 'string')\n    .join(' | ');\n\n  const statusIsClientRefusal = wire.status >= 400 && wire.status < 500 && wire.status !== 429;\n  const bodyNamesTheState = errorCode === CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE || /confirm/i.test(message);\n\n  return {\n    ok: statusIsClientRefusal && bodyNamesTheState,\n    discriminator:\n      `HTTP ${wire.status} · error_code=${errorCode === '' ? '<absent>' : errorCode}` +\n      ` (expected ${CONFIRMATION_ERROR_CODE}) · message=${JSON.stringify(scrubString(message) || '<absent>')}`,\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * THE SAME LINK WITH A TOKEN GOTRUE NEVER ISSUED — check (b2)'s instrument.\n *\n * The mirror under test says a link that was never issued confirms nothing, and following a\n * MUTATED token is how that is measured without touching any semantics REQ-001 does not state.\n * The token this returns was never minted, so following it says nothing about expiry, about single\n * use, or about resend — retired AT-001.11 stays untouched. Every other part of the link, the\n * `type` and the redirect included, is left exactly as GoTrue wrote it, so the only difference\n * between this request and the real one is the token itself.\n *\n * The mutation shifts digits and letters inside their own alphabet, which keeps the token the same\n * length and shape. A token that changed shape could be refused for being malformed rather than\n * for being unissued, and the check would then measure input validation instead of the mirror.\n */\nfunction tamperVerificationToken(link: string): { tampered: string; parameter: string } | null {\n  let url: URL;\n  try {\n    url = new URL(link);\n  } catch {\n    return null;\n  }\n  const parameter = ['token', 'token_hash', 'confirmation_token'].find((name) => {\n    const value = url.searchParams.get(name);\n    return typeof value === 'string' && value !== '';\n  });\n  if (parameter === undefined) return null;\n\n  const original = url.searchParams.get(parameter) as string;\n  const mutated = original.replace(/[0-9a-zA-Z]/g, (character) => {\n    if (character >= '0' && character <= '9') return String((Number(character) + 5) % 10);\n    const base = character >= 'a' ? 'a'.charCodeAt(0) : 'A'.charCodeAt(0);\n    return String.fromCharCode(base + ((character.charCodeAt(0) - base + 13) % 26));\n  });\n  if (mutated === original) return null;\n\n  url.searchParams.set(parameter, mutated);\n  return { tampered: url.toString(), parameter };\n}\n\n/** `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` read as the operator, on port 54322 — not through any API. */\nasync function confirmedAt(userId: string): Promise<string | null> {\n  const rows = await sql`select email_confirmed_at from auth.users where id = ${userId}::uuid`;\n  const value = rows[0]?.email_confirmed_at ?? null;\n  return value === null ? null : String(value instanceof Date ? value.toISOString() : value);\n}\n\n/**\n * THE POST-LINK AUTH STATE FOR GITHUB, FABRICATED BY OPERATOR AUTHORITY — carried over from the\n * predecessor item unchanged, including the correction that cost it real time.\n *\n * Linking a GitHub identity is an OAuth consent round trip: a person at github.com and a redirect\n * no script can drive. What linking LEAVES BEHIND is a row in `auth.identities`, and that row is\n * what the shipped code reads. So the row is written directly, as the operator. IT IS\n * FLIP-INDEPENDENT — a database write, not an auth flow — which is why (e) can reuse it under the\n * new setting without re-proving it.\n *\n * `created_at`, `updated_at` and `last_sign_in_at` are NULLABLE in the table, so a row without them\n * inserts happily; but GoTrue reads identities into a Go struct with non-pointer timestamps, so\n * `GET /auth/v1/user` then answers 500 for that user, and the whole thing looks exactly like an\n * expired token rather than like a malformed row three layers away. So they are filled here.\n */\nasync function fabricateGithubIdentity(userId: string, handle: string): Promise<void> {\n  await sql`\n    insert into auth.identities (id, user_id, provider, provider_id, identity_data,\n                                 created_at, updated_at, last_sign_in_at)\n    values (\n      gen_random_uuid(),\n      ${userId}::uuid,\n      'github',\n      ${`gh-${handle}-${RUN}`},\n      -- The casts are explicit because jsonb_build_object takes \"any\", so PostgreSQL has nothing to\n      -- infer a parameter's type from and refuses the statement outright rather than guessing.\n      jsonb_build_object('sub', ${userId}::text, 'user_name', ${handle}::text, 'provider_id', ${`gh-${handle}-${RUN}`}::text),\n      now(), now(), now()\n    )`;\n}\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------ the edge function */\n\nasync function callFunction(name: string, accessToken: string, body: unknown): Promise<Wire> {\n  const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/functions/v1/${name}`, {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: {\n      'content-type': 'application/json',\n      apikey: PUBLIC_KEY,\n      Authorization: `Bearer ${accessToken}`,\n      'x-forwarded-for': CLIENT_IP,\n    },\n    body: JSON.stringify(body),\n  });\n  const text = await response.text();\n  let parsed: unknown = text;\n  try {\n    parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n  } catch {\n    /* kept raw */\n  }\n  return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n}\n\n// The stale-mount hazard the header names: probe the function before any check depends on it. An\n// unauthenticated POST must be REFUSED by something, and a connection error is a different failure\n// from a refusal. Only the second means the function is being served.\n//\n// THE PROBE GUARDS REACHABILITY, NOT REVISION, AND (e)'s EVIDENCE DOES NOT NEED IT TO.\n// A reviewer read this as a hole: a stale mount serving an older worktree answers 401, so any\n// non-404 passes and the probe cannot tell which REVISION of `complete-signup` replied. The fact is\n// right; the consequence is not, and the reason was verified in the tree before this comment was\n// written. This branch changes NOTHING under `supabase/functions/` except the NEW\n// `_shared/verification.ts` (`git diff main...HEAD --stat -- supabase/functions/`: one file, 167\n// insertions), and `complete-signup/index.ts` imports only `_shared/accounts.ts`,\n// `_shared/github.ts` and `_shared/edge.ts` — never `verification.ts`. So every revision of\n// `complete-signup` this branch could be compared against is byte for byte the same code, and the\n// mount's revision is not load-bearing for anything (e) claims.\n// The operator side of the binding is recorded rather than inferred:\n// `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/stack-up.txt` carries the exact `bunx supabase functions serve` command and\n// the directory it ran in.\nawait (async () => {\n  try {\n    const probe = await fetch(`${API_URL}/functions/v1/complete-signup`, {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', apikey: PUBLIC_KEY },\n      body: '{}',\n    });\n    if (probe.status === 404) {\n      fail('the edge runtime answered 404 for complete-signup — `bunx supabase functions serve` is not serving THIS worktree');\n    }\n  } catch (error) {\n    fail(\n      'could not reach the edge runtime at all — run `bunx supabase functions serve` from THIS worktree ' +\n        `(${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)})`,\n    );\n  }\n})();\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- the mail catcher, MEASURED */\n\ntype CatcherKind = 'mailpit' | 'inbucket' | 'unknown';\n\n/**\n * WHICH MAIL CATCHER IS BEHIND PORT 54324, established by asking it rather than by remembering.\n *\n * The Supabase CLI has shipped Inbucket and, more recently, Mailpit, and their APIs are not the\n * same shape. The plan's risk 2 is exactly this. So both are probed, BOTH ANSWERS GO IN THE\n * TRANSCRIPT, and the extraction below branches on what actually replied. An assumption here would\n * fail as \"no confirmation email arrived\", which is indistinguishable from the mailer being\n * rate-limited — the single most misleading way this script could go wrong.\n */\nasync function discoverMailCatcher(): Promise<{ kind: CatcherKind; evidence: string }> {\n  const probe = async (path: string): Promise<string> => {\n    try {\n      const response = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}${path}`);\n      const text = (await response.text()).slice(0, 200);\n      return `GET ${path} -> HTTP ${response.status} ${JSON.stringify(text)}`;\n    } catch (error) {\n      return `GET ${path} -> unreachable (${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)})`;\n    }\n  };\n\n  const mailpit = await probe('/api/v1/info');\n  const inbucket = await probe('/api/v1/mailbox/probe');\n  const evidence = `catcher at ${MAIL_URL} · ${mailpit} · ${inbucket}`;\n\n  if (/HTTP 200/.test(mailpit)) return { kind: 'mailpit', evidence };\n  if (/HTTP 200/.test(inbucket)) return { kind: 'inbucket', evidence };\n  return { kind: 'unknown', evidence };\n}\n\nconst catcher = await discoverMailCatcher();\nconsole.log(`\\nmail catcher shape, measured: ${catcher.kind}\\n        ${catcher.evidence}\\n`);\n\n/**\n * Every URL in the message that looks like GoTrue's verify endpoint.\n *\n * `&amp;` IS DECODED FIRST, and that is not cosmetic: the confirmation email's HTML part escapes\n * the ampersands, so a link lifted straight out of it carries `&amp;type=signup` and GoTrue reads\n * the parameter name as `amp;type`. The failure looks like a rejected link rather than like a\n * mangled one.\n */\nfunction verifyLinksIn(text: string): string[] {\n  const decoded = text.replace(/&amp;/g, '&');\n  return decoded.match(/https?:\\/\\/[^\\s\"'<>)\\]]+\\/auth\\/v1\\/verify[^\\s\"'<>)\\]]*/g) ?? [];\n}\n\nasync function emailedVerificationLink(email: string): Promise<{ link: string | null; evidence: string }> {\n  const local = email.split('@')[0];\n  try {\n    if (catcher.kind === 'mailpit') {\n      const list = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/search?query=${encodeURIComponent(`to:${email}`)}&limit=20`);\n      const listed = (await list.json()) as { messages?: Array<{ ID?: string }> };\n      const id = listed.messages?.[0]?.ID;\n      if (!id) return { link: null, evidence: `mailpit search for ${email} returned no message` };\n      const message = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/message/${id}`);\n      const parsed = (await message.json()) as { Text?: string; HTML?: string };\n      const links = verifyLinksIn(`${parsed.Text ?? ''}\\n${parsed.HTML ?? ''}`);\n      return {\n        link: links[0] ?? null,\n        evidence: `mailpit held ${listed.messages?.length ?? 0} message(s) for ${email}; ${links.length} verify link(s) found`,\n      };\n    }\n    if (catcher.kind === 'inbucket') {\n      const list = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/mailbox/${encodeURIComponent(local)}`);\n      const listed = (await list.json()) as Array<{ id?: string }>;\n      const id = listed[0]?.id;\n      if (!id) return { link: null, evidence: `inbucket mailbox ${local} was empty` };\n      const message = await fetch(`${MAIL_URL}/api/v1/mailbox/${encodeURIComponent(local)}/${id}`);\n      const parsed = (await message.json()) as { body?: { text?: string; html?: string } };\n      const links = verifyLinksIn(`${parsed.body?.text ?? ''}\\n${parsed.body?.html ?? ''}`);\n      return {\n        link: links[0] ?? null,\n        evidence: `inbucket mailbox ${local} held ${listed.length} message(s); ${links.length} verify link(s) found`,\n      };\n    }\n    return { link: null, evidence: `the catcher at ${MAIL_URL} matched no known API shape` };\n  } catch (error) {\n    return { link: null, evidence: `reading the catcher failed: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}` };\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * THE FALLBACK THE PLAN'S RISK 2 PRE-AUTHORIZES, and it NARROWS the claim rather than rescuing it.\n *\n * GoTrue's admin endpoint mints a verification link with operator authority. If the catcher cannot\n * yield the emailed link, this is what gets followed — and the transcript then says, in the check's\n * own note, that the link followed was NOT the emailed one. That is a smaller claim, stated as a\n * smaller claim. It is never used when the catcher works.\n */\nasync function adminGeneratedLink(email: string): Promise<{ link: string | null; evidence: string }> {\n  const wire = await authPost('/auth/v1/admin/generate_link', { type: 'signup', email, password: PASSWORD }, SECRET_KEY);\n  const link = (wire.body as { action_link?: string } | null)?.action_link ?? null;\n  return {\n    link: typeof link === 'string' && link !== '' ? link : null,\n    evidence: `admin generate_link -> HTTP ${wire.status} (action_link ${link ? 'present' : 'absent'})`,\n  };\n}\n\n/* ============================================================================================\n * THE VERIFICATION ROUND TRIP — (a) through (d), as one function so the SECOND address in (e)\n * repeats exactly the same measurement rather than an abbreviated version of it.\n * ============================================================================================ */\n\n/**\n * What following a tampered link did — or why it could not be followed at all.\n *\n * `attempted: false` is never a pass. Check (b2) SKIPS on it, because a check that could not run\n * is a missing measurement rather than a satisfied one.\n */\ntype TamperedFollow = {\n  attempted: boolean;\n  why: string;\n  parameter: string;\n  status: number;\n  location: string;\n  confirmedAfter: string | null;\n};\n\ntype RoundTrip = {\n  email: string;\n  userId: string;\n  idFrom: 'response' | 'database';\n  signupWire: Wire;\n  /** the whole point of (a): with confirmations on there must be NO token in the signup answer */\n  signupCarriedSession: boolean;\n  confirmedBefore: string | null;\n  linkSource: 'emailed' | 'admin-generated' | 'none';\n  linkEvidence: string;\n  signInBefore: Wire;\n  /** check (b2): the never-issued-token negative, measured BEFORE the real link is followed */\n  tampered: TamperedFollow;\n  followStatus: number;\n  followLocation: string;\n  confirmedAfter: string | null;\n  signInAfter: Wire;\n  accessToken: string | null;\n  rawUserWire: Wire;\n  shippedVerdict: boolean;\n};\n\nasync function verificationRoundTrip(email: string): Promise<RoundTrip> {\n  const { wire: signupWire, userId, idFrom } = await signUp(email);\n  const signupBody = signupWire.body as { access_token?: unknown; session?: unknown } | null;\n  const signupCarriedSession =\n    typeof signupBody?.access_token === 'string' || (signupBody?.session !== null && signupBody?.session !== undefined);\n\n  const confirmedBefore = await confirmedAt(userId);\n\n  // (c) is measured BEFORE the link is followed, because afterwards the same call succeeds and the\n  // refusal would be unobtainable.\n  const signInBefore = (await signIn(email)).wire;\n\n  const fromCatcher = await emailedVerificationLink(email);\n  let link = fromCatcher.link;\n  let linkSource: RoundTrip['linkSource'] = link ? 'emailed' : 'none';\n  let linkEvidence = fromCatcher.evidence;\n  if (!link) {\n    const fallback = await adminGeneratedLink(email);\n    if (fallback.link) {\n      link = fallback.link;\n      linkSource = 'admin-generated';\n    }\n    linkEvidence = `${fromCatcher.evidence} · FALLBACK: ${fallback.evidence}`;\n  }\n\n  // (b2) THE NEVER-ISSUED TOKEN, FOLLOWED BEFORE THE REAL LINK.\n  //\n  // The order matters and is the whole design of this block: once the real link has confirmed the\n  // address, `email_confirmed_at` is non-null for every later read, and the negative becomes\n  // unmeasurable. So the tampered variant goes first, and the column is read straight afterwards.\n  const tampered: TamperedFollow = {\n    attempted: false,\n    why: '',\n    parameter: '',\n    status: 0,\n    location: '',\n    confirmedAfter: null,\n  };\n  if (!link) {\n    tampered.why = 'no verification link was obtained at all, so there was nothing to tamper with';\n  } else {\n    const mutation = tamperVerificationToken(link);\n    if (mutation === null) {\n      tampered.why = 'the link carries no non-empty token parameter this script knows how to mutate';\n    } else {\n      const followed = await fetch(mutation.tampered, { redirect: 'manual' });\n      tampered.attempted = true;\n      tampered.parameter = mutation.parameter;\n      tampered.status = followed.status;\n      tampered.location = redactedLocation(followed.headers.get('location') ?? '');\n      tampered.confirmedAfter = await confirmedAt(userId);\n    }\n  }\n\n  let followStatus = 0;\n  let followLocation = '';\n  if (link) {\n    const followed = await fetch(link, { redirect: 'manual' });\n    followStatus = followed.status;\n    // THE FRAGMENT IS CUT AND EVERY QUERY VALUE IS REPLACED. GoTrue redirects to the site URL with\n    // the freshly minted access token in the URL fragment, and this string goes into a file that\n    // gets pushed.\n    followLocation = redactedLocation(followed.headers.get('location') ?? '');\n  }\n\n  const confirmedAfter = await confirmedAt(userId);\n  const after = await signIn(email);\n  const rawUserWire: Wire = after.accessToken\n    ? await (async () => {\n        const response = await fetch(`${API_URL}/auth/v1/user`, {\n          headers: { apikey: PUBLIC_KEY, Authorization: `Bearer ${after.accessToken}` },\n        });\n        const text = await response.text();\n        let parsed: unknown = text;\n        try {\n          parsed = JSON.parse(text);\n        } catch {\n          /* kept raw */\n        }\n        return { status: response.status, body: parsed };\n      })()\n    : { status: 0, body: null };\n\n  return {\n    email,\n    userId,\n    idFrom,\n    signupWire,\n    signupCarriedSession,\n    confirmedBefore,\n    linkSource,\n    linkEvidence,\n    signInBefore,\n    tampered,\n    followStatus,\n    followLocation,\n    confirmedAfter,\n    signInAfter: after.wire,\n    accessToken: after.accessToken,\n    rawUserWire,\n    // THE SHIPPED FUNCTION, ON THE REAL BODY. This single line is what binds the extractor to\n    // GoTrue's actual serialisation; everything else in this script is the setup that makes it\n    // meaningful.\n    shippedVerdict: emailVerifiedFromUser(rawUserWire.body),\n  };\n}\n\nconst ngoEmail = address('ngo-verify');\nconst ngoTrip = await verificationRoundTrip(ngoEmail);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (a) SIGNUP UNDER THE FLIPPED CONFIG ISSUES NO SESSION, AND THE ADDRESS IS UNCONFIRMED.\n *\n * The first of the plan's three unverified-runtime-claims, measured rather than expected. Both\n * halves matter: no token on the wire, AND `email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read as the operator on\n * port 54322. The wire answer alone could be a client-library convention; the column is the fact.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nrecord(\n  'a',\n  'an email/password signup under enable_confirmations = true returns NO session, and auth.users.email_confirmed_at is NULL',\n  ngoTrip.signupWire.status < 400 && ngoTrip.signupCarriedSession === false && ngoTrip.confirmedBefore === null,\n  `signup HTTP ${ngoTrip.signupWire.status} ${redact(ngoTrip.signupWire.body)}` +\n    ` · carried a session: ${ngoTrip.signupCarriedSession}` +\n    ` · user id read from the ${ngoTrip.idFrom}` +\n    ` · auth.users.email_confirmed_at = ${ngoTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'}`,\n);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (b) THE CONFIRMATION EMAIL EXISTS IN THE CATCHER, AND THE LINK COMES OUT OF IT.\n *\n * The catcher's API shape was measured above, not assumed — the evidence line says which shape\n * answered. If the link came from the admin endpoint instead, this check says so IN ITS OWN NOTE\n * and the claim is narrower: an equivalent link was followed, but not the emailed one.\n *\n * AN UNRECOGNISED CATCHER SKIPS RATHER THAN FAILS, and the distinction is the shared invariant\n * about re-measuring a negative: a broken instrument and a true absence produce identical output.\n * \"The mailbox reads empty\" is not evidence that no email was sent when the thing being asked is\n * not the thing that answers. A skip says the evidence is missing, which is what is true.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nif (catcher.kind === 'unknown') {\n  skip(\n    'b',\n    'the local mail catcher holds a confirmation email for the address, and the verification link is extracted from it',\n    `the catcher at ${MAIL_URL} matched no known API shape, so this check has no working instrument. ` +\n      `It is NOT evidence that no confirmation email was sent — a broken reader and an empty mailbox look the same. ` +\n      `Probe results: ${catcher.evidence}. Fix the reader, or note which catcher this CLI version ships, then re-run.`,\n  );\n} else {\n  record(\n    'b',\n    'the local mail catcher holds a confirmation email for the address, and the verification link is extracted from it',\n    ngoTrip.linkSource === 'emailed',\n    `catcher shape: ${catcher.kind} · ${ngoTrip.linkEvidence} · link source: ${ngoTrip.linkSource}` +\n      (ngoTrip.linkSource === 'admin-generated'\n        ? ' — NARROWED: the EMAILED link was not what was followed; an operator-minted equivalent was.'\n        : ''),\n  );\n}\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (b2) A TOKEN GOTRUE NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.\n *\n * The fixture's mirror 2 says a link that was never issued has no confirming effect. Two blind\n * reviewers found the same defect: that mirror was labelled BOUND by checks (a)–(d), and none of\n * those checks ever follows an unissued link — the named evidence measured only the positive half.\n * This check is the negative, measured.\n *\n * The instrument is the REAL link with its token mutated inside its own alphabet, followed BEFORE\n * the real one. What it asserts is exactly one thing: `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` is still\n * NULL afterwards. It asserts NOTHING about expiry, single use or resend — the token followed was\n * never minted, so no lifetime and no use count is in play, and retired AT-001.11's semantics stay\n * untouched.\n *\n * THE RESPONSE STATUS IS RECORDED BUT NOT ASSERTED ON. GoTrue may answer a redirect carrying an\n * error, or a 4xx, and which one it picks is its own business. The column is the fact.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nif (!ngoTrip.tampered.attempted) {\n  skip(\n    'b2',\n    'a verification link carrying a token GoTrue never issued confirms nothing',\n    `${ngoTrip.tampered.why}. Link source was \"${ngoTrip.linkSource}\" (${ngoTrip.linkEvidence}). ` +\n      'This is NOT evidence that a never-issued token confirms an address — it is evidence that the ' +\n      'negative could not be measured on this run.',\n  );\n} else {\n  record(\n    'b2',\n    'a verification link carrying a token GoTrue never issued confirms nothing',\n    ngoTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter === null,\n    `the \"${ngoTrip.tampered.parameter}\" parameter of the ${ngoTrip.linkSource} link was mutated inside its own alphabet` +\n      ` · following the tampered link answered HTTP ${ngoTrip.tampered.status} -> ${ngoTrip.tampered.location || '<no location header>'}` +\n      ` · auth.users.email_confirmed_at immediately afterwards = ${ngoTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n      ' · this says nothing about expiry, single use or resend: the token followed was never issued' +\n      (ngoTrip.linkSource === 'admin-generated'\n        ? ' · NARROWED: the link mutated was the operator-minted one, because the catcher yielded none.'\n        : ''),\n  );\n}\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (c) SIGN-IN BEFORE CONFIRMATION IS REFUSED, WITH THE REFUSAL TEXT CAPTURED VERBATIM.\n *\n * The second unverified-runtime-claim. On the live stack this block sits UPSTREAM of the shipped\n * gate: an unconfirmed user cannot authenticate at all, so it never reaches a write path. That is\n * why the gate is not redundant rather than why it is — decision-8 makes verification the write\n * path's own floor, and non-public paths do not pass through this refusal.\n *\n * THE REFUSAL MUST BE THE CONFIRMATION ONE, not any refusal. `status >= 400` was the first\n * version's predicate and a reviewer showed what it accepts: a 429 from the mailer's rate limiter\n * and a 500 from a broken stack both record as PASS while confirmation enforces nothing. See\n * `unconfirmedSignInRefusal` above for the two halves a pass now needs.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nconst ngoRefusal = unconfirmedSignInRefusal(ngoTrip.signInBefore);\n\nrecord(\n  'c',\n  'sign-in BEFORE confirmation is refused by Auth itself, with a CONFIRMATION-SPECIFIC refusal (a 4xx that is not 429, naming the unconfirmed state)',\n  ngoRefusal.ok,\n  `sign-in ${ngoRefusal.discriminator}` +\n    ` · redacted body: ${redact(ngoTrip.signInBefore.body)} — captured verbatim, this is the refusal text` +\n    ' · the discriminator above is read from the in-memory body, because the redactor blanks error_code',\n);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (d) USING THE LINK FLIPS THE COLUMN, SIGN-IN THEN SUCCEEDS, AND THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR AGREES\n *     WITH GOTRUE'S REAL SERIALISATION.\n *\n * The third unverified-runtime-claim and the one this whole script exists for. `emailVerifiedFromUser`\n * is fed the RAW `/auth/v1/user` body — not a shape this script built — and must answer true.\n *\n * ITS FALSE HALF IS NOT EXERCISED HERE, and the transcript says so rather than dressing it up: an\n * unconfirmed user holds no session, so there is no token with which to read `/auth/v1/user` for\n * one. The false half is exercised at the loop tier and in the direct shape selftest.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nrecord(\n  'd',\n  'following the verification link flips email_confirmed_at, sign-in then succeeds, and the SHIPPED emailVerifiedFromUser answers true on the real /auth/v1/user body',\n  ngoTrip.followStatus >= 300 &&\n    ngoTrip.followStatus < 400 &&\n    ngoTrip.confirmedAfter !== null &&\n    ngoTrip.signInAfter.status === 200 &&\n    ngoTrip.accessToken !== null &&\n    ngoTrip.rawUserWire.status === 200 &&\n    ngoTrip.shippedVerdict === true,\n  `link follow HTTP ${ngoTrip.followStatus} -> ${ngoTrip.followLocation || '<no location header>'}` +\n    ` · email_confirmed_at before=${ngoTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'} after=${ngoTrip.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n    ` · sign-in after HTTP ${ngoTrip.signInAfter.status} (access token present: ${ngoTrip.accessToken !== null})` +\n    ` · /auth/v1/user HTTP ${ngoTrip.rawUserWire.status} ${redact(ngoTrip.rawUserWire.body)}` +\n    ` · the SHIPPED emailVerifiedFromUser says: ${ngoTrip.shippedVerdict}` +\n    ' · the extractor\\'s FALSE half is NOT exercised here: an unconfirmed user holds no session to read /auth/v1/user with',\n);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (e) BOTH EMAIL-CAPABLE ACCOUNT TYPES FLOW END TO END UNDER THE FLIPPED CONFIG.\n *\n * WHY THE PREDECESSOR'S COMPLETION EVIDENCE IS NOT CITED HERE, which is gate-1 ruling [2] adopted\n * whole: the flip changes the auth context beneath BOTH completion paths. That evidence was\n * produced with confirmations OFF, so it predates this change and no longer covers it. Both paths\n * are therefore re-proved rather than referenced.\n *\n * The NGO address, already confirmed by (a)–(d), completes through the DEPLOYED complete-signup\n * edge function. The volunteer address repeats the whole round trip on its own, then gains a\n * GitHub identity by the operator insert above — flip-independent, because it is a database write\n * and not an auth flow — and completes as a volunteer through the same deployed function.\n * ============================================================================================= */\n\nconst ngoCompletion = ngoTrip.accessToken\n  ? await callFunction('complete-signup', ngoTrip.accessToken, {\n      accountType: 'ngo',\n      organizationName: 'Riverside Shelter',\n      acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n    })\n  : { status: 0, body: 'no access token — the NGO never signed in' };\n\nconst ngoRows = await sql`\n  select a.account_type, o.name, m.role\n    from public.accounts a\n    left join public.org_memberships m on m.account_id = a.id\n    left join public.organizations o on o.id = m.org_id\n   where a.id = ${ngoTrip.userId}::uuid`;\n\nconst volunteerEmail = address('volunteer-verify');\nconst volunteerTrip = await verificationRoundTrip(volunteerEmail);\n\nif (volunteerTrip.accessToken) await fabricateGithubIdentity(volunteerTrip.userId, VOLUNTEER_HANDLE);\n\nconst volunteerCompletion = volunteerTrip.accessToken\n  ? await callFunction('complete-signup', volunteerTrip.accessToken, {\n      accountType: 'volunteer',\n      acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,\n    })\n  : { status: 0, body: 'no access token — the volunteer never signed in' };\n\nconst volunteerAccountRows = await sql`select account_type from public.accounts where id = ${volunteerTrip.userId}::uuid`;\nconst volunteerProfileRows = await sql`\n  select github_handle, top_languages, repository_count, contribution_summary, imported_at\n    from public.volunteer_profiles where account_id = ${volunteerTrip.userId}::uuid`;\nconst expectedStats = stubGithubStatsFor(VOLUNTEER_HANDLE);\nconst volunteerProfile = volunteerProfileRows[0] as\n  | { github_handle: string; top_languages: string[]; repository_count: number; contribution_summary: string; imported_at: Date }\n  | undefined;\n\n// THE VOLUNTEER'S PRE-CONFIRMATION REFUSAL GETS THE SAME TEETH AS (c)'s, per the same ruling: a\n// claim that the volunteer \"repeats (a)-(d)\" is only true if it is measured the same way.\nconst volunteerRefusal = unconfirmedSignInRefusal(volunteerTrip.signInBefore);\n\nrecord(\n  'e',\n  'both email-capable types flow end to end under the flipped config: the NGO completes after confirmation, and the volunteer confirms, links GitHub and completes with its profile populated',\n  // The NGO half.\n  ngoCompletion.status === 200 &&\n    ngoRows.length === 1 &&\n    ngoRows[0].account_type === 'ngo' &&\n    ngoRows[0].name === 'Riverside Shelter' &&\n    ngoRows[0].role === 'admin' &&\n    // The volunteer half: its OWN round trip repeated (a)-(d), then completion.\n    volunteerTrip.signupCarriedSession === false &&\n    volunteerTrip.confirmedBefore === null &&\n    // THE REFUSAL IS THE CONFIRMATION ONE, not merely a 4xx — the same predicate (c) uses.\n    volunteerRefusal.ok &&\n    // AND THE LINK CAME FROM THE CATCHER. Without this, the admin-minted fallback could carry the\n    // volunteer half while the claim \"repeats (b)\" quietly narrowed to something smaller. The NGO\n    // half already cannot do that, because (b) fails with a NARROWED note on the fallback.\n    volunteerTrip.linkSource === 'emailed' &&\n    volunteerTrip.confirmedAfter !== null &&\n    volunteerTrip.shippedVerdict === true &&\n    volunteerCompletion.status === 200 &&\n    volunteerAccountRows.length === 1 &&\n    volunteerAccountRows[0].account_type === 'volunteer' &&\n    volunteerProfileRows.length === 1 &&\n    volunteerProfile?.github_handle === VOLUNTEER_HANDLE &&\n    JSON.stringify(volunteerProfile?.top_languages) === JSON.stringify(expectedStats.topLanguages) &&\n    Number(volunteerProfile?.repository_count) === expectedStats.repositoryCount &&\n    volunteerProfile?.contribution_summary === expectedStats.contributionSummary,\n  `NGO completion HTTP ${ngoCompletion.status} ${redact(ngoCompletion.body)} · rows=${JSON.stringify(ngoRows)}\\n        ` +\n    `volunteer round trip: signup carried a session=${volunteerTrip.signupCarriedSession}` +\n    ` · confirmed before=${volunteerTrip.confirmedBefore ?? 'NULL'} after=${volunteerTrip.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n    ` · sign-in before confirmation ${volunteerRefusal.discriminator}, redacted body ${redact(volunteerTrip.signInBefore.body)}` +\n    ` · link source ${volunteerTrip.linkSource} (${volunteerTrip.linkEvidence})` +\n    ` · its own tampered-token probe: attempted=${volunteerTrip.tampered.attempted}` +\n    ` email_confirmed_at afterwards=${volunteerTrip.tampered.confirmedAfter ?? 'NULL'}` +\n    ` · shipped emailVerifiedFromUser says ${volunteerTrip.shippedVerdict}\\n        ` +\n    `volunteer completion HTTP ${volunteerCompletion.status} ${redact(volunteerCompletion.body)}` +\n    ` · account=${JSON.stringify(volunteerAccountRows)} · stored profile=${JSON.stringify(volunteerProfileRows)}` +\n    ` · the shipped stub's judgement for \"${VOLUNTEER_HANDLE}\"=${JSON.stringify(expectedStats)}`,\n);\n\n/* =============================================================================================\n * (f) THE MAILER RATE LIMIT — reported either way, so a starved run can never look like a clean\n *     one.\n *\n * `[auth.rate_limit] email_sent` caps auth emails per hour, and this run sends exactly TWO","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":989,"totalLines":990,"truncated":true}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\proof-local.ts","time":{"start":1786290395492,"end":1786290395506}},"id":"prt_fe7345902001mJi2VwxJAhJleD","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe73445270019yfmL1VZxizM0z"}}
HEAD:loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10251:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:30:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290434575,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_axWvrfDUFQWUu3Lq6g3M8628","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_fixture.ts"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_fixture.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /**\n2:  * REQ-001's fixture adapter — STORAGE ONLY. Every judgement is the shipped module's.\n3:  *\n4:  * READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING A GREEN FROM THIS SUITE, because the honest claim is narrower than\n5:  * \"REQ-001 works\" and wider than what most acceptance adapters can say.\n6:  *\n7:  * `adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts` stamps every `sut.<key>` a\n8:  * STAND-IN unconditionally, and the registry refuses a stand-in above the loop tier. So nothing here\n9:  * can reach the integration-tier run that is the evidence gate. What a loop-tier green from this\n10:  * suite means is exactly this:\n11:  *\n12:  *   - PROVED: the decisions in `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`,\n13:  *     `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` and `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`\n14:  *     behave as the nine acceptance criteria this suite lands require. THE THREE MODULES DO NOT\n15:  *     HAVE THE SAME STANDING, and saying they do would be an untrue stated fact: `accounts.ts` and\n16:  *     `github.ts` are imported by the deployed edge functions, byte for byte the code that ships.\n17:  *     `verification.ts` is imported by NO deployed function — it is the module the FUTURE Discovery\n18:  *     send route must import, and today only this suite and\n19:  *     `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` import it. That is decision D-D of the\n20:  *     verification leaf's plan and not an oversight; the module's own header says so too.\n21:  *     Every PRODUCT judgement below — every accept and every refusal about account types, the\n22:  *     GitHub precondition, the verified fact and the Discovery floor — comes from those modules,\n23:  *     and so does the onboarding import's content. THE ONE EXCEPTION is the fixture's own\n24:  *     BOOKKEEPING precondition refusals: `sendDiscoveryMessage` and `createOrganization` refuse an\n25:  *     unknown session and an account that never completed signup, which is this storage checking\n26:  *     that the world it was handed exists rather than any shipped rule judging anything. There is\n27:  *     no second copy of the product rules in this file, deliberately: the moment there is one, this\n28:  *     suite is grading a puppet and the green is worth nothing.\n29:  *   - NOT PROVED: that the migration is correct, that either edge function works, that row-level\n30:  *     security denies what it should, that Supabase Auth is configured, or that Google or GitHub\n31:  *     sign-in works. None of that is reachable from here — the storage below is a Map. The evidence\n32:  *     for that half at THIS tree's head is `loop/items/AI4DEV-58/proof-local.txt`, produced against\n33:  *     the live local stack on one machine and EXISTS: 9 checks, 8 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped. It\n34:  *     covers the migration, the GitHub gate, the imported profile, the database's own backstops\n35:  *     against a caller who never met TypeScript, atomicity, and the provider configuration. THE\n36:  *     SKIP IS THE GITHUB HANDSHAKE: no GitHub OAuth app exists for this project, so \"GitHub\n37:  *     sign-in works\" is not proved by the live tier either. The GOOGLE HANDSHAKE is unproved for\n38:  *     the same reason and was never re-attempted here — no Google credential was in the\n39:  *     environment when the predecessor measured it and none is now. No amount of green anywhere in\n40:  *     this suite proves either handshake. A reviewer cannot reproduce that transcript; it is one\n41:  *     machine's word.\n42:  *\n43:  *     `loop/items/AI4DEV-57/proof-local.txt` is retained for the ONE thing only it still covers:\n44:  *     `create-organization`, which was exercised there and is untouched by this leaf. Its\n45:  *     completion-path and schema evidence is SUPERSEDED — it predates this migration and called a\n46:  *     `complete_signup` that no longer exists.\n47:  *\n48:  * WHAT THE STORAGE HALF DOES MIRROR, because a stand-in that mirrors nothing tests nothing: one\n49:  * account row per auth user (so \"one account holds exactly one global type\" is structural here as it\n50:  * is in the schema, not a rule the adapter remembers), and completion is all-or-nothing (so a body\n51:  * asserting no partial state is asserting the same shape the database's `complete_signup` function\n52:  * guarantees). It mirrors the SHAPE; the database's own guarantee is proved on the live stack.\n53:  *\n54:  * ============================================================================================\n55:  * THE VENDOR MIRRORS, EACH WITH WHAT BINDS IT — the verification leaf's four, named one by one.\n56:  * ============================================================================================\n57:  *\n58:  * A MIRROR IS NOT A PRODUCT JUDGEMENT, and keeping the two apart is what this section is for. The\n59:  * two PRODUCT judgements about verification — whether an address is verified, and whether an\n60:  * unverified caller may send a Discovery message — come from the shipped\n61:  * `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts` and from nowhere else; there is no second copy of\n62:  * either rule in this file. What is left is a prediction of what Supabase Auth DOES, and a\n63:  * prediction has to say what would prove it wrong.\n64:  *\n65:  *   1. AN EMAIL/PASSWORD REGISTRATION STARTS UNCONFIRMED AND AN EMAIL CARRYING A VERIFICATION\n66:  *      LINK IS SENT.\n67:  *      BOUND — checks (a) and (b) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` on the live stack: the\n68:  *      signup's own wire response, `auth.users.email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read on the database,\n69:  *      and the confirmation email held by the local mail catcher.\n70:  *   2. A LINK THAT WAS NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.\n71:  *      BOUND — check (b2) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`, and by that check alone. It\n72:  *      follows a TAMPERED variant of the real link — the token mutated, so a token GoTrue never\n73:  *      issued — BEFORE the real link is followed, and reads `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` still\n74:  *      NULL afterwards. This entry used to cite checks (a)-(d), which measure only the POSITIVE\n75:  *      half; two blind reviewers found that overstatement independently, so the check that\n76:  *      measures the negative was added rather than the label softened. Note what this is NOT: it\n77:  *      is not a claim about expiry, single use or resend. The token followed was never minted, so\n78:  *      no lifetime and no use count is in play. AT-001.11 is retired and none of those semantics\n79:  *      is modelled here.\n80:  *   3. USING THE EMAILED LINK SETS `email_confirmed_at`.\n81:  *      BOUND — checks (b) and (d): the link is followed by HTTP GET and the column flips to\n82:  *      non-null, after which the real `/auth/v1/user` response is fed to the SHIPPED\n83:  *      `emailVerifiedFromUser`, which answers true.\n84:  *   4. A PROVIDER REGISTRATION (Google, GitHub) STARTS CONFIRMED, because the provider vouched for\n85:  *      the address.\n86:  *      **UNBOUND, AND SAID PLAINLY.** No OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, so no\n87:  *      live provider session is obtainable — the same recorded gap that leaves the OAuth handshake\n88:  *      itself unproved at every tier, stated in this header since the predecessor item. This item\n89:  *      binds it nowhere and claims nothing about it. NO TEST IN THIS SUITE READS A PROVIDER USER'S\n90:  *      VERIFIED STATE: both verification bodies register by email and password. If the prediction\n91:  *      is wrong — a real provider user arriving unconfirmed — the shipped gate FAILS CLOSED: it\n92:  *      refuses that caller, never allows one. The mirror is bound by the first item that ships a\n93:  *      real provider-path consumer, and not before.\n94:  *\n95:  * THE PROVISIONED PLATFORM ADMINISTRATOR STARTS CONFIRMED, and it is a mirror of a RECIPE rather\n96:  * than of a live measurement. `provisionPlatformAdmin` below marks its auth user confirmed and\n97:  * mints no verification link, because creating an administrator sends no email. The recipe it\n98:  * mirrors is the only one this repository records: the predecessor item's live-proof script creates\n99:  * that user through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with `email_confirm: true`. The earlier version of\n100:  * this fixture let the administrator start UNCONFIRMED by reusing the public email path, which\n101:  * contradicted that record.\n102:  * WHAT BINDS IT: nothing yet, and that is said plainly. NOTHING READS AN ADMINISTRATOR'S VERIFIED\n103:  * STATE — not AT-001.07, not anything else in this suite. The predecessor's transcript ran with\n104:  * confirmations OFF, so it does not measure the column under this leaf's flip either. So this\n105:  * mirror is labelled by its recipe and is never called live-bound; the first item that reads an\n106:  * administrator's verified state is the one that binds it.\n107:  *\n108:  * AND THE DISCOVERY SEND SURFACE IS NOT A MIRROR AT ALL — it mirrors nothing, because there is\n109:  * nothing to mirror. No Discovery route exists in this repository at either tier; it is\n110:  * REQ-002/004's. `sendDiscoveryMessage` below is a stand-in surface whose whole job is to put the\n111:  * shipped gate on a tested path. No green over it says anything about enforcement anywhere.\n112:  */\n113: \n114: import type { FixtureWorld, FixtureWorldStore } from '../../harness/fixtures.ts';\n115: import {\n116:   PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND,\n117:   PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES,\n118:   ngoOnlyActionAllowed,\n119:   validateCompleteSignup,\n120:   validateOrganizationName,\n121:   type AccountType,\n122:   type CompleteSignupRequest,\n123: } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n124: // BOTH shipped GitHub judgements, not one. The IMPORT SOURCE is the shipped stub, not a copy living\n125: // in this file — AT-001.05 compares the profile it reads back against `stubGithubStatsFor`, so if the\n126: // two were separate implementations the test would grade the fixture's copy and say nothing about\n127: // what the edge function writes. And `extractGithubHandle` is the OTHER shipped decision on this\n128: // path: `completeSignup` below derives the caller fact through it rather than reading the stored\n129: // handle straight, exactly as `resolveCaller` does in the edge function. Before that, a regression\n130: // in the extractor — returning null for a linked identity — would have rejected every linked\n131: // volunteer at the deployed edge while this suite stayed green.\n132: import { extractGithubHandle, stubGithubStatsFor } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts';\n133: // BOTH shipped verification judgements, for the same reason both GitHub ones are imported above.\n134: // `emailVerifiedFromUser` is how the verified fact is DERIVED from the rendered GoTrue user shape\n135: // on every read — never read straight off storage — and `discoveryMessageAllowed` is the ONLY thing\n136: // that decides whether a Discovery send is refused. Between them there is no rule about\n137: // verification left in this file to drift.\n138: import {\n139:   discoveryMessageAllowed,\n140:   emailVerifiedFromUser,\n141: } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';\n142: import type {\n143:   AccountRow,\n144:   AccountsSut,\n145:   AcknowledgmentRow,\n146:   CompleteSignupOutcome,\n147:   CreateOrganizationOutcome,\n148:   MembershipRow,\n149:   OrganizationRow,\n150:   SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome,\n151:   Session,\n152:   SessionProvider,\n153:   SignInOutcome,\n154:   VolunteerProfileRow,\n155:   World,\n156: } from './_contract.ts';\n157: \n158: /**\n159:  * WHICH REQUIREMENT THIS ADAPTER IS, declared by the adapter itself.\n160:  *\n161:  * `harness/suite-adapters.ts` constrains its map entry to match this literal, and `loadAdapter()` in\n162:  * `harness/index.ts` re-checks it against the requirement it was asked for. Without it the\n163:  * type-check could describe one suite while the run drove another.\n164:  */\n165: export const requirement = 'req-001' as const;\n166: \n167: interface AdapterOptions {\n168:   worlds: FixtureWorldStore;\n169: }\n170: \n171: /** An auth user as Supabase Auth holds one: credentials and the provider that established them. */\n172: interface AuthUser {\n173:   id: string;\n174:   email: string;\n175:   password: string | null;\n176:   provider: SessionProvider;\n177:   /**\n178:    * The handle of a LINKED GitHub identity, or null — Auth's `identities[]` narrowed to the one\n179:    * field anything here reads.\n180:    *\n181:    * It is stored on the auth user and NOT on the session, which is the whole reason the gate is a\n182:    * caller fact: linking happens after a session exists, changes nothing about how that session was\n183:    * established, and is a property of the user Auth answers for.\n184:    */\n185:   githubHandle: string | null;\n186:   /**\n187:    * GoTrue's `email_confirmed_at`, or null while the address is unconfirmed — vendor mirror 1 and\n188:    * 4 in this file's header.\n189:    *\n190:    * IT IS STORED AS THE VENDOR'S OWN FIELD VALUE, a nullable instant, and never as a boolean. The\n191:    * shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser` is what turns it into an answer, and it does so from the\n192:    * rendered `/auth/v1/user` shape rather than from this field directly. Storing a boolean here\n193:    * would put the judgement in storage, which is the one thing this file's opening paragraph\n194:    * promises it does not do.\n195:    */\n196:   emailConfirmedAt: string | null;\n197:   /**\n198:    * The verification link Auth emailed for this address, or null when no confirmation email was\n199:    * sent — the fixture's stand-in for the message the local mail catcher holds on the live stack.\n200:    *\n201:    * IT IS NOT CLEARED WHEN IT IS USED, deliberately. Clearing it would model SINGLE USE, and\n202:    * single-use semantics are retired AT-001.11's — \"not stated in REQ-001\". The fixture models\n203:    * what the criterion states and no more.\n204:    */\n205:   verificationLink: string | null;\n206: }\n207: \n208: interface StoredAcknowledgment extends AcknowledgmentRow {}\n209: \n210: interface State {\n211:   authUsers: Map<string, AuthUser>;\n212:   /** email -> auth user id, because sign-in arrives with an address rather than an id */\n213:   byEmail: Map<string, string>;\n214:   /**\n215:    * verification link -> auth user id, because a link arrives on its own with no address beside it\n216:    * — which is exactly the situation on the live stack, where the link is followed from an inbox.\n217:    *\n218:    * A link this map does not hold was never issued, and confirms nothing.\n219:    */\n220:   byVerificationLink: Map<string, string>;\n221:   accounts: Map<string, AccountRow>;\n222:   organizations: Map<string, OrganizationRow>;\n223:   /** `${organizationId}:${accountId}` -> row */\n224:   memberships: Map<string, MembershipRow>;\n225:   acknowledgments: StoredAcknowledgment[];\n226:   /** account id -> the imported volunteer profile, mirroring `public.volunteer_profiles`'s primary key */\n227:   volunteerProfiles: Map<string, VolunteerProfileRow>;\n228:   /**\n229:    * account id -> the Discovery message bodies that account has sent.\n230:    *\n231:    * There is NO table behind this and no route that writes one; see the header's closing paragraph.\n232:    * It exists so AT-001.10 can assert that a refused send wrote NOTHING, which the refusal's own\n233:    * return value cannot show.\n234:    */\n235:   discoveryMessages: Map<string, string[]>;\n236:   nextId: number;\n237: }\n238: \n239: function membershipKey(organizationId: string, accountId: string): string {\n240:   return `${organizationId}:${accountId}`;\n241: }\n242: \n243: function clone<T>(value: T): T {\n244:   return structuredClone(value);\n245: }\n246: \n247: class AccountsFixtureWorld implements World {\n248:   constructor(\n249:     private readonly base: FixtureWorld,\n250:     private readonly serial: number,\n251:   ) {}\n252: \n253:   email(local: string): string {\n254:     return `${local}+w${this.serial}@example.test`;\n255:   }\n256: \n257:   async teardown(): Promise<void> {\n258:     await this.base.teardown();\n259:   }\n260: }\n261: \n262: export function createFixtureAdapter({ worlds }: AdapterOptions) {\n263:   const state: State = {\n264:     authUsers: new Map(),\n265:     byEmail: new Map(),\n266:     byVerificationLink: new Map(),\n267:     accounts: new Map(),\n268:     organizations: new Map(),\n269:     memberships: new Map(),\n270:     acknowledgments: [],\n271:     volunteerProfiles: new Map(),\n272:     discoveryMessages: new Map(),\n273:     nextId: 1,\n274:   };\n275:   const openedWorlds = new Set<AccountsFixtureWorld>();\n276:   let worldSerial = 0;\n277: \n278:   const nextId = (prefix: string): string => `${prefix}-${state.nextId++}`;\n279: \n280:   /**\n281:    * The instant a confirmation is recorded at.\n282:    *\n283:    * Fixed rather than read from a clock, for the same reason the acknowledgment's instant is: no\n284:    * criterion here depends on WHICH instant, only on whether one is present, and a real timestamp\n285:    * would make the capture non-deterministic for no gain. The shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser`\n286:    * judges presence and never parses the value, so this is the whole of what the field has to be.\n287:    */\n288:   const CONFIRMED_AT = '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z';\n289: \n290:   const register = (\n291:     email: string,\n292:     password: string | null,\n293:     provider: SessionProvider,\n294:     githubHandle: string | null = null,\n295:     /**\n296:      * Whether the CREATOR already confirmed the address, which only an operator can do.\n297:      *\n298:      * It exists for `provisionPlatformAdmin` and for nothing else. See the header: the recipe this\n299:      * repository records creates an administrator through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with\n300:      * `email_confirm: true`, so that user never receives a confirmation email and never has a link.\n301:      */\n302:     confirmedByTheCreator = false,\n303:   ): Session => {\n304:     // Supabase Auth's own uniqueness, mirrored: a second registration on one address is not a new\n305:     // user. Left out, a body could accidentally create two auth users for one address and then read\n306:     // back \"one account per user\" from a situation the real system cannot be in.\n307:     const existing = state.byEmail.get(email);\n308:     if (existing) throw new Error(`fixture: ${email} is already registered — Supabase Auth would refuse this`);\n309: \n310:     // VENDOR MIRRORS 1 AND 4, and the header names what binds each. A public email/password\n311:     // registration starts UNCONFIRMED and Auth emails a confirmation link. A registration a\n312:     // provider vouched for, and one an operator created with the address already confirmed, both\n313:     // start CONFIRMED and receive no confirmation email, so neither has a link. The provider half\n314:     // is declared UNBOUND in the header — no OAuth credential exists here — and nothing in this\n315:     // suite reads a provider user's verified state.\n316:     const id = nextId('user');\n317:     const confirmedAtCreation = provider !== 'email' || confirmedByTheCreator;\n318:     const emailConfirmedAt = confirmedAtCreation ? CONFIRMED_AT : null;\n319:     // Derived from the user id rather than from a second counter, so the link is unique by\n320:     // construction and a link read in a failure message says which user it belongs to.\n321:     const verificationLink = confirmedAtCreation ? null : `verify-${id}`;\n322: \n323:     const user: AuthUser = { id, email, password, provider, githubHandle, emailConfirmedAt, verificationLink };\n324:     state.authUsers.set(user.id, user);\n325:     state.byEmail.set(email, user.id);\n326:     if (verificationLink !== null) state.byVerificationLink.set(verificationLink, user.id);\n327:     return { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider: user.provider };\n328:   };\n329: \n330:   /**\n331:    * The stored auth state rendered as the canonical GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` response shape.\n332:    *\n333:    * ONE RENDERER FOR BOTH SHIPPED EXTRACTORS. `extractGithubHandle` reads `identities[]` and\n334:    * `emailVerifiedFromUser` reads `email_confirmed_at`, and both read them off the SAME object here\n335:    * — which is the point: on the live stack they read one response body, so rendering two different\n336:    * shapes for them would be a divergence this suite could never notice.\n337:    *\n338:    * WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVED by anything that calls this: that this shape is the shape GoTrue really\n339:    * sends. That is a prediction of the vendor. The live proof is what binds it.\n340:    */\n341:   const renderAuthUser = (user: AuthUser): Record<string, unknown> => ({\n342:     id: user.id,\n343:     email: user.email,\n344:     email_confirmed_at: user.emailConfirmedAt,\n345:     identities:\n346:       user.githubHandle === null\n347:         ? []\n348:         : [{ provider: 'github', identity_data: { user_name: user.githubHandle } }],\n349:   });\n350: \n351:   /**\n352:    * Which providers can sign this user back in — Auth's `identities[]`, reduced to what AT-001.02\n353:    * asks about.\n354:    *\n355:    * A GitHub signup and a later GitHub LINK produce the same answer here, deliberately: after either,\n356:    * the account carries a GitHub identity, and \"a later sign-in via GitHub returns to the same\n357:    * account\" is a statement about the identity, not about which provider happened to create the user.\n358:    */\n359:   const reachableThroughProvider = (user: AuthUser, provider: SessionProvider): boolean =>\n360:     provider === 'github' ? user.githubHandle !== null : user.provider === provider;\n361: \n362:   /**\n363:    * The `complete-signup` operation, at the loop tier.\n364:    *\n365:    * EVERY DECISION ON THIS PATH COMES FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE. What is left here is the four writes,\n366:    * performed together or not at all — which is the shape `public.complete_signup` guarantees inside\n367:    * one transaction on the real database, and which step 7 of the plan proves there.\n368:    */\n369:   const completeSignup = async (\n370:     session: Session,\n371:     request: CompleteSignupRequest,\n372:     ip: string,\n373:   ): Promise<CompleteSignupOutcome> => {\n374:     const authUser = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n375:     if (!authUser) {\n376:       return { ok: false, reason: 'no authenticated user — sign in before completing signup' };\n377:     }\n378: \n379:     // THE CALLER FACT COMES FROM THE STORED AUTH USER, never from the request. That is the same\n380:     // shape the edge function has, where it comes from `/auth/v1/user` — a client cannot assert it\n381:     // in either place, which is the whole security property of the volunteer gate.\n382:     //\n383:     // AND IT IS DERIVED, NOT READ. The stored state is rendered as the canonical GoTrue\n384:     // `/auth/v1/user` shape — an `identities[]` array, empty when nothing is linked — and the handle\n385:     // is then judged out of it by the SHIPPED `extractGithubHandle`, which is precisely what\n386:     // `resolveCaller` does with the real response. Passing `authUser.githubHandle` in directly, as\n387:     // this used to, pre-narrowed the fact and left the extractor unexecuted by any test: storage\n388:     // would have been doing a judgement's job, which is the one thing this file's opening paragraph\n389:     // promises it does not do. What is still NOT proved here is GoTrue's real serialisation — that\n390:     // this shape is the shape Auth sends. Only the live proof touches that.\n391:     //\n392:     // The rendering itself is `renderAuthUser` above, shared with the verified-fact read, because\n393:     // on the live stack both facts come out of ONE response body and two renderings here could\n394:     // diverge with nothing able to notice.\n395:     const callerUser = renderAuthUser(authUser);\n396:     const decision = validateCompleteSignup(request, { githubHandle: extractGithubHandle(callerUser) });\n397:     if (!decision.ok) return { ok: false, reason: decision.reason };\n398:     const { accountType, organizationName, acknowledgmentTextVersion, githubHandle } = decision.value;\n399: \n400:     // ONE ROW PER AUTH USER is what makes \"one account holds exactly one global type\" structural\n401:     // rather than remembered — the schema states it as a primary key, and this states it as a\n402:     // refusal at the same point.\n403:     if (state.accounts.has(session.accountId)) {\n404:       return { ok: false, reason: 'this account has already completed signup — one account holds exactly one global type' };\n405:     }\n406: \n407:     const account: AccountRow = { id: session.accountId, accountType };\n408:     let organization: OrganizationRow | null = null;\n409:     let membership: MembershipRow | null = null;\n410: \n411:     if (organizationName !== null) {\n412:       organization = { id: nextId('org'), name: organizationName };\n413:       membership = { organizationId: organization.id, accountId: account.id, role: 'admin' };\n414:     }\n415: \n416:     // THE ONBOARDING IMPORT, COMPUTED HERE AND WRITTEN BELOW WITH EVERYTHING ELSE — which is the\n417:     // shape `public.complete_signup` guarantees inside one transaction on the real database. There\n418:     // is no queue and no deferred job: a volunteer completion either lands the account AND the\n419:     // populated profile, or lands nothing. AT-001.05's \"a queued-but-empty import fails this test\"\n420:     // is unrepresentable rather than merely untested.\n421:     let volunteerProfile: VolunteerProfileRow | null = null;\n422:     if (githubHandle !== null) {\n423:       const stats = stubGithubStatsFor(githubHandle);\n424:       volunteerProfile = {\n425:         accountId: session.accountId,\n426:         githubHandle,\n427:         topLanguages: stats.topLanguages,\n428:         repositoryCount: stats.repositoryCount,\n429:         contributionSummary: stats.contributionSummary,\n430:         // Fixed rather than read from a clock, for the same reason the acknowledgment's instant is:\n431:         // nothing in these criteria depends on WHICH instant, and the live-stack proof reads the\n432:         // database's own `now()`.\n433:         importedAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n434:       };\n435:     }\n436: \n437:     const acknowledgment: StoredAcknowledgment = {\n438:       accountId: account.id,\n439:       kind: PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND,\n440:       // Fixed rather than read from a clock: nothing in this leaf's four criteria depends on WHICH\n441:       // instant it is, only that one is recorded, and a real timestamp would make the capture\n442:       // non-deterministic for no gain. The live-stack proof reads `now()` from the database.\n443:       acknowledgedAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n444:       ip,\n445:       textVersion: acknowledgmentTextVersion,\n446:     };\n447: \n448:     // All of them, together. Nothing above this line has mutated `state`.\n449:     state.accounts.set(account.id, account);\n450:     if (organization) state.organizations.set(organization.id, organization);\n451:     if (membership) state.memberships.set(membershipKey(membership.organizationId, membership.accountId), membership);\n452:     if (volunteerProfile) state.volunteerProfiles.set(volunteerProfile.accountId, volunteerProfile);\n453:     state.acknowledgments.push(acknowledgment);\n454: \n455:     return { ok: true, accountId: account.id, organizationId: organization?.id ?? null };\n456:   };\n457: \n458:   const sut: AccountsSut = {\n459:     // Read straight off the shipped constant. A literal here would be a second statement of the same\n460:     // fact, and AT-001.07 would then be asserting what this file says rather than what ships.\n461:     publicSignupAccountTypes: async () => [...PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES],\n462: \n463:     registerWithEmailPassword: async (email, password) => register(email, password, 'email'),\n464:     registerWithProvider: async (provider, email) => register(email, null, provider),\n465:     // A GitHub signup links the GitHub identity it signed up with — that is what makes it a GitHub\n466:     // signup rather than a session that merely says 'github'. Both halves are recorded here in one\n467:     // step because Auth performs them in one round trip.\n468:     registerWithGithub: async (email, githubHandle) => register(email, null, 'github', githubHandle),\n469: \n470:     linkGithubIdentity: async (session, githubHandle) => {\n471:       const user = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n472:       // A throw, not a refusal: a body that links an identity onto a user Auth never registered has\n473:       // a bug in the TEST, and returning a polite outcome would let that bug read as a product\n474:       // refusal further down.\n475:       if (!user) throw new Error(`fixture: no auth user ${session.accountId} to link a GitHub identity to`);\n476:       user.githubHandle = githubHandle;\n477:     },\n478: \n479:     signInWithEmailPassword: async (email, password): Promise<SignInOutcome> => {\n480:       const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n481:       const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n482:       // One reason for both branches: telling \"no such account\" apart from \"wrong password\" is an\n483:       // existence oracle, and REQ-001 spends AT-001.21 forbidding exactly that shape elsewhere.\n484:       if (!user || user.password === null || user.password !== password) {\n485:         return { ok: false, reason: 'the email or password is incorrect' };\n486:       }\n487:       return { ok: true, session: { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider: user.provider } };\n488:     },\n489: \n490:     signInWithProvider: async (provider, email): Promise<SignInOutcome> => {\n491:       const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n492:       const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n493:       // One reason again, for the AT-001.21 reason above: \"no such account\" and \"that provider is\n494:       // not linked to it\" must not be distinguishable to a caller.\n495:       if (!user || !reachableThroughProvider(user, provider)) {\n496:         return { ok: false, reason: `no account of this address is reachable through ${provider}` };\n497:       }\n498:       // THE SAME ACCOUNT, which is the clause under test. The id comes from the stored user, so a\n499:       // second identity minted per sign-in would show up as a different `accountId` rather than\n500:       // being invisible.\n501:       return { ok: true, session: { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider } };\n502:     },\n503: \n504:     // DERIVED THROUGH THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR, never read off storage. `user.emailConfirmedAt` is\n505:     // rendered into the canonical `/auth/v1/user` shape and `emailVerifiedFromUser` judges it —\n506:     // the same R3 pattern `completeSignup` uses for the GitHub handle, and for the same reason:\n507:     // the extractor is what the future Discovery route will run, so it has to sit on the tested\n508:     // path rather than being a function no test ever executes.\n509:     emailVerified: async (accountId) => {\n510:       const user = state.authUsers.get(accountId);\n511:       // A throw, not `false`. `false` is the answer for an unverified user, so returning it here\n512:       // would make a body that read a user Auth never registered look like a correct negative —\n513:       // and the whole discriminating pair in AT-001.09 turns on telling those two apart.\n514:       if (!user) throw new Error(`fixture: no auth user ${accountId} whose verified state could be read`);\n515:       return emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(user));\n516:     },\n517: \n518:     emailedVerificationLink: async (email) => {\n519:       const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n520:       const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n521:       return user?.verificationLink ?? null;\n522:     },\n523: \n524:     // VENDOR MIRRORS 2 AND 3 — the header names what binds each. Using the link Auth emailed\n525:     // confirms that address; a link that was never issued flips nothing and says so.\n526:     //\n527:     // NOTHING HERE MODELS EXPIRY, SINGLE USE OR RESEND. The link is NOT removed from the map after\n528:     // it is used, because removing it would be single-use semantics, and AT-001.11 — which is\n529:     // where those semantics lived — is retired as unstated in REQ-001.\n530:     useVerificationLink: async (link) => {\n531:       const userId = state.byVerificationLink.get(link);\n532:       const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n533:       if (!user) return { ok: false };\n534:       user.emailConfirmedAt = CONFIRMED_AT;\n535:       return { ok: true };\n536:     },\n537: \n538:     completeSignup,\n539: \n540:     // THE STAND-IN SURFACE FOR A ROUTE THAT DOES NOT EXIST — see the header's closing paragraph\n541:     // and `AccountsSut`'s fourth kind. Every judgement below is the shipped module's; what is left\n542:     // here is one refusal about bookkeeping and one write.\n543:     sendDiscoveryMessage: async (session, body): Promise<SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome> => {\n544:       const authUser = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n545:       if (!authUser) return { ok: false, reason: 'no authenticated user — sign in before sending a Discovery message' };\n546:       // The same bookkeeping refusal `createOrganization` gives, and it is THIS FILE'S, not a\n547:       // product rule: an account that has not completed signup has no sender to record against.\n548:       // It is checked FIRST so that AT-001.10's refusal is unambiguously the gate's own — the\n549:       // account there has completed signup, so this branch cannot be what answers.\n550:       if (!state.accounts.has(session.accountId)) {\n551:         return { ok: false, reason: 'complete signup before sending a Discovery message' };\n552:       }\n553: \n554:       // THE VERIFIED FACT IS A CALLER FACT, derived exactly as `emailVerified` derives it: from the\n555:       // rendered response shape, through the shipped extractor. It is never a request field —\n556:       // a request field is something a client asserts about itself, and a floor built on one is no\n557:       // floor at all.\n558:       const emailVerified = emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(authUser));\n559: \n560:       // THE ONLY DECISION ON THIS PATH, and it is the shipped one. The refusal text is the GATE's,\n561:       // carried through unchanged — AT-001.10 matches the reason, so restating it here would make\n562:       // the test grade this file instead of the module that ships.\n563:       const allowed = discoveryMessageAllowed({ emailVerified });\n564:       if (!allowed.ok) return { ok: false, reason: allowed.reason };\n565: \n566:       // ONLY REACHED ON ALLOW, so a refusal writes nothing — which is what `discoveryMessagesBy`\n567:       // is read for. The body is stored opaquely: recipients, threads and message state are\n568:       // REQ-002/004's semantics and none of them is invented here.\n569:       const sent = state.discoveryMessages.get(session.accountId) ?? [];\n570:       sent.push(body);\n571:       state.discoveryMessages.set(session.accountId, sent);\n572:       return { ok: true };\n573:     },\n574: \n575:     discoveryMessagesBy: async (accountId) => clone(state.discoveryMessages.get(accountId) ?? []),\n576: \n577:     createOrganization: async (session, organizationName): Promise<CreateOrganizationOutcome> => {\n578:       const account = state.accounts.get(session.accountId);\n579:       if (!account) return { ok: false, reason: 'complete signup before creating an organisation' };\n580: \n581:       // THE REFUSAL IS THE SHIPPED MODULE'S, not this file's. That is what makes AT-001.06 a test of\n582:       // an application boundary rather than of a helper called directly from a test body.\n583:       const allowed = ngoOnlyActionAllowed(account.accountType);\n584:       if (!allowed.ok) return { ok: false, reason: allowed.reason };\n585: \n586:       // The name rule is the shipped module's too. It used to be spelled out here, which made this\n587:       // file hold a second copy of a rule — the one thing its opening paragraph promises it does\n588:       // not do.\n589:       const name = validateOrganizationName(organizationName);\n590:       if (!name.ok) return { ok: false, reason: name.reason };\n591: \n592:       const organization: OrganizationRow = { id: nextId('org'), name: name.value };\n593:       const membership: MembershipRow = { organizationId: organization.id, accountId: account.id, role: 'admin' };\n594:       state.organizations.set(organization.id, organization);\n595:       state.memberships.set(membershipKey(organization.id, account.id), membership);\n596:       return { ok: true, organizationId: organization.id };\n597:     },\n598: \n599:     account: async (accountId) => clone(state.accounts.get(accountId) ?? null),\n600:     organization: async (organizationId) => clone(state.organizations.get(organizationId) ?? null),\n601:     membership: async (organizationId, accountId) => clone(state.memberships.get(membershipKey(organizationId, accountId)) ?? null),\n602:     acknowledgments: async (accountId) => clone(state.acknowledgments.filter((row) => row.accountId === accountId)),\n603:     volunteerProfile: async (accountId) => clone(state.volunteerProfiles.get(accountId) ?? null),\n604: \n605:     // The two searches a refused action needs: it hands back no identifier, so the rows it must NOT\n606:     // have written can only be looked for by the name that was attempted and by who holds what.\n607:     organizationsNamed: async (name) => clone([...state.organizations.values()].filter((row) => row.name === name.trim())),\n608:     membershipsOf: async (accountId) => clone([...state.memberships.values()].filter((row) => row.accountId === accountId)),\n609: \n610:     // DISCRIMINATING — and WHICH predicate that green grades is worth being exact about, because\n611:     // this one is not the shipped one. `public.has_platform_acknowledgment` is SQL, and a\n612:     // TypeScript module cannot supply a SQL predicate; the one judgement inside it — which\n613:     // acknowledgment kind counts — is shared, which is why the constant below is imported rather\n614:     // than spelled. So AT-001.01's loop-tier green proves THE RULE and THIS storage: a completion\n615:     // writes the row, and the answer is false before and true after. The shipped SQL predicate\n616:     // could still return true unconditionally and this would stay green. What proves that one is\n617:     // step 7(h) of the plan, against the live database, and nothing else in this item.\n618:     hasPlatformAcknowledgment: async (accountId) =>\n619:       state.acknowledgments.some((row) => row.accountId === accountId && row.kind === PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND),\n620: \n621:     provisionPlatformAdmin: async (email, password) => {\n622:       // CONFIRMED AT PROVISIONING, AND NO VERIFICATION LINK — the header's administrator paragraph\n623:       // says why: the only provisioning recipe this repository records creates the user with\n624:       // `email_confirm: true`, and creating an administrator sends no email.\n625:       const session = register(email, password, 'email', null, true);\n626:       // Written directly, bypassing `completeSignup` — which is not a shortcut but the point:\n627:       // `parseAccountType` refuses this type, so the public path CANNOT produce it, and the only way\n628:       // an administrator exists is an authority the public never holds. The type is still taken from\n629:       // the shipped vocabulary rather than spelled as a literal.\n630:       const accountType: AccountType = 'platform_admin';\n631:       state.accounts.set(session.accountId, { id: session.accountId, accountType });\n632:       return session;\n633:     },\n634:   };\n635: \n636:   return {\n637:     sut: { accounts: sut },\n638:     fixtures: {\n639:       world: async (name: string) => {\n640:         const base = await worlds.world(name);\n641:         const world = new AccountsFixtureWorld(base, ++worldSerial);\n642:         openedWorlds.add(world);\n643:         return world;\n644:       },\n645:     },\n646:     teardown: async () => {\n647:       await Promise.all([...openedWorlds].map((world) => world.teardown()));\n648:       openedWorlds.clear();\n649:       state.authUsers.clear();\n650:       state.byEmail.clear();\n651:       state.byVerificationLink.clear();\n652:       state.accounts.clear();\n653:       state.organizations.clear();\n654:       state.memberships.clear();\n655:       state.acknowledgments.length = 0;\n656:       state.volunteerProfiles.clear();\n657:       state.discoveryMessages.clear();\n658:       state.nextId = 1;\n659:     },\n660:   };\n661: }\n\n(End of file - total 661 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/**\n * REQ-001's fixture adapter — STORAGE ONLY. Every judgement is the shipped module's.\n *\n * READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING A GREEN FROM THIS SUITE, because the honest claim is narrower than\n * \"REQ-001 works\" and wider than what most acceptance adapters can say.\n *\n * `adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts` stamps every `sut.<key>` a\n * STAND-IN unconditionally, and the registry refuses a stand-in above the loop tier. So nothing here\n * can reach the integration-tier run that is the evidence gate. What a loop-tier green from this\n * suite means is exactly this:\n *\n *   - PROVED: the decisions in `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`,\n *     `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` and `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`\n *     behave as the nine acceptance criteria this suite lands require. THE THREE MODULES DO NOT\n *     HAVE THE SAME STANDING, and saying they do would be an untrue stated fact: `accounts.ts` and\n *     `github.ts` are imported by the deployed edge functions, byte for byte the code that ships.\n *     `verification.ts` is imported by NO deployed function — it is the module the FUTURE Discovery\n *     send route must import, and today only this suite and\n *     `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` import it. That is decision D-D of the\n *     verification leaf's plan and not an oversight; the module's own header says so too.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_fixture.ts","text":"/**\n * REQ-001's fixture adapter — STORAGE ONLY. Every judgement is the shipped module's.\n *\n * READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING A GREEN FROM THIS SUITE, because the honest claim is narrower than\n * \"REQ-001 works\" and wider than what most acceptance adapters can say.\n *\n * `adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts` stamps every `sut.<key>` a\n * STAND-IN unconditionally, and the registry refuses a stand-in above the loop tier. So nothing here\n * can reach the integration-tier run that is the evidence gate. What a loop-tier green from this\n * suite means is exactly this:\n *\n *   - PROVED: the decisions in `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`,\n *     `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` and `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`\n *     behave as the nine acceptance criteria this suite lands require. THE THREE MODULES DO NOT\n *     HAVE THE SAME STANDING, and saying they do would be an untrue stated fact: `accounts.ts` and\n *     `github.ts` are imported by the deployed edge functions, byte for byte the code that ships.\n *     `verification.ts` is imported by NO deployed function — it is the module the FUTURE Discovery\n *     send route must import, and today only this suite and\n *     `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` import it. That is decision D-D of the\n *     verification leaf's plan and not an oversight; the module's own header says so too.\n *     Every PRODUCT judgement below — every accept and every refusal about account types, the\n *     GitHub precondition, the verified fact and the Discovery floor — comes from those modules,\n *     and so does the onboarding import's content. THE ONE EXCEPTION is the fixture's own\n *     BOOKKEEPING precondition refusals: `sendDiscoveryMessage` and `createOrganization` refuse an\n *     unknown session and an account that never completed signup, which is this storage checking\n *     that the world it was handed exists rather than any shipped rule judging anything. There is\n *     no second copy of the product rules in this file, deliberately: the moment there is one, this\n *     suite is grading a puppet and the green is worth nothing.\n *   - NOT PROVED: that the migration is correct, that either edge function works, that row-level\n *     security denies what it should, that Supabase Auth is configured, or that Google or GitHub\n *     sign-in works. None of that is reachable from here — the storage below is a Map. The evidence\n *     for that half at THIS tree's head is `loop/items/AI4DEV-58/proof-local.txt`, produced against\n *     the live local stack on one machine and EXISTS: 9 checks, 8 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped. It\n *     covers the migration, the GitHub gate, the imported profile, the database's own backstops\n *     against a caller who never met TypeScript, atomicity, and the provider configuration. THE\n *     SKIP IS THE GITHUB HANDSHAKE: no GitHub OAuth app exists for this project, so \"GitHub\n *     sign-in works\" is not proved by the live tier either. The GOOGLE HANDSHAKE is unproved for\n *     the same reason and was never re-attempted here — no Google credential was in the\n *     environment when the predecessor measured it and none is now. No amount of green anywhere in\n *     this suite proves either handshake. A reviewer cannot reproduce that transcript; it is one\n *     machine's word.\n *\n *     `loop/items/AI4DEV-57/proof-local.txt` is retained for the ONE thing only it still covers:\n *     `create-organization`, which was exercised there and is untouched by this leaf. Its\n *     completion-path and schema evidence is SUPERSEDED — it predates this migration and called a\n *     `complete_signup` that no longer exists.\n *\n * WHAT THE STORAGE HALF DOES MIRROR, because a stand-in that mirrors nothing tests nothing: one\n * account row per auth user (so \"one account holds exactly one global type\" is structural here as it\n * is in the schema, not a rule the adapter remembers), and completion is all-or-nothing (so a body\n * asserting no partial state is asserting the same shape the database's `complete_signup` function\n * guarantees). It mirrors the SHAPE; the database's own guarantee is proved on the live stack.\n *\n * ============================================================================================\n * THE VENDOR MIRRORS, EACH WITH WHAT BINDS IT — the verification leaf's four, named one by one.\n * ============================================================================================\n *\n * A MIRROR IS NOT A PRODUCT JUDGEMENT, and keeping the two apart is what this section is for. The\n * two PRODUCT judgements about verification — whether an address is verified, and whether an\n * unverified caller may send a Discovery message — come from the shipped\n * `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts` and from nowhere else; there is no second copy of\n * either rule in this file. What is left is a prediction of what Supabase Auth DOES, and a\n * prediction has to say what would prove it wrong.\n *\n *   1. AN EMAIL/PASSWORD REGISTRATION STARTS UNCONFIRMED AND AN EMAIL CARRYING A VERIFICATION\n *      LINK IS SENT.\n *      BOUND — checks (a) and (b) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` on the live stack: the\n *      signup's own wire response, `auth.users.email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read on the database,\n *      and the confirmation email held by the local mail catcher.\n *   2. A LINK THAT WAS NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.\n *      BOUND — check (b2) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`, and by that check alone. It\n *      follows a TAMPERED variant of the real link — the token mutated, so a token GoTrue never\n *      issued — BEFORE the real link is followed, and reads `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` still\n *      NULL afterwards. This entry used to cite checks (a)-(d), which measure only the POSITIVE\n *      half; two blind reviewers found that overstatement independently, so the check that\n *      measures the negative was added rather than the label softened. Note what this is NOT: it\n *      is not a claim about expiry, single use or resend. The token followed was never minted, so\n *      no lifetime and no use count is in play. AT-001.11 is retired and none of those semantics\n *      is modelled here.\n *   3. USING THE EMAILED LINK SETS `email_confirmed_at`.\n *      BOUND — checks (b) and (d): the link is followed by HTTP GET and the column flips to\n *      non-null, after which the real `/auth/v1/user` response is fed to the SHIPPED\n *      `emailVerifiedFromUser`, which answers true.\n *   4. A PROVIDER REGISTRATION (Google, GitHub) STARTS CONFIRMED, because the provider vouched for\n *      the address.\n *      **UNBOUND, AND SAID PLAINLY.** No OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, so no\n *      live provider session is obtainable — the same recorded gap that leaves the OAuth handshake\n *      itself unproved at every tier, stated in this header since the predecessor item. This item\n *      binds it nowhere and claims nothing about it. NO TEST IN THIS SUITE READS A PROVIDER USER'S\n *      VERIFIED STATE: both verification bodies register by email and password. If the prediction\n *      is wrong — a real provider user arriving unconfirmed — the shipped gate FAILS CLOSED: it\n *      refuses that caller, never allows one. The mirror is bound by the first item that ships a\n *      real provider-path consumer, and not before.\n *\n * THE PROVISIONED PLATFORM ADMINISTRATOR STARTS CONFIRMED, and it is a mirror of a RECIPE rather\n * than of a live measurement. `provisionPlatformAdmin` below marks its auth user confirmed and\n * mints no verification link, because creating an administrator sends no email. The recipe it\n * mirrors is the only one this repository records: the predecessor item's live-proof script creates\n * that user through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with `email_confirm: true`. The earlier version of\n * this fixture let the administrator start UNCONFIRMED by reusing the public email path, which\n * contradicted that record.\n * WHAT BINDS IT: nothing yet, and that is said plainly. NOTHING READS AN ADMINISTRATOR'S VERIFIED\n * STATE — not AT-001.07, not anything else in this suite. The predecessor's transcript ran with\n * confirmations OFF, so it does not measure the column under this leaf's flip either. So this\n * mirror is labelled by its recipe and is never called live-bound; the first item that reads an\n * administrator's verified state is the one that binds it.\n *\n * AND THE DISCOVERY SEND SURFACE IS NOT A MIRROR AT ALL — it mirrors nothing, because there is\n * nothing to mirror. No Discovery route exists in this repository at either tier; it is\n * REQ-002/004's. `sendDiscoveryMessage` below is a stand-in surface whose whole job is to put the\n * shipped gate on a tested path. No green over it says anything about enforcement anywhere.\n */\n\nimport type { FixtureWorld, FixtureWorldStore } from '../../harness/fixtures.ts';\nimport {\n  PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND,\n  PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES,\n  ngoOnlyActionAllowed,\n  validateCompleteSignup,\n  validateOrganizationName,\n  type AccountType,\n  type CompleteSignupRequest,\n} from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n// BOTH shipped GitHub judgements, not one. The IMPORT SOURCE is the shipped stub, not a copy living\n// in this file — AT-001.05 compares the profile it reads back against `stubGithubStatsFor`, so if the\n// two were separate implementations the test would grade the fixture's copy and say nothing about\n// what the edge function writes. And `extractGithubHandle` is the OTHER shipped decision on this\n// path: `completeSignup` below derives the caller fact through it rather than reading the stored\n// handle straight, exactly as `resolveCaller` does in the edge function. Before that, a regression\n// in the extractor — returning null for a linked identity — would have rejected every linked\n// volunteer at the deployed edge while this suite stayed green.\nimport { extractGithubHandle, stubGithubStatsFor } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts';\n// BOTH shipped verification judgements, for the same reason both GitHub ones are imported above.\n// `emailVerifiedFromUser` is how the verified fact is DERIVED from the rendered GoTrue user shape\n// on every read — never read straight off storage — and `discoveryMessageAllowed` is the ONLY thing\n// that decides whether a Discovery send is refused. Between them there is no rule about\n// verification left in this file to drift.\nimport {\n  discoveryMessageAllowed,\n  emailVerifiedFromUser,\n} from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';\nimport type {\n  AccountRow,\n  AccountsSut,\n  AcknowledgmentRow,\n  CompleteSignupOutcome,\n  CreateOrganizationOutcome,\n  MembershipRow,\n  OrganizationRow,\n  SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome,\n  Session,\n  SessionProvider,\n  SignInOutcome,\n  VolunteerProfileRow,\n  World,\n} from './_contract.ts';\n\n/**\n * WHICH REQUIREMENT THIS ADAPTER IS, declared by the adapter itself.\n *\n * `harness/suite-adapters.ts` constrains its map entry to match this literal, and `loadAdapter()` in\n * `harness/index.ts` re-checks it against the requirement it was asked for. Without it the\n * type-check could describe one suite while the run drove another.\n */\nexport const requirement = 'req-001' as const;\n\ninterface AdapterOptions {\n  worlds: FixtureWorldStore;\n}\n\n/** An auth user as Supabase Auth holds one: credentials and the provider that established them. */\ninterface AuthUser {\n  id: string;\n  email: string;\n  password: string | null;\n  provider: SessionProvider;\n  /**\n   * The handle of a LINKED GitHub identity, or null — Auth's `identities[]` narrowed to the one\n   * field anything here reads.\n   *\n   * It is stored on the auth user and NOT on the session, which is the whole reason the gate is a\n   * caller fact: linking happens after a session exists, changes nothing about how that session was\n   * established, and is a property of the user Auth answers for.\n   */\n  githubHandle: string | null;\n  /**\n   * GoTrue's `email_confirmed_at`, or null while the address is unconfirmed — vendor mirror 1 and\n   * 4 in this file's header.\n   *\n   * IT IS STORED AS THE VENDOR'S OWN FIELD VALUE, a nullable instant, and never as a boolean. The\n   * shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser` is what turns it into an answer, and it does so from the\n   * rendered `/auth/v1/user` shape rather than from this field directly. Storing a boolean here\n   * would put the judgement in storage, which is the one thing this file's opening paragraph\n   * promises it does not do.\n   */\n  emailConfirmedAt: string | null;\n  /**\n   * The verification link Auth emailed for this address, or null when no confirmation email was\n   * sent — the fixture's stand-in for the message the local mail catcher holds on the live stack.\n   *\n   * IT IS NOT CLEARED WHEN IT IS USED, deliberately. Clearing it would model SINGLE USE, and\n   * single-use semantics are retired AT-001.11's — \"not stated in REQ-001\". The fixture models\n   * what the criterion states and no more.\n   */\n  verificationLink: string | null;\n}\n\ninterface StoredAcknowledgment extends AcknowledgmentRow {}\n\ninterface State {\n  authUsers: Map<string, AuthUser>;\n  /** email -> auth user id, because sign-in arrives with an address rather than an id */\n  byEmail: Map<string, string>;\n  /**\n   * verification link -> auth user id, because a link arrives on its own with no address beside it\n   * — which is exactly the situation on the live stack, where the link is followed from an inbox.\n   *\n   * A link this map does not hold was never issued, and confirms nothing.\n   */\n  byVerificationLink: Map<string, string>;\n  accounts: Map<string, AccountRow>;\n  organizations: Map<string, OrganizationRow>;\n  /** `${organizationId}:${accountId}` -> row */\n  memberships: Map<string, MembershipRow>;\n  acknowledgments: StoredAcknowledgment[];\n  /** account id -> the imported volunteer profile, mirroring `public.volunteer_profiles`'s primary key */\n  volunteerProfiles: Map<string, VolunteerProfileRow>;\n  /**\n   * account id -> the Discovery message bodies that account has sent.\n   *\n   * There is NO table behind this and no route that writes one; see the header's closing paragraph.\n   * It exists so AT-001.10 can assert that a refused send wrote NOTHING, which the refusal's own\n   * return value cannot show.\n   */\n  discoveryMessages: Map<string, string[]>;\n  nextId: number;\n}\n\nfunction membershipKey(organizationId: string, accountId: string): string {\n  return `${organizationId}:${accountId}`;\n}\n\nfunction clone<T>(value: T): T {\n  return structuredClone(value);\n}\n\nclass AccountsFixtureWorld implements World {\n  constructor(\n    private readonly base: FixtureWorld,\n    private readonly serial: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  email(local: string): string {\n    return `${local}+w${this.serial}@example.test`;\n  }\n\n  async teardown(): Promise<void> {\n    await this.base.teardown();\n  }\n}\n\nexport function createFixtureAdapter({ worlds }: AdapterOptions) {\n  const state: State = {\n    authUsers: new Map(),\n    byEmail: new Map(),\n    byVerificationLink: new Map(),\n    accounts: new Map(),\n    organizations: new Map(),\n    memberships: new Map(),\n    acknowledgments: [],\n    volunteerProfiles: new Map(),\n    discoveryMessages: new Map(),\n    nextId: 1,\n  };\n  const openedWorlds = new Set<AccountsFixtureWorld>();\n  let worldSerial = 0;\n\n  const nextId = (prefix: string): string => `${prefix}-${state.nextId++}`;\n\n  /**\n   * The instant a confirmation is recorded at.\n   *\n   * Fixed rather than read from a clock, for the same reason the acknowledgment's instant is: no\n   * criterion here depends on WHICH instant, only on whether one is present, and a real timestamp\n   * would make the capture non-deterministic for no gain. The shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser`\n   * judges presence and never parses the value, so this is the whole of what the field has to be.\n   */\n  const CONFIRMED_AT = '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z';\n\n  const register = (\n    email: string,\n    password: string | null,\n    provider: SessionProvider,\n    githubHandle: string | null = null,\n    /**\n     * Whether the CREATOR already confirmed the address, which only an operator can do.\n     *\n     * It exists for `provisionPlatformAdmin` and for nothing else. See the header: the recipe this\n     * repository records creates an administrator through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with\n     * `email_confirm: true`, so that user never receives a confirmation email and never has a link.\n     */\n    confirmedByTheCreator = false,\n  ): Session => {\n    // Supabase Auth's own uniqueness, mirrored: a second registration on one address is not a new\n    // user. Left out, a body could accidentally create two auth users for one address and then read\n    // back \"one account per user\" from a situation the real system cannot be in.\n    const existing = state.byEmail.get(email);\n    if (existing) throw new Error(`fixture: ${email} is already registered — Supabase Auth would refuse this`);\n\n    // VENDOR MIRRORS 1 AND 4, and the header names what binds each. A public email/password\n    // registration starts UNCONFIRMED and Auth emails a confirmation link. A registration a\n    // provider vouched for, and one an operator created with the address already confirmed, both\n    // start CONFIRMED and receive no confirmation email, so neither has a link. The provider half\n    // is declared UNBOUND in the header — no OAuth credential exists here — and nothing in this\n    // suite reads a provider user's verified state.\n    const id = nextId('user');\n    const confirmedAtCreation = provider !== 'email' || confirmedByTheCreator;\n    const emailConfirmedAt = confirmedAtCreation ? CONFIRMED_AT : null;\n    // Derived from the user id rather than from a second counter, so the link is unique by\n    // construction and a link read in a failure message says which user it belongs to.\n    const verificationLink = confirmedAtCreation ? null : `verify-${id}`;\n\n    const user: AuthUser = { id, email, password, provider, githubHandle, emailConfirmedAt, verificationLink };\n    state.authUsers.set(user.id, user);\n    state.byEmail.set(email, user.id);\n    if (verificationLink !== null) state.byVerificationLink.set(verificationLink, user.id);\n    return { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider: user.provider };\n  };\n\n  /**\n   * The stored auth state rendered as the canonical GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` response shape.\n   *\n   * ONE RENDERER FOR BOTH SHIPPED EXTRACTORS. `extractGithubHandle` reads `identities[]` and\n   * `emailVerifiedFromUser` reads `email_confirmed_at`, and both read them off the SAME object here\n   * — which is the point: on the live stack they read one response body, so rendering two different\n   * shapes for them would be a divergence this suite could never notice.\n   *\n   * WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVED by anything that calls this: that this shape is the shape GoTrue really\n   * sends. That is a prediction of the vendor. The live proof is what binds it.\n   */\n  const renderAuthUser = (user: AuthUser): Record<string, unknown> => ({\n    id: user.id,\n    email: user.email,\n    email_confirmed_at: user.emailConfirmedAt,\n    identities:\n      user.githubHandle === null\n        ? []\n        : [{ provider: 'github', identity_data: { user_name: user.githubHandle } }],\n  });\n\n  /**\n   * Which providers can sign this user back in — Auth's `identities[]`, reduced to what AT-001.02\n   * asks about.\n   *\n   * A GitHub signup and a later GitHub LINK produce the same answer here, deliberately: after either,\n   * the account carries a GitHub identity, and \"a later sign-in via GitHub returns to the same\n   * account\" is a statement about the identity, not about which provider happened to create the user.\n   */\n  const reachableThroughProvider = (user: AuthUser, provider: SessionProvider): boolean =>\n    provider === 'github' ? user.githubHandle !== null : user.provider === provider;\n\n  /**\n   * The `complete-signup` operation, at the loop tier.\n   *\n   * EVERY DECISION ON THIS PATH COMES FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE. What is left here is the four writes,\n   * performed together or not at all — which is the shape `public.complete_signup` guarantees inside\n   * one transaction on the real database, and which step 7 of the plan proves there.\n   */\n  const completeSignup = async (\n    session: Session,\n    request: CompleteSignupRequest,\n    ip: string,\n  ): Promise<CompleteSignupOutcome> => {\n    const authUser = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n    if (!authUser) {\n      return { ok: false, reason: 'no authenticated user — sign in before completing signup' };\n    }\n\n    // THE CALLER FACT COMES FROM THE STORED AUTH USER, never from the request. That is the same\n    // shape the edge function has, where it comes from `/auth/v1/user` — a client cannot assert it\n    // in either place, which is the whole security property of the volunteer gate.\n    //\n    // AND IT IS DERIVED, NOT READ. The stored state is rendered as the canonical GoTrue\n    // `/auth/v1/user` shape — an `identities[]` array, empty when nothing is linked — and the handle\n    // is then judged out of it by the SHIPPED `extractGithubHandle`, which is precisely what\n    // `resolveCaller` does with the real response. Passing `authUser.githubHandle` in directly, as\n    // this used to, pre-narrowed the fact and left the extractor unexecuted by any test: storage\n    // would have been doing a judgement's job, which is the one thing this file's opening paragraph\n    // promises it does not do. What is still NOT proved here is GoTrue's real serialisation — that\n    // this shape is the shape Auth sends. Only the live proof touches that.\n    //\n    // The rendering itself is `renderAuthUser` above, shared with the verified-fact read, because\n    // on the live stack both facts come out of ONE response body and two renderings here could\n    // diverge with nothing able to notice.\n    const callerUser = renderAuthUser(authUser);\n    const decision = validateCompleteSignup(request, { githubHandle: extractGithubHandle(callerUser) });\n    if (!decision.ok) return { ok: false, reason: decision.reason };\n    const { accountType, organizationName, acknowledgmentTextVersion, githubHandle } = decision.value;\n\n    // ONE ROW PER AUTH USER is what makes \"one account holds exactly one global type\" structural\n    // rather than remembered — the schema states it as a primary key, and this states it as a\n    // refusal at the same point.\n    if (state.accounts.has(session.accountId)) {\n      return { ok: false, reason: 'this account has already completed signup — one account holds exactly one global type' };\n    }\n\n    const account: AccountRow = { id: session.accountId, accountType };\n    let organization: OrganizationRow | null = null;\n    let membership: MembershipRow | null = null;\n\n    if (organizationName !== null) {\n      organization = { id: nextId('org'), name: organizationName };\n      membership = { organizationId: organization.id, accountId: account.id, role: 'admin' };\n    }\n\n    // THE ONBOARDING IMPORT, COMPUTED HERE AND WRITTEN BELOW WITH EVERYTHING ELSE — which is the\n    // shape `public.complete_signup` guarantees inside one transaction on the real database. There\n    // is no queue and no deferred job: a volunteer completion either lands the account AND the\n    // populated profile, or lands nothing. AT-001.05's \"a queued-but-empty import fails this test\"\n    // is unrepresentable rather than merely untested.\n    let volunteerProfile: VolunteerProfileRow | null = null;\n    if (githubHandle !== null) {\n      const stats = stubGithubStatsFor(githubHandle);\n      volunteerProfile = {\n        accountId: session.accountId,\n        githubHandle,\n        topLanguages: stats.topLanguages,\n        repositoryCount: stats.repositoryCount,\n        contributionSummary: stats.contributionSummary,\n        // Fixed rather than read from a clock, for the same reason the acknowledgment's instant is:\n        // nothing in these criteria depends on WHICH instant, and the live-stack proof reads the\n        // database's own `now()`.\n        importedAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n      };\n    }\n\n    const acknowledgment: StoredAcknowledgment = {\n      accountId: account.id,\n      kind: PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND,\n      // Fixed rather than read from a clock: nothing in this leaf's four criteria depends on WHICH\n      // instant it is, only that one is recorded, and a real timestamp would make the capture\n      // non-deterministic for no gain. The live-stack proof reads `now()` from the database.\n      acknowledgedAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n      ip,\n      textVersion: acknowledgmentTextVersion,\n    };\n\n    // All of them, together. Nothing above this line has mutated `state`.\n    state.accounts.set(account.id, account);\n    if (organization) state.organizations.set(organization.id, organization);\n    if (membership) state.memberships.set(membershipKey(membership.organizationId, membership.accountId), membership);\n    if (volunteerProfile) state.volunteerProfiles.set(volunteerProfile.accountId, volunteerProfile);\n    state.acknowledgments.push(acknowledgment);\n\n    return { ok: true, accountId: account.id, organizationId: organization?.id ?? null };\n  };\n\n  const sut: AccountsSut = {\n    // Read straight off the shipped constant. A literal here would be a second statement of the same\n    // fact, and AT-001.07 would then be asserting what this file says rather than what ships.\n    publicSignupAccountTypes: async () => [...PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES],\n\n    registerWithEmailPassword: async (email, password) => register(email, password, 'email'),\n    registerWithProvider: async (provider, email) => register(email, null, provider),\n    // A GitHub signup links the GitHub identity it signed up with — that is what makes it a GitHub\n    // signup rather than a session that merely says 'github'. Both halves are recorded here in one\n    // step because Auth performs them in one round trip.\n    registerWithGithub: async (email, githubHandle) => register(email, null, 'github', githubHandle),\n\n    linkGithubIdentity: async (session, githubHandle) => {\n      const user = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n      // A throw, not a refusal: a body that links an identity onto a user Auth never registered has\n      // a bug in the TEST, and returning a polite outcome would let that bug read as a product\n      // refusal further down.\n      if (!user) throw new Error(`fixture: no auth user ${session.accountId} to link a GitHub identity to`);\n      user.githubHandle = githubHandle;\n    },\n\n    signInWithEmailPassword: async (email, password): Promise<SignInOutcome> => {\n      const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n      const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n      // One reason for both branches: telling \"no such account\" apart from \"wrong password\" is an\n      // existence oracle, and REQ-001 spends AT-001.21 forbidding exactly that shape elsewhere.\n      if (!user || user.password === null || user.password !== password) {\n        return { ok: false, reason: 'the email or password is incorrect' };\n      }\n      return { ok: true, session: { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider: user.provider } };\n    },\n\n    signInWithProvider: async (provider, email): Promise<SignInOutcome> => {\n      const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n      const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n      // One reason again, for the AT-001.21 reason above: \"no such account\" and \"that provider is\n      // not linked to it\" must not be distinguishable to a caller.\n      if (!user || !reachableThroughProvider(user, provider)) {\n        return { ok: false, reason: `no account of this address is reachable through ${provider}` };\n      }\n      // THE SAME ACCOUNT, which is the clause under test. The id comes from the stored user, so a\n      // second identity minted per sign-in would show up as a different `accountId` rather than\n      // being invisible.\n      return { ok: true, session: { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider } };\n    },\n\n    // DERIVED THROUGH THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR, never read off storage. `user.emailConfirmedAt` is\n    // rendered into the canonical `/auth/v1/user` shape and `emailVerifiedFromUser` judges it —\n    // the same R3 pattern `completeSignup` uses for the GitHub handle, and for the same reason:\n    // the extractor is what the future Discovery route will run, so it has to sit on the tested\n    // path rather than being a function no test ever executes.\n    emailVerified: async (accountId) => {\n      const user = state.authUsers.get(accountId);\n      // A throw, not `false`. `false` is the answer for an unverified user, so returning it here\n      // would make a body that read a user Auth never registered look like a correct negative —\n      // and the whole discriminating pair in AT-001.09 turns on telling those two apart.\n      if (!user) throw new Error(`fixture: no auth user ${accountId} whose verified state could be read`);\n      return emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(user));\n    },\n\n    emailedVerificationLink: async (email) => {\n      const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n      const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n      return user?.verificationLink ?? null;\n    },\n\n    // VENDOR MIRRORS 2 AND 3 — the header names what binds each. Using the link Auth emailed\n    // confirms that address; a link that was never issued flips nothing and says so.\n    //\n    // NOTHING HERE MODELS EXPIRY, SINGLE USE OR RESEND. The link is NOT removed from the map after\n    // it is used, because removing it would be single-use semantics, and AT-001.11 — which is\n    // where those semantics lived — is retired as unstated in REQ-001.\n    useVerificationLink: async (link) => {\n      const userId = state.byVerificationLink.get(link);\n      const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n      if (!user) return { ok: false };\n      user.emailConfirmedAt = CONFIRMED_AT;\n      return { ok: true };\n    },\n\n    completeSignup,\n\n    // THE STAND-IN SURFACE FOR A ROUTE THAT DOES NOT EXIST — see the header's closing paragraph\n    // and `AccountsSut`'s fourth kind. Every judgement below is the shipped module's; what is left\n    // here is one refusal about bookkeeping and one write.\n    sendDiscoveryMessage: async (session, body): Promise<SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome> => {\n      const authUser = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n      if (!authUser) return { ok: false, reason: 'no authenticated user — sign in before sending a Discovery message' };\n      // The same bookkeeping refusal `createOrganization` gives, and it is THIS FILE'S, not a\n      // product rule: an account that has not completed signup has no sender to record against.\n      // It is checked FIRST so that AT-001.10's refusal is unambiguously the gate's own — the\n      // account there has completed signup, so this branch cannot be what answers.\n      if (!state.accounts.has(session.accountId)) {\n        return { ok: false, reason: 'complete signup before sending a Discovery message' };\n      }\n\n      // THE VERIFIED FACT IS A CALLER FACT, derived exactly as `emailVerified` derives it: from the\n      // rendered response shape, through the shipped extractor. It is never a request field —\n      // a request field is something a client asserts about itself, and a floor built on one is no\n      // floor at all.\n      const emailVerified = emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(authUser));\n\n      // THE ONLY DECISION ON THIS PATH, and it is the shipped one. The refusal text is the GATE's,\n      // carried through unchanged — AT-001.10 matches the reason, so restating it here would make\n      // the test grade this file instead of the module that ships.\n      const allowed = discoveryMessageAllowed({ emailVerified });\n      if (!allowed.ok) return { ok: false, reason: allowed.reason };\n\n      // ONLY REACHED ON ALLOW, so a refusal writes nothing — which is what `discoveryMessagesBy`\n      // is read for. The body is stored opaquely: recipients, threads and message state are\n      // REQ-002/004's semantics and none of them is invented here.\n      const sent = state.discoveryMessages.get(session.accountId) ?? [];\n      sent.push(body);\n      state.discoveryMessages.set(session.accountId, sent);\n      return { ok: true };\n    },\n\n    discoveryMessagesBy: async (accountId) => clone(state.discoveryMessages.get(accountId) ?? []),\n\n    createOrganization: async (session, organizationName): Promise<CreateOrganizationOutcome> => {\n      const account = state.accounts.get(session.accountId);\n      if (!account) return { ok: false, reason: 'complete signup before creating an organisation' };\n\n      // THE REFUSAL IS THE SHIPPED MODULE'S, not this file's. That is what makes AT-001.06 a test of\n      // an application boundary rather than of a helper called directly from a test body.\n      const allowed = ngoOnlyActionAllowed(account.accountType);\n      if (!allowed.ok) return { ok: false, reason: allowed.reason };\n\n      // The name rule is the shipped module's too. It used to be spelled out here, which made this\n      // file hold a second copy of a rule — the one thing its opening paragraph promises it does\n      // not do.\n      const name = validateOrganizationName(organizationName);\n      if (!name.ok) return { ok: false, reason: name.reason };\n\n      const organization: OrganizationRow = { id: nextId('org'), name: name.value };\n      const membership: MembershipRow = { organizationId: organization.id, accountId: account.id, role: 'admin' };\n      state.organizations.set(organization.id, organization);\n      state.memberships.set(membershipKey(organization.id, account.id), membership);\n      return { ok: true, organizationId: organization.id };\n    },\n\n    account: async (accountId) => clone(state.accounts.get(accountId) ?? null),\n    organization: async (organizationId) => clone(state.organizations.get(organizationId) ?? null),\n    membership: async (organizationId, accountId) => clone(state.memberships.get(membershipKey(organizationId, accountId)) ?? null),\n    acknowledgments: async (accountId) => clone(state.acknowledgments.filter((row) => row.accountId === accountId)),\n    volunteerProfile: async (accountId) => clone(state.volunteerProfiles.get(accountId) ?? null),\n\n    // The two searches a refused action needs: it hands back no identifier, so the rows it must NOT\n    // have written can only be looked for by the name that was attempted and by who holds what.\n    organizationsNamed: async (name) => clone([...state.organizations.values()].filter((row) => row.name === name.trim())),\n    membershipsOf: async (accountId) => clone([...state.memberships.values()].filter((row) => row.accountId === accountId)),\n\n    // DISCRIMINATING — and WHICH predicate that green grades is worth being exact about, because\n    // this one is not the shipped one. `public.has_platform_acknowledgment` is SQL, and a\n    // TypeScript module cannot supply a SQL predicate; the one judgement inside it — which\n    // acknowledgment kind counts — is shared, which is why the constant below is imported rather\n    // than spelled. So AT-001.01's loop-tier green proves THE RULE and THIS storage: a completion\n    // writes the row, and the answer is false before and true after. The shipped SQL predicate\n    // could still return true unconditionally and this would stay green. What proves that one is\n    // step 7(h) of the plan, against the live database, and nothing else in this item.\n    hasPlatformAcknowledgment: async (accountId) =>\n      state.acknowledgments.some((row) => row.accountId === accountId && row.kind === PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND),\n\n    provisionPlatformAdmin: async (email, password) => {\n      // CONFIRMED AT PROVISIONING, AND NO VERIFICATION LINK — the header's administrator paragraph\n      // says why: the only provisioning recipe this repository records creates the user with\n      // `email_confirm: true`, and creating an administrator sends no email.\n      const session = register(email, password, 'email', null, true);\n      // Written directly, bypassing `completeSignup` — which is not a shortcut but the point:\n      // `parseAccountType` refuses this type, so the public path CANNOT produce it, and the only way\n      // an administrator exists is an authority the public never holds. The type is still taken from\n      // the shipped vocabulary rather than spelled as a literal.\n      const accountType: AccountType = 'platform_admin';\n      state.accounts.set(session.accountId, { id: session.accountId, accountType });\n      return session;\n    },\n  };\n\n  return {\n    sut: { accounts: sut },\n    fixtures: {\n      world: async (name: string) => {\n        const base = await worlds.world(name);\n        const world = new AccountsFixtureWorld(base, ++worldSerial);\n        openedWorlds.add(world);\n        return world;\n      },\n    },\n    teardown: async () => {\n      await Promise.all([...openedWorlds].map((world) => world.teardown()));\n      openedWorlds.clear();\n      state.authUsers.clear();\n      state.byEmail.clear();\n      state.byVerificationLink.clear();\n      state.accounts.clear();\n      state.organizations.clear();\n      state.memberships.clear();\n      state.acknowledgments.length = 0;\n      state.volunteerProfiles.clear();\n      state.discoveryMessages.clear();\n      state.nextId = 1;\n    },\n  };\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":661,"totalLines":661,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_fixture.ts","time":{"start":1786290434558,"end":1786290434571}},"id":"prt_fe734f0ed001AMHn7PtydLkLnQ","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe734da68001vMIP14G4j0Ngat"}}
HEAD:loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10252:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:33:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290444182,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_wof8OsVw4YgOgoFgxgmI3660","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_contract.ts"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_contract.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /**\n2:  * What REQ-001 adds to the shared harness contract.\n3:  *\n4:  * The generic seams — tier, clock, fixture worlds, sentinels, faults, static scan, config, vendor\n5:  * sims, the harness shape itself — live in `tests/at/harness/contracts.ts` and are re-exported here\n6:  * so a test body imports one file. What is genuinely REQ-001's is below: the accounts system under\n7:  * test, the row shapes its assertions read back, and what this requirement's fixture world can do.\n8:  *\n9:  * TYPE ALIASES, NOT INTERFACES, throughout — the rule `harness/contracts.ts` states and gives its\n10:  * reasons for. Nothing reachable from the objects `open()` hands a test body may be an interface,\n11:  * because an interface can be reopened with `declare module` and an OPTIONAL member merged into it\n12:  * reads `undefined` at run time while every type-check stays green.\n13:  *\n14:  * THE JUDGEMENT TYPES ARE IMPORTED FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE, not restated here. `AccountType`,\n15:  * `CompleteSignupRequest` and the rest come from `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, which is\n16:  * the same module the two edge functions import. Restating them would be two independent statements\n17:  * about one thing with nothing able to notice them diverging — and that is the defect this whole\n18:  * harness exists to delete, not a shape to reproduce inside it.\n19:  */\n20: \n21: import type { WorldSeam } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\n22: import type {\n23:   AccountType,\n24:   CompleteSignupRequest,\n25: } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n26: \n27: export type {\n28:   Clock,\n29:   ConfigRegistry,\n30:   FaultHandle,\n31:   Faults,\n32:   Fixtures,\n33:   ProviderOutcome,\n34:   Sentinel,\n35:   Sentinels,\n36:   StaticScan,\n37:   Tier,\n38:   WorldSeam,\n39: } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\n40: export { TIERS } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\n41: \n42: export type { AccountType, CompleteSignupRequest };\n43: \n44: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- what gets read back */\n45: \n46: /** One row of `public.accounts` — one per authenticated user, holding exactly one global type. */\n47: export type AccountRow = {\n48:   id: string;\n49:   accountType: AccountType;\n50: };\n51: \n52: /** One row of `public.organizations`. */\n53: export type OrganizationRow = {\n54:   id: string;\n55:   name: string;\n56: };\n57: \n58: /** One row of `public.org_memberships` — the per-NGO role, which is NOT the global account type. */\n59: export type MembershipRow = {\n60:   organizationId: string;\n61:   accountId: string;\n62:   role: 'admin' | 'member';\n63: };\n64: \n65: /**\n66:  * One row of `public.acknowledgments` — EXACTLY the three fields AT-001.01 names recorded, plus the\n67:  * account and kind that identify it.\n68:  *\n69:  * Name, title and authority attestation are AT-001.19's fields and belong to the acknowledgment-\n70:  * identity deliverable (`loop/decomp/req-001.md` D4.L1), which is declared red by this leaf. They\n71:  * are deliberately absent here: adding columns for a criterion this leaf does not test would be\n72:  * speculation, and a reader would have no way to tell it from work that had been done.\n73:  */\n74: export type AcknowledgmentRow = {\n75:   accountId: string;\n76:   kind: string;\n77:   /** ISO-8601 instant — AT-001.01's \"timestamp\" */\n78:   acknowledgedAt: string;\n79:   /**\n80:    * AT-001.01's \"IP\": the address the gateway chain REPORTED, never a verified source address.\n81:    * Measured on the live local stack — a spoofed `x-forwarded-for` is stored verbatim, and with no\n82:    * header at all the stored value is the gateway's own hop. See `callerIp` in\n83:    * `supabase/functions/_shared/edge.ts`.\n84:    */\n85:   ip: string;\n86:   /** which version of the ToS + Platform Promise text was accepted — AT-001.01's \"text version\" */\n87:   textVersion: string;\n88: };\n89: \n90: /**\n91:  * One row of `public.volunteer_profiles` — AT-001.05's \"profile\", and every column of it.\n92:  *\n93:  * THE STATS ARE THE STUB IMPORT FIXTURE'S, NOT GITHUB'S. `stubGithubStatsFor` in\n94:  * `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` produces them, the decomposition manifest's cross-contract\n95:  * ratifies that stand-in (\"stub import fixture until W3\"), and no code in this item calls\n96:  * api.github.com. What the row proves is that onboarding FIRED and landed POPULATED — the\n97:  * criterion's \"a queued-but-empty import fails this test\" — and not that any statistic is real.\n98:  */\n99: export type VolunteerProfileRow = {\n100:   accountId: string;\n101:   /** the handle of the GitHub identity linked to the account — AT-001.05's \"linked handle\" */\n102:   githubHandle: string;\n103:   /** AT-001.05's \"top languages\"; never empty, in the schema by CHECK and here by assertion */\n104:   topLanguages: string[];\n105:   /** AT-001.05's \"repository count\" */\n106:   repositoryCount: number;\n107:   /** AT-001.05's \"contribution summary\" */\n108:   contributionSummary: string;\n109:   /** ISO-8601 instant the import landed */\n110:   importedAt: string;\n111: };\n112: \n113: /* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the session */\n114: \n115: /**\n116:  * An authenticated session, and WHICH PROVIDER ESTABLISHED IT.\n117:  *\n118:  * `provider` is recorded, never simulated. Supabase Auth records the provider that produced a\n119:  * session; how the session was obtained — an email and a password, or a completed Google consent\n120:  * round trip — happens entirely upstream of anything this requirement's leaf ships. AT-001.03's\n121:  * body asserts that `completeSignup` behaves identically for `'google'` and `'email'`, which is a\n122:  * real property of the shipped decision module (it never reads this field). It is NOT a claim that a\n123:  * Google round trip works: see the suite's own note on AT-001.03, and the plan's per-id table.\n124:  *\n125:  * `'github'` joins the two for AT-001.02 and carries exactly the same caveat: a github-established\n126:  * session is the state Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, and no handshake is simulated to reach\n127:  * it. The establishing provider still participates in NO decision the shipped modules make — the\n128:  * volunteer gate reads a LINKED IDENTITY, which is a different fact (see `linkGithubIdentity`).\n129:  */\n130: export type SessionProvider = 'email' | 'google' | 'github';\n131: \n132: export type Session = {\n133:   /** the auth user id, which is also the `public.accounts` primary key once signup completes */\n134:   accountId: string;\n135:   email: string;\n136:   provider: SessionProvider;\n137: };\n138: \n139: /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the outcomes */\n140: \n141: export type SignInOutcome = { ok: true; session: Session } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n142: \n143: export type CompleteSignupOutcome =\n144:   | { ok: true; accountId: string; organizationId: string | null }\n145:   | { ok: false; reason: string };\n146: \n147: export type CreateOrganizationOutcome = { ok: true; organizationId: string } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n148: \n149: /**\n150:  * The outcome of an attempted Discovery message — and the refusal carries WHY, for the reason\n151:  * `Decision` in the shipped module gives: AT-001.10 asserts that the block NAMES verification as\n152:  * the remedy, so a bare boolean would make the criterion untestable.\n153:  */\n154: export type SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome = { ok: true } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n155: \n156: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the SUT */\n157: \n158: /**\n159:  * REQ-001's accounts system under test.\n160:  *\n161:  * The split is the point, and there are now FOUR kinds of member here rather than three:\n162:  *\n163:  *   1. THE TWO PRODUCT OPERATIONS — `completeSignup` and `createOrganization`. At loop tier the\n164:  *      adapter runs them over its own storage but delegates every judgement to\n165:  *      `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, the module the edge functions import; at\n166:  *      integration tier they would be the deployed edge functions.\n167:  *   2. SUPABASE AUTH'S HALF — registration, sign-in, identity linking, and the verification state\n168:  *      (`emailVerified`, `emailedVerificationLink`, `useVerificationLink`). None of it is this\n169:  *      requirement's code. It is what produces the states the product operations judge.\n170:  *   3. READ-BACK — the row shapes an assertion reads after the fact.\n171:  *   4. ONE STAND-IN SURFACE, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE — `sendDiscoveryMessage`, with\n172:  *      `discoveryMessagesBy` reading it back.\n173:  *\n174:  * THE FOURTH KIND NEEDS ITS OWN SENTENCE, because it is the one that could be misread. NO\n175:  * DISCOVERY ROUTE EXISTS IN THIS TREE. The Discovery message route is REQ-002/004's, and what\n176:  * this requirement's leaf ships is the DECISION that route must consult —\n177:  * `discoveryMessageAllowed` in `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`. So\n178:  * `sendDiscoveryMessage` is not a deployed operation being tested; it is a stand-in surface whose\n179:  * only job is to put the shipped decision on a tested path, exactly as\n180:  * `hasPlatformAcknowledgment` below is the hook a project-creation leaf must call. A green over\n181:  * it says the DECISION is right. It says nothing about enforcement anywhere, because there is\n182:  * nowhere yet to enforce it.\n183:  */\n184: export type AccountsSut = {\n185:   /** AT-001.07's second clause: which types the PUBLIC signup surface offers. */\n186:   publicSignupAccountTypes(): Promise<readonly string[]>;\n187: \n188:   /* --- Supabase Auth's half. Not this leaf's code; it is what produces a session to complete. --- */\n189: \n190:   /**\n191:    * Register an auth user with an email and a password, and return the resulting session.\n192:    *\n193:    * IT STILL RETURNS A SESSION, AND THE HONEST GAP THAT LEAVES IS STATED HERE RATHER THAN HIDDEN.\n194:    * With `enable_confirmations = true` the live GoTrue issues NO session at signup, so on the real\n195:    * stack a completed-but-unverified account is not reachable by the public path. This fixture's\n196:    * `Session` has always been an identity handle rather than an access token, and session ISSUANCE\n197:    * is D2.L2's subject (AT-001.12, .13 — declared red). So at the loop tier such an account IS\n198:    * constructible, and AT-001.10's body constructs one deliberately: the gate exists because\n199:    * decision-8 makes verification the write path's own floor, not a property borrowed from the\n200:    * session layer. The live-stack behaviour is measured in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`\n201:    * checks (a) and (c), and nowhere else in this item.\n202:    */\n203:   registerWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;\n204:   /**\n205:    * A session Auth recorded as having come from an external provider.\n206:    *\n207:    * It performs NO handshake and fabricates no authorization code or token exchange: it is the state\n208:    * Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, which is the only part of the story any agent can reach.\n209:    */\n210:   registerWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<Session>;\n211:   /**\n212:    * A GitHub OAuth signup, as Auth records it: a session established by GitHub whose account already\n213:    * carries the linked GitHub identity — AT-001.02's \"signs up via GitHub OAuth\".\n214:    *\n215:    * Like `registerWithProvider` it performs NO handshake. It is `registerWithProvider('github', …)`\n216:    * plus the fact that a GitHub signup necessarily links the GitHub identity it signed up with, and\n217:    * it is a separate member rather than an argument because that second half is what AT-001.02's\n218:    * \"the GitHub identity is linked to it\" clause is about.\n219:    */\n220:   registerWithGithub(email: string, githubHandle: string): Promise<Session>;\n221:   /**\n222:    * The state Auth is in after a `linkIdentity` round trip — AT-001.04's \"linking completes signup\".\n223:    *\n224:    * A LINKED IDENTITY IS NOT THE ESTABLISHING PROVIDER, and keeping the two apart is the point of\n225:    * this member existing at all: an email- or Google-established volunteer links GitHub and their\n226:    * session's `provider` does not change. The gate in\n227:    * `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts` reads the link; nothing shipped reads the provider.\n228:    *\n229:    * Same posture as every other Auth member here — no handshake, no fabricated authorization code.\n230:    */\n231:   linkGithubIdentity(session: Session, githubHandle: string): Promise<void>;\n232:   /** Return sign-in with the same credentials — AT-001.01's final clause. */\n233:   signInWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;\n234:   /**\n235:    * The return visit through an external provider — AT-001.02's \"a later sign-in via GitHub returns\n236:    * to the same account\".\n237:    *\n238:    * What it asserts is IDENTITY CONTINUITY, which is the half of that clause reachable without a\n239:    * person: an existing user whose account carries that provider's identity signs in to the SAME\n240:    * account rather than to a second one. The consent round trip itself is not performed and is named\n241:    * unproved in the plan's per-id table.\n242:    */\n243:   signInWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;\n244: \n245:   /* ------------------------- Supabase Auth's verification state ------------------------------- */\n246: \n247:   /**\n248:    * Whether Supabase Auth reports this account's email address confirmed — AT-001.09's observable.\n249:    *\n250:    * IT IS DERIVED, NOT STORED-AND-READ. The adapter renders the account's stored Auth state as the\n251:    * canonical GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` shape and judges the answer out of it with the SHIPPED\n252:    * `emailVerifiedFromUser`. That is the same pattern `completeSignup` uses for the GitHub handle\n253:    * (the GitHub leaf's gate-2 ruling R3), and it exists so the shipped extractor sits on the tested\n254:    * path rather than being a function no test ever runs — which is exactly how the future Discovery\n255:    * route will resolve the same fact from the same response.\n256:    *\n257:    * WHAT NO LOOP-TIER GREEN OVER IT PROVES: that the rendered shape is the shape GoTrue really\n258:    * sends. Only `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` check (d) touches that, by feeding a real\n259:    * response to the same shipped function.\n260:    */\n261:   emailVerified(accountId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n262:   /**\n263:    * The verification link emailed to this address, or `null` when none was emailed.\n264:    *\n265:    * `null` is a real answer rather than only the empty case: a provider registration is confirmed\n266:    * by the provider and no confirmation email is sent for it.\n267:    */\n268:   emailedVerificationLink(email: string): Promise<string | null>;\n269:   /**\n270:    * Use a verification link. Using the link Auth emailed for an address confirms that address; a\n271:    * link that was never issued returns `ok: false` and flips nothing.\n272:    *\n273:    * NO EXPIRY, NO SINGLE USE AND NO RESEND IS MODELLED OR ASSERTED. AT-001.11 is retired — the\n274:    * acceptance file's own words are that \"verification-link expiry/single-use/resend semantics are\n275:    * not stated in REQ-001\" — so building any of them here would be asserting a criterion that was\n276:    * deliberately withdrawn.\n277:    *\n278:    * THE NEVER-ISSUED NEGATIVE IS NOT AT-001.11 GROUND, and the difference is worth being exact\n279:    * about. It is not a claim about a link's lifetime; it guards this test's own oracle. A\n280:    * link-shaped string that verified an account it never belonged to would make \"using the emailed\n281:    * link is what flips it\" mean nothing at all, because any string would flip anything.\n282:    */\n283:   useVerificationLink(link: string): Promise<{ ok: boolean }>;\n284: \n285:   /* ----------------------------------- the two product operations ----------------------------- */\n286: \n287:   /** `supabase/functions/complete-signup` — turns an authenticated auth user into a typed account. */\n288:   completeSignup(session: Session, request: CompleteSignupRequest, ip: string): Promise<CompleteSignupOutcome>;\n289:   /** `supabase/functions/create-organization` — the NGO-only action AT-001.06 drives. */\n290:   createOrganization(session: Session, organizationName: string): Promise<CreateOrganizationOutcome>;\n291: \n292:   /* --------------------------- the Discovery gate's stand-in surface -------------------------- */\n293: \n294:   /**\n295:    * Attempt to send a Discovery message — AT-001.10's action.\n296:    *\n297:    * THERE IS NO DEPLOYED ROUTE BEHIND THIS, at either tier. See the fourth kind in this type's own\n298:    * header: the Discovery route is REQ-002/004's and does not exist, so this member is a stand-in\n299:    * surface that exists to put the SHIPPED `discoveryMessageAllowed` on a tested path. At loop\n300:    * tier the adapter derives the caller's verified fact through the shipped\n301:    * `emailVerifiedFromUser`, consults the shipped gate, and refuses with the GATE's own reason.\n302:    *\n303:    * THE BODY IS AN OPAQUE STRING AND NOTHING ELSE. Recipients, threads, attachments and message\n304:    * state are REQ-002/004's semantics; inventing any of them here to make this member look real\n305:    * would be building another requirement's surface early. What AT-001.10 needs is an attempted\n306:    * write and a way to see whether it happened, and one string supplies both.\n307:    */\n308:   sendDiscoveryMessage(session: Session, body: string): Promise<SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome>;\n309:   /**\n310:    * The Discovery message bodies this account has sent — read-back, and it is not optional.\n311:    *\n312:    * A BLOCK WHOSE WRITE HAPPENED ANYWAY IS NOT A BLOCK. The refusal's own return value cannot show\n313:    * that nothing was written, for the same reason `organizationsNamed` exists: a refusal hands\n314:    * back no identifier, so the row that must NOT be there can only be looked for by who would have\n315:    * written it.\n316:    */\n317:   discoveryMessagesBy(accountId: string): Promise<string[]>;\n318: \n319:   /* ------------------------------------------- read-back -------------------------------------- */\n320: \n321:   account(accountId: string): Promise<AccountRow | null>;\n322:   organization(organizationId: string): Promise<OrganizationRow | null>;\n323:   membership(organizationId: string, accountId: string): Promise<MembershipRow | null>;\n324:   acknowledgments(accountId: string): Promise<AcknowledgmentRow[]>;\n325:   /**\n326:    * The volunteer's imported GitHub profile, or `null` — AT-001.05's observable.\n327:    *\n328:    * `null` is a real and asserted answer, not merely the empty case: AT-001.05's pre-completion\n329:    * negative reads it AFTER the identity is linked and BEFORE the signup completes, and requires\n330:    * null there. That is what proves the population is CAUSED by completion rather than sitting in a\n331:    * queue somewhere waiting to be drained.\n332:    */\n333:   volunteerProfile(accountId: string): Promise<VolunteerProfileRow | null>;\n334: \n335:   /**\n336:    * The two read-backs a REFUSED action needs, which the keyed ones above cannot serve.\n337:    *\n338:    * Asserting that a rejection wrote nothing means looking for rows whose identifiers were never\n339:    * handed out — a refused `createOrganization` returns no organisation id. So one search by the\n340:    * name that was attempted, and one listing of everything an account is a member of. Without them\n341:    * the strongest statement available is \"the call said no\", and an implementation that writes both\n342:    * rows and then says no would satisfy it.\n343:    */\n344:   organizationsNamed(name: string): Promise<OrganizationRow[]>;\n345:   membershipsOf(accountId: string): Promise<MembershipRow[]>;\n346:   /**\n347:    * `public.has_platform_acknowledgment(account_id)` — the observable form of AT-001.01's \"before\n348:    * any project creation is possible\".\n349:    *\n350:    * It MUST DISCRIMINATE: false for an authenticated user who has not completed signup, true after.\n351:    * The body asserts both halves for exactly that reason.\n352:    *\n353:    * WHICH IMPLEMENTATION THAT ASSERTION REACHES depends on the tier, and the difference matters. At\n354:    * loop tier it reaches the fixture adapter's storage query, so what goes green is the RULE and the\n355:    * adapter's storage — the shipped SQL function could return true unconditionally and the loop tier\n356:    * would not notice. `public.has_platform_acknowledgment` itself is proved by step 7(h) of the\n357:    * plan, against the live database, and by nothing else in this item.\n358:    *\n359:    * What this leaf cannot do at either tier is ENFORCE the clause — nothing in the tree creates a\n360:    * project, and building project creation belongs to another requirement. This is the hook the leaf\n361:    * that lands project creation must call.\n362:    */\n363:   hasPlatformAcknowledgment(accountId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n364: \n365:   /* ------------------------------------ the provisioned admin --------------------------------- */\n366: \n367:   /**\n368:    * Provision a `platform_admin` — the ONLY legal way one exists, because the public path refuses\n369:    * the type. It sits apart from `completeSignup` rather than being one of its options for that\n370:    * reason.\n371:    *\n372:    * ON THE LIVE STACK THIS IS **NOT** A SERVICE-ROLE WRITE, and the correction matters more than\n373:    * most: this comment used to say it was, and a later reader following it would have granted the\n374:    * service role an INSERT on `public.accounts` — a write path straight past `complete_signup`'s\n375:    * `platform_admin` refusal, which is the one guard that sits on the only write path. The service\n376:    * role holds no INSERT anywhere in this schema, by measurement and by decision. Provisioning is a\n377:    * direct database operation by an operator: a NARROWER authority than the service role, not a\n378:    * wider one, and not one any running service holds.\n379:    */\n380:   provisionPlatformAdmin(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;\n381: };\n382: \n383: /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the world */\n384: \n385: /**\n386:  * What REQ-001's fixture world adds to the shared seam.\n387:  *\n388:  * An INTERSECTION, not `interface World extends WorldSeam`, for the alias reason above.\n389:  *\n390:  * One member, and it earns its place: every test in this suite registers auth users, and two tests\n391:  * that happened to pick the same address would interfere in a way that looks like a product defect.\n392:  * `email()` namespaces an address to the world that issued it, so isolation is structural rather\n393:  * than a convention each body has to remember.\n394:  */\n395: export type World = WorldSeam & {\n396:   /** a mail address unique to THIS world, e.g. `email('ngo')` */\n397:   email(local: string): string;\n398: };\n\n(End of file - total 398 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/**\n * What REQ-001 adds to the shared harness contract.\n *\n * The generic seams — tier, clock, fixture worlds, sentinels, faults, static scan, config, vendor\n * sims, the harness shape itself — live in `tests/at/harness/contracts.ts` and are re-exported here\n * so a test body imports one file. What is genuinely REQ-001's is below: the accounts system under\n * test, the row shapes its assertions read back, and what this requirement's fixture world can do.\n *\n * TYPE ALIASES, NOT INTERFACES, throughout — the rule `harness/contracts.ts` states and gives its\n * reasons for. Nothing reachable from the objects `open()` hands a test body may be an interface,\n * because an interface can be reopened with `declare module` and an OPTIONAL member merged into it\n * reads `undefined` at run time while every type-check stays green.\n *\n * THE JUDGEMENT TYPES ARE IMPORTED FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE, not restated here. `AccountType`,\n * `CompleteSignupRequest` and the rest come from `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, which is\n * the same module the two edge functions import. Restating them would be two independent statements\n * about one thing with nothing able to notice them diverging — and that is the defect this whole\n * harness exists to delete, not a shape to reproduce inside it.\n */\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_contract.ts","text":"/**\n * What REQ-001 adds to the shared harness contract.\n *\n * The generic seams — tier, clock, fixture worlds, sentinels, faults, static scan, config, vendor\n * sims, the harness shape itself — live in `tests/at/harness/contracts.ts` and are re-exported here\n * so a test body imports one file. What is genuinely REQ-001's is below: the accounts system under\n * test, the row shapes its assertions read back, and what this requirement's fixture world can do.\n *\n * TYPE ALIASES, NOT INTERFACES, throughout — the rule `harness/contracts.ts` states and gives its\n * reasons for. Nothing reachable from the objects `open()` hands a test body may be an interface,\n * because an interface can be reopened with `declare module` and an OPTIONAL member merged into it\n * reads `undefined` at run time while every type-check stays green.\n *\n * THE JUDGEMENT TYPES ARE IMPORTED FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE, not restated here. `AccountType`,\n * `CompleteSignupRequest` and the rest come from `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, which is\n * the same module the two edge functions import. Restating them would be two independent statements\n * about one thing with nothing able to notice them diverging — and that is the defect this whole\n * harness exists to delete, not a shape to reproduce inside it.\n */\n\nimport type { WorldSeam } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\nimport type {\n  AccountType,\n  CompleteSignupRequest,\n} from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n\nexport type {\n  Clock,\n  ConfigRegistry,\n  FaultHandle,\n  Faults,\n  Fixtures,\n  ProviderOutcome,\n  Sentinel,\n  Sentinels,\n  StaticScan,\n  Tier,\n  WorldSeam,\n} from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\nexport { TIERS } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\n\nexport type { AccountType, CompleteSignupRequest };\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- what gets read back */\n\n/** One row of `public.accounts` — one per authenticated user, holding exactly one global type. */\nexport type AccountRow = {\n  id: string;\n  accountType: AccountType;\n};\n\n/** One row of `public.organizations`. */\nexport type OrganizationRow = {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n};\n\n/** One row of `public.org_memberships` — the per-NGO role, which is NOT the global account type. */\nexport type MembershipRow = {\n  organizationId: string;\n  accountId: string;\n  role: 'admin' | 'member';\n};\n\n/**\n * One row of `public.acknowledgments` — EXACTLY the three fields AT-001.01 names recorded, plus the\n * account and kind that identify it.\n *\n * Name, title and authority attestation are AT-001.19's fields and belong to the acknowledgment-\n * identity deliverable (`loop/decomp/req-001.md` D4.L1), which is declared red by this leaf. They\n * are deliberately absent here: adding columns for a criterion this leaf does not test would be\n * speculation, and a reader would have no way to tell it from work that had been done.\n */\nexport type AcknowledgmentRow = {\n  accountId: string;\n  kind: string;\n  /** ISO-8601 instant — AT-001.01's \"timestamp\" */\n  acknowledgedAt: string;\n  /**\n   * AT-001.01's \"IP\": the address the gateway chain REPORTED, never a verified source address.\n   * Measured on the live local stack — a spoofed `x-forwarded-for` is stored verbatim, and with no\n   * header at all the stored value is the gateway's own hop. See `callerIp` in\n   * `supabase/functions/_shared/edge.ts`.\n   */\n  ip: string;\n  /** which version of the ToS + Platform Promise text was accepted — AT-001.01's \"text version\" */\n  textVersion: string;\n};\n\n/**\n * One row of `public.volunteer_profiles` — AT-001.05's \"profile\", and every column of it.\n *\n * THE STATS ARE THE STUB IMPORT FIXTURE'S, NOT GITHUB'S. `stubGithubStatsFor` in\n * `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` produces them, the decomposition manifest's cross-contract\n * ratifies that stand-in (\"stub import fixture until W3\"), and no code in this item calls\n * api.github.com. What the row proves is that onboarding FIRED and landed POPULATED — the\n * criterion's \"a queued-but-empty import fails this test\" — and not that any statistic is real.\n */\nexport type VolunteerProfileRow = {\n  accountId: string;\n  /** the handle of the GitHub identity linked to the account — AT-001.05's \"linked handle\" */\n  githubHandle: string;\n  /** AT-001.05's \"top languages\"; never empty, in the schema by CHECK and here by assertion */\n  topLanguages: string[];\n  /** AT-001.05's \"repository count\" */\n  repositoryCount: number;\n  /** AT-001.05's \"contribution summary\" */\n  contributionSummary: string;\n  /** ISO-8601 instant the import landed */\n  importedAt: string;\n};\n\n/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the session */\n\n/**\n * An authenticated session, and WHICH PROVIDER ESTABLISHED IT.\n *\n * `provider` is recorded, never simulated. Supabase Auth records the provider that produced a\n * session; how the session was obtained — an email and a password, or a completed Google consent\n * round trip — happens entirely upstream of anything this requirement's leaf ships. AT-001.03's\n * body asserts that `completeSignup` behaves identically for `'google'` and `'email'`, which is a\n * real property of the shipped decision module (it never reads this field). It is NOT a claim that a\n * Google round trip works: see the suite's own note on AT-001.03, and the plan's per-id table.\n *\n * `'github'` joins the two for AT-001.02 and carries exactly the same caveat: a github-established\n * session is the state Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, and no handshake is simulated to reach\n * it. The establishing provider still participates in NO decision the shipped modules make — the\n * volunteer gate reads a LINKED IDENTITY, which is a different fact (see `linkGithubIdentity`).\n */\nexport type SessionProvider = 'email' | 'google' | 'github';\n\nexport type Session = {\n  /** the auth user id, which is also the `public.accounts` primary key once signup completes */\n  accountId: string;\n  email: string;\n  provider: SessionProvider;\n};\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the outcomes */\n\nexport type SignInOutcome = { ok: true; session: Session } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n\nexport type CompleteSignupOutcome =\n  | { ok: true; accountId: string; organizationId: string | null }\n  | { ok: false; reason: string };\n\nexport type CreateOrganizationOutcome = { ok: true; organizationId: string } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n\n/**\n * The outcome of an attempted Discovery message — and the refusal carries WHY, for the reason\n * `Decision` in the shipped module gives: AT-001.10 asserts that the block NAMES verification as\n * the remedy, so a bare boolean would make the criterion untestable.\n */\nexport type SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome = { ok: true } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the SUT */\n\n/**\n * REQ-001's accounts system under test.\n *\n * The split is the point, and there are now FOUR kinds of member here rather than three:\n *\n *   1. THE TWO PRODUCT OPERATIONS — `completeSignup` and `createOrganization`. At loop tier the\n *      adapter runs them over its own storage but delegates every judgement to\n *      `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, the module the edge functions import; at\n *      integration tier they would be the deployed edge functions.\n *   2. SUPABASE AUTH'S HALF — registration, sign-in, identity linking, and the verification state\n *      (`emailVerified`, `emailedVerificationLink`, `useVerificationLink`). None of it is this\n *      requirement's code. It is what produces the states the product operations judge.\n *   3. READ-BACK — the row shapes an assertion reads after the fact.\n *   4. ONE STAND-IN SURFACE, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE — `sendDiscoveryMessage`, with\n *      `discoveryMessagesBy` reading it back.\n *\n * THE FOURTH KIND NEEDS ITS OWN SENTENCE, because it is the one that could be misread. NO\n * DISCOVERY ROUTE EXISTS IN THIS TREE. The Discovery message route is REQ-002/004's, and what\n * this requirement's leaf ships is the DECISION that route must consult —\n * `discoveryMessageAllowed` in `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`. So\n * `sendDiscoveryMessage` is not a deployed operation being tested; it is a stand-in surface whose\n * only job is to put the shipped decision on a tested path, exactly as\n * `hasPlatformAcknowledgment` below is the hook a project-creation leaf must call. A green over\n * it says the DECISION is right. It says nothing about enforcement anywhere, because there is\n * nowhere yet to enforce it.\n */\nexport type AccountsSut = {\n  /** AT-001.07's second clause: which types the PUBLIC signup surface offers. */\n  publicSignupAccountTypes(): Promise<readonly string[]>;\n\n  /* --- Supabase Auth's half. Not this leaf's code; it is what produces a session to complete. --- */\n\n  /**\n   * Register an auth user with an email and a password, and return the resulting session.\n   *\n   * IT STILL RETURNS A SESSION, AND THE HONEST GAP THAT LEAVES IS STATED HERE RATHER THAN HIDDEN.\n   * With `enable_confirmations = true` the live GoTrue issues NO session at signup, so on the real\n   * stack a completed-but-unverified account is not reachable by the public path. This fixture's\n   * `Session` has always been an identity handle rather than an access token, and session ISSUANCE\n   * is D2.L2's subject (AT-001.12, .13 — declared red). So at the loop tier such an account IS\n   * constructible, and AT-001.10's body constructs one deliberately: the gate exists because\n   * decision-8 makes verification the write path's own floor, not a property borrowed from the\n   * session layer. The live-stack behaviour is measured in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`\n   * checks (a) and (c), and nowhere else in this item.\n   */\n  registerWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;\n  /**\n   * A session Auth recorded as having come from an external provider.\n   *\n   * It performs NO handshake and fabricates no authorization code or token exchange: it is the state\n   * Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, which is the only part of the story any agent can reach.\n   */\n  registerWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<Session>;\n  /**\n   * A GitHub OAuth signup, as Auth records it: a session established by GitHub whose account already\n   * carries the linked GitHub identity — AT-001.02's \"signs up via GitHub OAuth\".\n   *\n   * Like `registerWithProvider` it performs NO handshake. It is `registerWithProvider('github', …)`\n   * plus the fact that a GitHub signup necessarily links the GitHub identity it signed up with, and\n   * it is a separate member rather than an argument because that second half is what AT-001.02's\n   * \"the GitHub identity is linked to it\" clause is about.\n   */\n  registerWithGithub(email: string, githubHandle: string): Promise<Session>;\n  /**\n   * The state Auth is in after a `linkIdentity` round trip — AT-001.04's \"linking completes signup\".\n   *\n   * A LINKED IDENTITY IS NOT THE ESTABLISHING PROVIDER, and keeping the two apart is the point of\n   * this member existing at all: an email- or Google-established volunteer links GitHub and their\n   * session's `provider` does not change. The gate in\n   * `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts` reads the link; nothing shipped reads the provider.\n   *\n   * Same posture as every other Auth member here — no handshake, no fabricated authorization code.\n   */\n  linkGithubIdentity(session: Session, githubHandle: string): Promise<void>;\n  /** Return sign-in with the same credentials — AT-001.01's final clause. */\n  signInWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;\n  /**\n   * The return visit through an external provider — AT-001.02's \"a later sign-in via GitHub returns\n   * to the same account\".\n   *\n   * What it asserts is IDENTITY CONTINUITY, which is the half of that clause reachable without a\n   * person: an existing user whose account carries that provider's identity signs in to the SAME\n   * account rather than to a second one. The consent round trip itself is not performed and is named\n   * unproved in the plan's per-id table.\n   */\n  signInWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;\n\n  /* ------------------------- Supabase Auth's verification state ------------------------------- */\n\n  /**\n   * Whether Supabase Auth reports this account's email address confirmed — AT-001.09's observable.\n   *\n   * IT IS DERIVED, NOT STORED-AND-READ. The adapter renders the account's stored Auth state as the\n   * canonical GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` shape and judges the answer out of it with the SHIPPED\n   * `emailVerifiedFromUser`. That is the same pattern `completeSignup` uses for the GitHub handle\n   * (the GitHub leaf's gate-2 ruling R3), and it exists so the shipped extractor sits on the tested\n   * path rather than being a function no test ever runs — which is exactly how the future Discovery\n   * route will resolve the same fact from the same response.\n   *\n   * WHAT NO LOOP-TIER GREEN OVER IT PROVES: that the rendered shape is the shape GoTrue really\n   * sends. Only `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` check (d) touches that, by feeding a real\n   * response to the same shipped function.\n   */\n  emailVerified(accountId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n  /**\n   * The verification link emailed to this address, or `null` when none was emailed.\n   *\n   * `null` is a real answer rather than only the empty case: a provider registration is confirmed\n   * by the provider and no confirmation email is sent for it.\n   */\n  emailedVerificationLink(email: string): Promise<string | null>;\n  /**\n   * Use a verification link. Using the link Auth emailed for an address confirms that address; a\n   * link that was never issued returns `ok: false` and flips nothing.\n   *\n   * NO EXPIRY, NO SINGLE USE AND NO RESEND IS MODELLED OR ASSERTED. AT-001.11 is retired — the\n   * acceptance file's own words are that \"verification-link expiry/single-use/resend semantics are\n   * not stated in REQ-001\" — so building any of them here would be asserting a criterion that was\n   * deliberately withdrawn.\n   *\n   * THE NEVER-ISSUED NEGATIVE IS NOT AT-001.11 GROUND, and the difference is worth being exact\n   * about. It is not a claim about a link's lifetime; it guards this test's own oracle. A\n   * link-shaped string that verified an account it never belonged to would make \"using the emailed\n   * link is what flips it\" mean nothing at all, because any string would flip anything.\n   */\n  useVerificationLink(link: string): Promise<{ ok: boolean }>;\n\n  /* ----------------------------------- the two product operations ----------------------------- */\n\n  /** `supabase/functions/complete-signup` — turns an authenticated auth user into a typed account. */\n  completeSignup(session: Session, request: CompleteSignupRequest, ip: string): Promise<CompleteSignupOutcome>;\n  /** `supabase/functions/create-organization` — the NGO-only action AT-001.06 drives. */\n  createOrganization(session: Session, organizationName: string): Promise<CreateOrganizationOutcome>;\n\n  /* --------------------------- the Discovery gate's stand-in surface -------------------------- */\n\n  /**\n   * Attempt to send a Discovery message — AT-001.10's action.\n   *\n   * THERE IS NO DEPLOYED ROUTE BEHIND THIS, at either tier. See the fourth kind in this type's own\n   * header: the Discovery route is REQ-002/004's and does not exist, so this member is a stand-in\n   * surface that exists to put the SHIPPED `discoveryMessageAllowed` on a tested path. At loop\n   * tier the adapter derives the caller's verified fact through the shipped\n   * `emailVerifiedFromUser`, consults the shipped gate, and refuses with the GATE's own reason.\n   *\n   * THE BODY IS AN OPAQUE STRING AND NOTHING ELSE. Recipients, threads, attachments and message\n   * state are REQ-002/004's semantics; inventing any of them here to make this member look real\n   * would be building another requirement's surface early. What AT-001.10 needs is an attempted\n   * write and a way to see whether it happened, and one string supplies both.\n   */\n  sendDiscoveryMessage(session: Session, body: string): Promise<SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome>;\n  /**\n   * The Discovery message bodies this account has sent — read-back, and it is not optional.\n   *\n   * A BLOCK WHOSE WRITE HAPPENED ANYWAY IS NOT A BLOCK. The refusal's own return value cannot show\n   * that nothing was written, for the same reason `organizationsNamed` exists: a refusal hands\n   * back no identifier, so the row that must NOT be there can only be looked for by who would have\n   * written it.\n   */\n  discoveryMessagesBy(accountId: string): Promise<string[]>;\n\n  /* ------------------------------------------- read-back -------------------------------------- */\n\n  account(accountId: string): Promise<AccountRow | null>;\n  organization(organizationId: string): Promise<OrganizationRow | null>;\n  membership(organizationId: string, accountId: string): Promise<MembershipRow | null>;\n  acknowledgments(accountId: string): Promise<AcknowledgmentRow[]>;\n  /**\n   * The volunteer's imported GitHub profile, or `null` — AT-001.05's observable.\n   *\n   * `null` is a real and asserted answer, not merely the empty case: AT-001.05's pre-completion\n   * negative reads it AFTER the identity is linked and BEFORE the signup completes, and requires\n   * null there. That is what proves the population is CAUSED by completion rather than sitting in a\n   * queue somewhere waiting to be drained.\n   */\n  volunteerProfile(accountId: string): Promise<VolunteerProfileRow | null>;\n\n  /**\n   * The two read-backs a REFUSED action needs, which the keyed ones above cannot serve.\n   *\n   * Asserting that a rejection wrote nothing means looking for rows whose identifiers were never\n   * handed out — a refused `createOrganization` returns no organisation id. So one search by the\n   * name that was attempted, and one listing of everything an account is a member of. Without them\n   * the strongest statement available is \"the call said no\", and an implementation that writes both\n   * rows and then says no would satisfy it.\n   */\n  organizationsNamed(name: string): Promise<OrganizationRow[]>;\n  membershipsOf(accountId: string): Promise<MembershipRow[]>;\n  /**\n   * `public.has_platform_acknowledgment(account_id)` — the observable form of AT-001.01's \"before\n   * any project creation is possible\".\n   *\n   * It MUST DISCRIMINATE: false for an authenticated user who has not completed signup, true after.\n   * The body asserts both halves for exactly that reason.\n   *\n   * WHICH IMPLEMENTATION THAT ASSERTION REACHES depends on the tier, and the difference matters. At\n   * loop tier it reaches the fixture adapter's storage query, so what goes green is the RULE and the\n   * adapter's storage — the shipped SQL function could return true unconditionally and the loop tier\n   * would not notice. `public.has_platform_acknowledgment` itself is proved by step 7(h) of the\n   * plan, against the live database, and by nothing else in this item.\n   *\n   * What this leaf cannot do at either tier is ENFORCE the clause — nothing in the tree creates a\n   * project, and building project creation belongs to another requirement. This is the hook the leaf\n   * that lands project creation must call.\n   */\n  hasPlatformAcknowledgment(accountId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n\n  /* ------------------------------------ the provisioned admin --------------------------------- */\n\n  /**\n   * Provision a `platform_admin` — the ONLY legal way one exists, because the public path refuses\n   * the type. It sits apart from `completeSignup` rather than being one of its options for that\n   * reason.\n   *\n   * ON THE LIVE STACK THIS IS **NOT** A SERVICE-ROLE WRITE, and the correction matters more than\n   * most: this comment used to say it was, and a later reader following it would have granted the\n   * service role an INSERT on `public.accounts` — a write path straight past `complete_signup`'s\n   * `platform_admin` refusal, which is the one guard that sits on the only write path. The service\n   * role holds no INSERT anywhere in this schema, by measurement and by decision. Provisioning is a\n   * direct database operation by an operator: a NARROWER authority than the service role, not a\n   * wider one, and not one any running service holds.\n   */\n  provisionPlatformAdmin(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;\n};\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the world */\n\n/**\n * What REQ-001's fixture world adds to the shared seam.\n *\n * An INTERSECTION, not `interface World extends WorldSeam`, for the alias reason above.\n *\n * One member, and it earns its place: every test in this suite registers auth users, and two tests\n * that happened to pick the same address would interfere in a way that looks like a product defect.\n * `email()` namespaces an address to the world that issued it, so isolation is structural rather\n * than a convention each body has to remember.\n */\nexport type World = WorldSeam & {\n  /** a mail address unique to THIS world, e.g. `email('ngo')` */\n  email(local: string): string;\n};","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":398,"totalLines":398,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_contract.ts","time":{"start":1786290444173,"end":1786290444180}},"id":"prt_fe73516700016zNhGScdblPGXC","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe734f578001LZ7j7nF32EROiX"}}
