Gate 2 rulings — AI4DEV-81 (per-item integration verification)\n2: \n3: Ruler: the FIX orchestrator sitting (fable). 
Subject: the draft at head d112367. Sources:\n4: `artifacts/gate2-slice1-terra.distilled.md` (6 findings), 
`artifacts/gate2-slice2-terra.distilled.md`\n5: (6 findings, one out-of-scope observation), 
`artifacts/gate2-slice2-flash.distilled.md` (5 findings).\n6: 17 findings, 17 rulings. Every claim below is quoted 
verbatim from the distillate. Every\n7: load-bearing code fact was re-verified against this tree before ruling.\n8: 
\n9: ## Panel disposition, slice 1 — the gate stands on terra's single seat\n10: \n11: The slice-1 flash seat 
terminated abnormally twice (vendor-side, zero-token telemetry both\n12: times; no distillate exists — re-measured 
against the artifacts directory, which holds no\n13: `gate2-slice1-flash.*` file). Ruling: **no third retry; the 
slice-1 code gate proceeds on\n14: terra's landed seat.** Reasons, recorded in full:\n15: \n16: 1. The seat is 
recorded FAILED-TO-LAND, never clean. The invariant \"never treat an empty\n17:    reviewer output as a clean gate\" 
is honored by naming the failure, not by retrying until\n18:    the failure disappears.\n19: 2. Two identical attempts 
failed the same vendor-side way at this slot. The mechanical retry\n20:    budget was already spent by the conductor's 
contract; a third identical attempt has weak\n21:    prospects, and a varied attempt would change what the review 
covers.\n22: 3. The landed seat produced six substantive findings — the gate produced signal, and each\n23:    finding 
is ruled on its merits below. Fixes will change this exact code, so a late second\n24:    read of the pre-fix draft 
has diminished value.\n25: 4. The flash reader is one of the two AUDIT seats. It reads this same harness machinery at 
the\n26:    audit, post-fix, where its measured value on a prior item was two real findings. The audit\n27:    brief 
states explicitly that flash's gate-2 slice-1 seat never landed, so the audit sitting\n28:    weighs its harness 
findings knowing they are a first read.\n29: 5. A concurrent re-run while the executor writes would violate 
one-writer; a serial re-run\n30:    would cost a full sitting cycle for a seat that failed twice.\n31: \n32: ## Slice 
1 (harness machinery) — reader terra, 6 findings\n33: \n34: ### S1-1 — ACCEPT (critical, attestation.ts:67)\n35: \n36: 
> \"`writeAttestation` deletes and writes the attestation table for any supplied database URL\n37: > without applying 
the slot identity, port, or project-ID safety checks.\"\n38: \n39: Verified: `writeAttestation(dbUrl, nonce)` is 
exported and writes on whatever URL it is handed.\n40: The real path is guarded — `prepare()` proves the slot first 
(`proveSlotTarget` at\n41: db-pool.ts:1267 runs `localStackProblems`) and passes the same proven `status.dbUrl` at 
:1284 —\n42: but the tree's own doctrine says exported destructive entry points carry their own guard\n43: 
(`resetSlotDatabase`'s comment; the \"proof travels in\" idiom of `resetLocalDatabase`, audit\n44: ruling B2 on the 
slot-pool item).\n45: \n46: **Remedy:** the proof travels in. `writeAttestation` takes the proven identity read (or 
an\n47: equivalent proven-status parameter) rather than a bare URL, mirroring `resetLocalDatabase`, so\n48: no 
importer can aim it at an unproven database. A selftest proves the unproven path is refused.\n49: \n50: ### S1-2 — 
ACCEPT (high, clock.ts:30; live-email.ts:82)\n51: \n52: > \"`createAttestedRealClock` trusts the structural 
`LiveAttestation` type instead of validating\n53: > a branded slot attestation; `createLiveEmail` has the same flaw at 
`live-email.ts:82`.\"\n54: \n55: Verified: `liveSutCapability` and `liveFixturesCapability` both validate the 
brand\n56: (`attestationOf(attestation, SLOT_ATTESTATION_BRAND)`, capabilities.ts:509 and :531) and refuse\n57: a 
plain structural object. The clock and email constructors do not — a plain object literal\n58: `{ evidence, 
constructedFor }` mints a real clock or a live email capability. The threat model\n59: this tree names is the honest 
mistake nothing notices, and this is one.\n60: \n61: **Remedy:** both constructors validate the brand the same way and 
refuse an unbranded object;\n62: selftests prove the refusal.\n63: \n64: ### S1-3 — ACCEPT IN PART (high, 
capabilities.ts:473)\n65: \n66: > \"`liveSutCapability` admits any `sut.*` capability name and accepts inherited 
methods through\n67: > `surface[method]`, rather than using a closed, own-method enumeration.\"\n68: \n69: **The 
inherited-method half is ACCEPTED.** Verified: the admission check at capabilities.ts:501\n70: (`typeof 
surface[method] !== 'function'`) walks the prototype chain, so an enumeration naming\n71: `toString`, `constructor` or 
`hasOwnProperty` passes the existence check — a real grant covering\n72: a member the adapter never wrote. Remedy: a 
member counts only if found before\n73: `Object.prototype` (the live adapter is a plain object literal; authored 
prototypes stay\n74: admissible). Selftest: an enumeration naming `toString` is refused.\n75: \n76: **The any-name 
half is REJECTED.** The name scoping is the caller's by design: `buildLiveLedger`\n77: derives keys from the live 
adapter module, whose factory return type is the suite's SUT contract\n78: (`_live.ts` is typed `{ sut: { accounts: 
AccountsSut } }`), so a rogue key fails typecheck at the\n79: adapter, and a runtime bypass requires a deliberate cast 
— outside the stated threat model\n80: (capabilities.ts:74-77: an author \"has to write something at least as 
deliberate as a cast\").\n81: The constructor cannot know the suite's key set without importing the suite, which would 
invert\n82: the dependency.\n83: \n84: ### S1-4 — ACCEPT (high, capabilities.ts:621)\n85: \n86: > \"The pending-method 
proxy's `has` trap returns false for an omitted unbacked method, rather\n87: > than making the method observably 
pending.\"\n88: \n89: Verified: the trap is `Reflect.has(target, property)`, and its own comment claims the 
opposite\n90: behaviour (\"an unbacked method is present and refuses rather than being absent and skippable\").\n91: 
For a contract method the raw adapter omits entirely, `'method' in sut` answers false — the\n92: skippable path the 
comment says cannot exist.\n93: \n94: **Remedy:** `has` answers true for string properties (symbols keep 
`Reflect.has`), so an\n95: omitted unbacked method is present and its read throws `CapabilityPending` naming it. 
Selftest:\n96: an omitted contract method is `in`-present and pending on read. Failure direction stays\n97: 
false-red.\n98: \n99: ### S1-5 — REJECT (high, registry.ts:702)\n100: \n101: > \"A bare test body or `default` body is 
typed as a loop-tier body but is also executed at\n102: > integration tier.\"\n103: \n104: The claim is factually 
accurate, and the behaviour is the deliberate, documented design — not an\n105: oversight. registry.ts:822-832 states 
the exact tradeoff: the single-body form runs at every\n106: tier and is typed at the richest tier; the per-tier form 
is the opt-in protection at exactly the\n107: ids whose procedures differ; \"an id that needs it and does not take it 
fails at run time rather\n108: than at compile time.\" The failure direction is false-red (a loop-typed body reaching 
`freezeAt`\n109: at integration hits a runtime absence and the test goes red), never false-green. Refusing bare\n110: 
bodies above loop would break the tier-agnostic single-body form every existing suite uses —\n111: req-016's and the 
24 pending ids' bare bodies run at integration by design and are covered by\n112: the pending/refusal machinery. Risk 
accepted as designed; no change.\n113: \n114: ### S1-6 — ACCEPT (high, runner.ts:711)\n115: \n116: > \"The runner 
forwards an unvalidated Mailpit URL from stack status, and the live-email probe\n117: > grants identity after any HTTP 
200 response.\"\n118: \n119: Verified, both halves: `localStackProblems` checks apiUrl and dbUrl against the slot's 
own\n120: config ports (runner.ts:754-755) but never `mailUrl`, which flows unchecked from\n121: `supabase status` 
into the child (db-pool.ts:1332, :1335); and the probe in `createLiveEmail`\n122: accepts any HTTP 200 — the version 
regex falls back to \"an unstated version\", so any answering\n123: endpoint passes identification.\n124: \n125: 
**Remedy:** (a) the mail URL joins `localStackProblems` — loopback host and the slot config's\n126: own mail-catcher 
port; (b) the probe requires the Mailpit identification shape (parseable JSON\n127: carrying a string `Version`), 
refusing any other 200. Failure direction stays false-red.\n128: \n129: ## Slice 2 (suite, declarations, process text) 
— reader terra, 6 findings\n130: \n131: ### S2-1 — ACCEPT, CONVERGED with flash F2 (critical, 
_integration.ts:399)\n132: \n133: > \"AT-001.12 unconditionally waits 135 seconds despite the suite's 30-second Vitest 
timeout.\"\n134: \n135: Verified: `testTimeout: 30_000` (vitest.config.ts:18); the runner passes exactly that 
config\n136: with no override (runner.ts:1344-1363); at00112 waits `SLOT_JWT_EXPIRY_MS + 15_000` = 135 s,\n137: and 
at00113 waits up to 150 s (flash's extension of the same defect, F2). Both declared-green\n138: time-based bodies 
would time out red — the declared integration green cannot occur.\n139: \n140: **Remedy:** a scoped timeout raise for 
the integration bodies that wait out real time — either a\n141: per-body timeout at registration or a tier-scoped 
`testTimeout` the runner passes only at\n142: integration. Two constraints: the loop tier stays at 30 s, and the 
raised value still bounds a\n143: runaway. The goal-phase `--expect` run is the proof.\n144: \n145: ### S2-2 — ACCEPT, 
CONVERGED with flash F1 (critical, _live.ts:338)\n146: \n147: > \"Logout deletes the cached token before AT-001.12 
sends its revoked-session write.\"\n148: \n149: Verified: `signOut` ends with `sessions.delete(session.sessionId)` 
(_live.ts:338); at00112 then\n150: calls `createOrganization(session, …)`, which throws client-side in `tokensOf` 
(_live.ts:151-162)\n151: before any HTTP request. The revocation clause is never measured against the live stack, and 
the\n152: body cannot reach green.\n153: \n154: **Remedy:** `signOut` keeps the cached tokens; the post-logout write 
sends the revoked token to\n155: the live stack, and the stack's refusal is the measured fact. R-D3's divergence 
handle is\n156: untouched — it covers handles that never held a session, which is a different case from a\n157: 
session that existed and was revoked. If the live stack ACCEPTS the revoked-but-unexpired\n158: bearer token, the body 
goes red and that red is a true product signal: investigated as a defect\n159: first, per the plan's step-7 doctrine, 
never a declaration bent to the run.\n160: \n161: ### S2-3 — ACCEPT, with a verify-first arm (high, 
_integration.ts:190)\n162: \n163: > \"AT-001.01's \\\"atomicity\\\" arm omits `acknowledgmentTextVersion`, which the 
edge validator\n164: > rejects before the database transaction starts.\"\n165: \n166: Verified: the arm at 
_integration.ts:190 sends no `acknowledgmentTextVersion`; a validator\n167: refusal precedes the transaction, so 
\"zero rows left\" is trivially true and mid-transaction\n168: atomicity is unproved.\n169: \n170: **Remedy, in 
order:** the executor inspects the deployed complete-signup function. If an input\n171: exists that passes upfront 
validation and fails inside the transaction after earlier writes,\n172: the arm drives that input and asserts zero 
rows. If no such input is drivable from outside,\n173: the arm stays, its comment is rewritten to claim exactly what 
it proves (the deployed path\n174: refuses a completion without acknowledgment and leaves no rows), and the item 
record plus the\n175: audit claim checklist state that in-transaction rollback is not externally drivable on the\n176: 
deployed surface. The green then rests on the full-outcome positive oracle plus the refusal\n177: arm, which is the 
migrated transcript check gate-1 ruling 9 conditioned the green on. No\n178: fault-injection seam is added to shipped 
code for this.\n179: \n180: ### S2-4 — ACCEPT (high, _integration.ts:312)\n181: \n182: > \"AT-001.09 labels two 
iterations NGO and volunteer but never creates or reads either global\n183: > account type.\"\n184: \n185: Verified: 
the two iterations differ only in the email label; neither completes signup as a type\n186: nor asserts one, so the 
parameterization gate-1 finding 8 required is nominal.\n187: \n188: **Remedy:** each iteration completes signup as its 
type after verification — NGO with\n189: organization and acknowledgment, volunteer with linked GitHub identity and 
acknowledgment — and\n190: asserts the account row carries that global type, so a type-specific verification 
regression\n191: fails the body.\n192: \n193: ### S2-5 — ACCEPT, CONVERGED with flash F3 (high, 
_integration.ts:469)\n194: \n195: > \"AT-001.13 does not verify that the rotated token remains for the signed-in 
user.\"\n196: \n197: Verified: `account === null || account.id.length > 0` is a tautology — every possible value\n198: 
satisfies it (flash F3 says the same: the assertion \"always passes\").\n199: \n200: **Remedy:** capture the account 
id at sign-in; after rotation assert `client.auth.getUser()`\n201: returns THAT id, and assert the `sut.account` row 
is non-null with the same id — session\n202: continuity for the same user, not for some user.\n203: \n204: ### S2-6 — 
REJECT (medium, pr-body.md:1)\n205: \n206: > \"The diff displays board identifiers other than AI4DEV-81, including 
