deliberately NO `observedByProduct()` here. A method by which the clock reports its\n49:  * own time cannot 
distinguish a clock the product reads from one that only moves the test's\n50:  * notion of time — it is the clock 
answering a question about itself. That wiring is proven\n51:  * BEHAVIOURALLY instead: move the clock, then assert 
product behaviour that depends on time\n52:  * changed (the anti-spam window in AT-016.08). Sampling the clock from 
inside the product process\n53:  * is a real capability, but it needs a product process to sample, so it belongs to a 
later\n54:  * integration-tier slice, not to a self-report.\n55:  */\n56: export type Clock = {\n57:   freezeAt(iso: 
string): Promise<void>;\n58:   advance(ms: number): Promise<void>;\n59: };\n60: \n61: /**\n62:  * TIME AT THE 
INTEGRATION TIER, and the seam is gone rather than disabled.\n63:  *\n64:  * Above the loop tier the harness runs 
against a real database whose sessions really expire, so\n65:  * there is nothing for a control seam to command: a 
clock that could jump forward would not move\n66:  * GoTrue's notion of `now` by one millisecond, and a body that 
called it would be asserting against\n67:  * a fiction while believing it had aged a real session.\n68:  *\n69:  * SO 
THE METHODS ARE ABSENT FROM THE TYPE, not present and throwing. `registry.ts` hands an\n70:  * integration body a 
context whose `h.clock` is this type, so `await h.clock.advance(3600_000)` in\n71:  * an integration body is a COMPILE 
error naming the method — which is the difference between a\n72:  * mistake a reviewer has to notice and one the 
type-checker refuses.\n73:  */\n74: export type RealClock = {\n75:   /** the wall clock, which is the only clock a 
live stack shares with the test */\n76:   now(): number;\n77: };\n78: \n79: /* 
------------------------------------------------- H3 sentinels + faults + static scan */\n80: \n81: export type 
Sentinel = {\n82:   id: string;\n83:   value: string;\n84: };\n85: \n86: export type Sentinels = {\n87:   plant(kind: 
string, value: string): Promise<Sentinel>;\n88:   /** scan a named store/scope for planted sentinels (presence AND 
absence) */\n89:   scan(scope: string): Promise<Sentinel[]>;\n90: };\n91: \n92: export type FaultHandle = {\n93:   /** 
