OUT REAL TIME (gate-2 ruling S2-1).\n73:    *\n74:    * `vitest.config.ts` pins `testTimeout: 30_000` and the runner 
passes exactly that at every tier.\n75:    * That is the right budget for a body whose clock can be commanded: at the 
loop tier a session\n76:    * expiry is one `advance()` call. Against a real GoTrue there is nothing to command, so 
the two\n77:    * ids whose criteria are ABOUT the passage of time have to wait for it — and a body that waits\n78:    
* 135 seconds under a 30-second budget times out red however correct it is.\n79:    *\n80:    * IT IS PER TIER, so the 
loop tier keeps the 30 seconds unchanged: a map naming only\n81:    * `integration` leaves every other tier on 
vitest's own value. And it BOUNDS the wait rather than\n82:    * removing it — a value is still a value, so a body 
that hangs still fails instead of running\n83:    * until somebody notices.\n84:    */\n85:   timeoutMs?: 
Partial<Record<Tier, number>>;\n86: }\n87: \n88: /**\n89:  * The timeout for THIS tier, or `undefined` to leave 
vitest's own `testTimeout` in place. PURE, so\n90:  * the rule is unit testable without registering anything.\n91:  
*\n92:  * NO DEFAULT AND NO FALLBACK TO ANOTHER TIER'S VALUE. A raise is granted to the tier it was written\n93:  * 
for and to nothing else — a `default` here would be a way for one id's real-time budget to become\n94:  * every 
tier's, which is exactly the loop-tier promise this item is not allowed to touch.\n95:  */\n96: export function 
tierTimeout(timeoutMs: Partial<Record<Tier, number>> | undefined, tier: Tier | null): number | undefined {\n97:   if 
(!timeoutMs || tier === null) return undefined;\n98:   const chosen = timeoutMs[tier];\n99:   return typeof chosen === 
'number' && Number.isFinite(chosen) && chosen > 0 ? chosen : undefined;\n100: }\n101: \n102: /**\n103:  * WHICH SUITE 
THIS IS. The two facts a suite supplies, and the only two it is allowed to supply.\n104:  *\n105:  * `requirement` is 
a key of `suite-adapters.ts`'s registry, so it is not a free string: a\n106:  * requirement with no registered adapter 
cannot be bound at all. `sut` is constrained to the keys\n107:  * that adapter really exposes, so a misspelling is a 
compile error instead of a run-time\n108:  * `sut-missing`. Everything else about the seam — the system under test's 
