});\n682:   // Tracked from here on: every later failure must still tear the harness down.\n683:   try {\n684:     
expect(h.tier, `harness built tier \"${h.tier}\" for a --tier ${TIER} run`).toBe(TIER);\n685: \n686:     /*\n687:      
* TIER SEMANTICS: above `loop`, nothing the suite leans on may be a stand-in. The RULE is\n688:      * unchanged and 
is not relaxed by one capability. What changed is the SHAPE OF THE REFUSAL.\n689:      *\n690:      * This was a bare 
`expect(await h.stubbedCapabilities()).toEqual([])`, and the failure it\n691:      * produced fit NEITHER declarable 
red kind — so an integration run of any suite was not merely\n692:      * red, it was UNDECLARABLE, and `--expect` 
could never be honoured at the tier that is the\n693:      * closing gate. A red nobody can describe exactly is a red 
nobody understands, which is the\n694:      * doctrine `expected.ts` states and this line contradicted.\n695:      
*\n696:      * `CapabilityPending` names the exact stubbed capabilities, which is precisely the\n697:      * 
`capability-pending` shape a declaration rebuilds and compares from position 0. The gate is if\n698:      * anything 
STRICTER than before: the names travel into the report, so a declaration has to say\n699:      * WHICH capabilities 
are still stubbed rather than only that some are.\n700:      *\n701:      * `expect.hasAssertions()` is satisfied by 
the throw rather than by an assertion, exactly as it\n702:      * is for every `AtPending` body in every suite: a 
